      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>: ,1, -I._ .'_. '1 ''.,':t. i [ , cf -4 – , , 1 i '. ' f ',. ? ~:::</head>
        <p>: ,1, -I._ .'_. '1 ''.,':t. i [ , cf -4 - , , 1 i '. ' f ',. ? ~:::</p>
        <p>LADIES LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>It is perfectly correct to say that amongst the most interesting of fashion news are jottings rela- tive to our head-gear. So far as the coming season is concerned it would appear—so far as can be ascertained—that high-crowned hats are to be in vogue. Of course you know very well that dur- ing the last season or two the chveaux de paille which have been on sale have been of ex traordinary proportions compared with what they were only a few years ago. One or two very early spring models have appeared in fancy S win. and Italian plaits the crowns of which belong distinctly to the conical type. Every week now, however, must bring forth fresh revelations.</p>
        <p>It is hinted that tight-fitting bodices will un- doubtedly be worn in the spring, and as belts and bands will still be fashionable, the fronts require a good deal of trimming or veiling to prove becom- ing, and these tight-banded bodices should ;Fly be worn by very long waisted figures. The fancy yokes, vests, and chemisettes are still exceedingly popular. An example of a bodice which was pointed out to ma the other day fastened in front and the yoke might also fasten in the centre. Charming yokes are composed of separate sprays of guipure laid on a lining of satin, and these have a more irregular and artistic effect than the piece ;guipure with a set pattern.</p>
        <p>:king dress is made of faced cloth, strapped all round the edges and up the seams of the skirt. The coat has a long bisque at the back, and the fronts end in square tabs. Tne vest is of pleated satin. This becoming toilette is completed by a necklet of fur, which should harmonise with the colour of the cloth.</p>
        <p>It is regrettable to note that the skirts even of the plainest walking dresses are still absurdly long all round, while their sheathlike tightness to the knees and their inordinate width below renders it impossible to lift them up effectually. Sensible women, however, will avoid the extreme of this style. The ekirts of the newest cloth gowns are quite devoid of any trace of fulness at the top, and fasten invisibly at the sides under narrow bands of some kind of trimming or other which run from waist to hem. It is unnecessary to say that such a skirt requires the most careful cutting and fitting and should never be attempted by an inexpe- rienced dressmaker. The shaped flounce is now rarely seen, but, as has been said, all skirts widen out from the knees, while most are ornamented with trimming of some sort—from stitched strap- pings ot the material itself to bands of velvet covered viich guipure. For cycling, the very short jacket is not so much in favour as one with a basque of medium length. These coats are frequently braided in military style, with frogging&amp; across the front and Austrian knots on the cuffs. Lmaves and Eton jackets of various sorts still enjoy some measure of favour. Many of the cloth zoauves are trimmed with rows upon rows of narrow ribbon velvet, others are braided, and a few are embroidered with chenille. •</p>
        <p>A beautiful gown pointed out recently worn was of smoke-grey cloth. The jupe, which was opened down the front with rounded corners, had a border of fine sontache braiding, with large corner designs embroidered in self-colouring. The cor- sage-bolero was made close-fitting, and was fastened to one side with large antique silver but- tons. Round the deeolette was arranged a novel pelerine collar made to hang well over.the shoul- ders, and composed of several rounded tabs made to overlap one another, each being soutache with fine braid, and ornamented with a centre design. The sleeves were finished with embroidered cuffs. In perfect harmony with this charming costume was worn a turban toque of grey straw. Your loving JENNIE</p>
