      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CEIA</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CEIA</p>
        <p>The Queen has a great love for fruit and flowers, and these are always found in great profusion on her break- fast, luncheon, and dinner tab:e. Both fruit and flowers are, ae a rule, sent from Froginore, the latter being usually of a very simple nature, which together with maiden-hair fern in rich profusion, are always beauti- fully a'ranged. Te task of adorning the Royal dinner table, takes a cLief deck Ir, and his assistants two hours. _ . _</p>
        <p>Simple and quiet as are Her Majesty's tastes, the pre' parat ions for °inner are very extensive. The clerk of the kitchen is always in attendance, and at one side ot the room is a carving table, where he presides. The dinner service in daily use is of an extremely costly and elaborate nature. As a ruts it is either of gold or silver, or consists of rare Worcester, Dresden or Sevies china. In her love of fl ral wealth, the Priacess of Wales re- sembles Her Majesty, for le,th at Sand,ineham, and Marlborough House, the profuQion of bleoms ak)out the apartments of the Princess, is one of thair mest remark- able leatures. There are always some hundreds of vases of cut flowers in the howl, when Her 11 iyal Highness is in residence, and two de.:kers find constant work in keeping them in good coaditiou aud renewing the supply. the plate-room at Marlborough House centaina an caormeus amounc, of asluable property. It is la t.sllutely firepreof, auarled with unceasing ve,ilence. It is ie this room that the special dinner aqvices—nhe of whieh is the fieest in existence—are kept, b?sides th silver for OT di nary use. Here too are to be seenahe Silver Wedding gilt, yacht-racing cups, centre pieces, salver,. flasks, tankards, Christmas, Easter, and chrieteniue. Rifts. The floor of the apartment is tiled, and the fittings are mahogany. I wonder if the majority of womrn will ever learn the art of entertaining. Few indeed, at present, have mastered it to perfection. We come acaos tale chilly host2ss, the Rushing hostess, the tactless hostess, and the fussy hostess, but very rare the perfect specimen. Take the fussy description. Have we not all, at some time in our life, accepted ac invitation to a certain house and been absolutely worried to death by the im- o:tunitic-s of an over a-talons hostess, who was so noxi- ous for our comfort and enj•)Nment that she did not leave Us one moment to do as we felt inclined our- e elves ? " I always enjoy myself at ivlrs. A's. She does not worry U 4 to keep on doing something all the time we are there, but leaves us to ourselves a little," said a woman to me only last week. I c neld quite understand. The secret of true hospitality is to adapt oneself to the whims and inclinations of one's guest In every parti- cular the desires of the visitor must be of Tarqmount importance if we would be the " perfect hostQss." at the first show of the Cat Club which took place in London recently, nearly 600 animals were entered, and almost every species of cat, from the tailess Manxman to the bushy-tailed chinchilla was represented. The prizes,wlaich were given away by Viscountess Maitland. were valued at about £5OO. The exhibitors i. cud d Lady Marcus Beresford. to whom the Cat Club owe: its existence. Many women new-a-days are engaged in the breeding of cats en scientifie lines, and the mak rity seem to contrive to make their hobby a firancial suc- cess. At the recent chow, several of the cats were , marked £5O, and £lOO, and one, at the advanced figure of £3OO. _</p>
        <p>We are all familiar with the country walking stick but the town walking etick is a comparatively new inven- tion. All the most up-to-date women in London are carrying one, whenever the umbrella can he dispensed with, and the best are mounted in silver and gold, studded Pith jewels, more or less costly. To the feks who cling to old fashioned tastes, the fad may seem a little "new womanish,". but it undoubtedly affords abundant opportunity for graceful posing, and that artiiitic by-play of movement which lends an additional charm to the pressnce of a pretty woman. For the newest tcques. one can have nothing but the warmest praise, so dainty are they in colouring. and so bi c •ming in shape. Fur is the fashionable fabric for these tcques, and of the many furs in vogue. sab'e and chinchilla are the two most worn. On eevrral sable toques, white wax-like camellias, the leaves tinged with pink, are Een clustering in big bunches unier the brim, where it is turned off the face, while a gathered, and wired drapery of mirror velvet, repeats exactly the peculiar pink seen in the flowers. This particular form rif headgear is very soft and pretty, and looks cosy and tight fitting for windy weather. To scent the hair, a capital plan is to fashion a Dutch cap in some thin soft washing silk, and place betwren this and the equally soft thin silken lining a little peau d'espagne, which may he had of chemists. When the hair is brushed for the night, it should be roughly' twisted up (but no hairpins used) and ths cap put on, and tied by means of soft narrow ribbon strings. In the morning the hair will be deliciously scent( d. Those of my readers wno indulge in pawder, and ta ' the majority a little is an improvement, especialiy at this hortibly tryino season, sill rejoice to hear of a new invention that does away with the powder puff, and its often inopportune appearances. Only the other after- noon I witnessed at an " At Home " a trying instance of this description, A girl in unear hing tier handker- cliH, unearthed her puff at the same time which went , naily careering acrofis the room. The girl' confusion can be readiiy imagined, and her departure was rather more hurried than originally intended. When speaking of the incident with her later, I took fri•im my pocket a dainty little white and gold booklet, containing sheets of soft, delicately perfumed paper, so prepare as to have a coating of fine pow&amp;r. It was the new inveetion, Papier Poudre, and my friend will neve. carry a puff again. Rubbed ever the face, a sheet usines-ea all grease and moisture, and leaves an equal, fin- film of powder, riving to the skin a soft velvety appearance. It cleanses the skin too,and acts •-is a tonic. The pie ee at any chem- ists is only a few re ice, and therefore well wiztin the reach of all.</p>
        <p>SHRIMPS NVIT'r Ric —Carefully pick two teacupfuls of stiihno. P, t half an ouncri of butter in a Eauce- ran, add a little thyme, minced onion and parsley, with at and pepper to taste. Throw in thr3 shrimps and stir until all is brown, then add a lir.slß milk and boil up. Arrarge some boiled rice ol a dish, in a ring, pour the shrimps, etc., in the centre, and serve. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
