      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Mar.</head>
        <p>Mar.</p>
        <p>um EXAMINER FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 3 1899</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>PRINCESS DELILAIL</p>
        <p>BY MRS. GEORGE CORBETT, Author of "When the Sea Gives up its Dead,* "The Prince Physician," "The Vindication of Olive Trevethick, ' "The Star of Yukon," "The Adventures of an Ugly Girl," "Deb e 31/ally's," "A Fair Blockade Runner," Ica.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: loins Mills, part owner of a gold mine and of a most valuable star-sapphire, loses all to the millionaire, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, at gambling. Rizzen-Quicke gives it him back on the condition that he never gambles again. In gratitude Mills gives the star- sapphire to his benefactor's wife. Meanwhile two aristocratic scoundrels, Prince Carlos of Mitylena and the Duke of Solltesein, are plotting with a stockbroker named Mosenthal to float a bogus gold- makers' company, and incidentally they hope to capture some of the wealth of Rizzen-Quicke and Mills. John Mills mysteriously disappears after withdrawing 4,000 sovereigns from his bank. Teddy Rizzen-Quicke falls in love with the Princess Delilah, who has some mysterious connection with the Prince and the Duke. She promises to marry Teddy if he can secure the star-sapphire for her. It is Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke's ambition that her son, Teddy, should marry the Princess, and her daughter. Polly, Prince Carlos. Teddy disappears in a mysterious way after cashing a large cheque wholly in sovereigns. The star-sapphire also goes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER VII. CUPID V. MAMMON.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII. CUPID V. MAMMON.</p>
        <p>Someone else had claimed the attention of Prince Carlos, and he did not hear Mr. Rizzen- Quicke's question. But Polly had just been dancing with Lord Alfred Vavasour-Pomeroy, and her partner brought her back to her mother in time to gather the import of what was said. It struck Lord Alfred as rather odd that Mr. Quicke should address Mr. Mosenthal so abruptly; but he saw that some little mystery was to the fore when Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke hurriedly murmured to her husband: "Don't make a fuss about the seal, Joe. Can't you see who it is that's wearing it ? " Joe, thus apostrophised, looked into the face of the wearer of the seal, and instantly recognised it, although he had not seen it as often as his wife had done. "Well, I'm shot. It's Mosenthal 'himself!" he exclaimed. Then he was suddenly struck by the ridiculous aspect of the affair, and burst into a fit of laughter which forthwith put his wife at her ease, although the ex-pawnbroker had an= uncomfortable suspicion that, when a more suitable opportunity occurred, he would be duly called to task for what Joe Quicke had always considered a discreditable piece of sharp practice on his part. Nevertheless, all explanations were staved off for the time being, and he only needed to remember the present social status of the Rizzen-Quickes to feel certain that they would 'do nothing which ' would betray his antecedents to others. Being thus I reassured he availed himself of the Gold King's ' merriment to slip off to another part of the room again. Lord Alfred had just made the remarkable discovery that Polly Quicke was a charming girl in every way, while she was rapidly arriving at the conclusion that she felt much more at home in the society of the young lord than she had ever done in that of Prince Carlos, whose devotion to her in public was not quite on a par with the private protestations which had appeared to lend a reasonable colouring to the idea that she was destined to become a Princess herself. She was of a contented, happy-go-lucky temperament, which was more inclined to rejoice in present and future blessings than to bemoan the shortcomings of the past, and she looked too joyous to justify any insinuations to the effect that the apparent default of Prince Carlos was a source of concern to her. Yet Mrs. Quicke was, for a little while, some- what disappointed at the turn affairs seemed to be taking. She thought it would be too bad if her princely ambition was destined to failure in both cases. She had not as yet had time to resign herself to the collapse of Teddy's love affair, and just when she was smarting under the annoyance of the mutual recognition between herself and the ex-pawnbroker, it worried her beyond endurance to see that Prince Carlos was not Folly's most. attentive cavalier. "Joe," she said abruptly, "I'm going home. I don't think things of this sort are to my liking, after all." "That's just how I feel, Jane," responded Joe. "I shall be a fine sight happier when I get rid of all this starch and discomfort, and get into an old coat again. I'd sooner sit at home talking to you, with a glass of toddy and a pipe, than I would go through this performance again. That rascally pawnbroker's cleared off, but I can tackle him about that seal another time." "I really wouldn't worry about it," advised Mrs. Quicke, "though I don't know that I would fret for my own sake if people did find out how very poor we once were. But we have the children to think of, and we don't want the past to be thrown into their teeth." While this conversation had been going on in a subdued tone, Lord Alfred had been improving the occasion with Polly, and as a token of his fast- growing allegiance to her, it may be mentioned that he was committing the solecism of forgetting, that a partner was waiting for him for the dance which had already begun. Polly would have liked to remain until the very last, but a glance at her mother's face shewed her that she was anything but happy, and she instantly signified her willingness to go home at once. The Duke of Solltesein noted their impending departure, and hurried up to where his friend Prince Carlos, was moodily watching Lady Flora waltzing round the room with a young army captain, who had been his persistent rival all the evening. "Look here, Carlos," said the Duke, "you are making a fool of yourself, and you will ruin our plans if you don't mind. You have hardly been near little Rizzen-Quicke to-night, and-" "I beg. your pardon, I have danced twice with her." "Tush! What are two dances compared with the time you have managed to spend in Lady Flora's society? Take my advice, and do your best now to atone for recent deficiences." "There is still plenty of time. People of that stamp always stay till the last minute." "On the contrary, they are going away now, and unless you are very quick, you will be in their black books. r' Whatever might be the motive of Prince Carlos of Mitylene for desiring to maintain the goodwill of the Rizzen-Quickes, it is certain that his friend's admonition reinvested it with its full importance, and he hurried 'across the corner of the ball-room just in time to intercept the family trio ere they quitted it. "Miss Rizzen-Quicke," he exclaimed, "you are surely not going to forsake us yet? I have just come in to see if you will favour me with another dance, and I beg of you not to refuse." "One would think you were really anxious to dance with me," commented Polly. "I am more than anxious," was the response, "for I have had so many social debts to pay to- night that I have hardly had time as yet to consider my own inclinations. Now, however, I am at liberty, and I was hoping that you would allow me to devote myself almost exclusively to you. "What a pity that your hope is not destined to be fulfilled, Prince Carlos! Now you mention it, I recollect that we haven't seen very much of each other this evening, but I have been enjoying myself so immensely that I had not noticed it. Of course it is too late now to make up for lost time, for my mother is tired and wishes to go home. But you will know how to console yourself for my absence. Good-bye." Prince Carlos, .thus rebuffed, bit his lip reflectively. "Now, how am I to take that speech ? " he mused. "1-lave I really made so little impression upon Miss Polly that she has been as happy with- out me as with me? Or is it a little ruse which is intended to hide her wounded feelings? Cupid makes fools of us all at times, but who would have thought that Prince Carlos of Mitylene would have encouraged Cupid at the expense of Mammon? I , must .call at the Rizzen-Quickes to-morrow, and drecbver my lost ground, otherwise I shall have Otto to reckon with; and I must pay less heed to the fascinations of Lady Flora, or I shall risk the tumbling down of my castle of cards about my ears." "So there vcu are, Carlos. I have been looking all over the place for you, for I want. you to make yourself useful." Prince Carlos was not in love with the hostess of the evening, according to the popular definition of the tend. But he had always bee.a of the opinion that se" •+,: • • gits.ooroachable in many things. and</p>
        <p>she was his stanaara or measurement where all other women were concerned. "Not anofher woman in the world has so auper,lvi a-figure as she. No other has suck wonderful violet eyes, and nowhere else could be heard such rich music as in her voice."</p>
        <p>Such were some of his tenets of belief, and it was this same melodious voice which now challenged his attention.</p>
        <p>"In what way can I serve you ? " he saked. "By attaching yourself to Mr. Price, an American, who has been brought here by the Countess Sforza and her party." "Will he be worth the attention which you ask me to bestow upon him ? " "He is immensely rich, has no relations, and is already suffering from a 'swelled head,' the result Of his success in English. society. I do not know whether he is entirely a self-made _llan or not, but his personal appearance .is pre- possessing. His manners are as good as those of most people, and his speech is grammatically correct, so he is certainly a desirable enough com- panion, even for the presumably fastidious Prince Carlos."</p>
        <p>"What a glowing description! One would almost think that he had made a strong impression on your heart." "On my heart ? " "Well, on the organ which passes for a heart," said the Prince, and the mere suggestion that the Princess Delilah could herself fall in love brought smiles of amusement to the faces of both speakers, which only goes to shew that Prince Carlos knew his companion much less intimately than he imagined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>A REJECTED OFFER.</p>
        <p>A week passed by, and the Rizzen-Quickes began to look anxiously forward to Teddy's return. They had had no letter from him since the one he sent to them on the morring of his departure, and his father had at lea t a dozen times mentally rehearsed the little lect ,re which he intended to bestow upon him. _</p>
        <p>"I don't mind giving him a liberal allowance," reflected Joe, "but he might have the decency to treat us with something like politeness in return or what we have done for him. I never knew such an outrageous thing as to go off like that without giving us the least inkling of the name of the ship he was going in, or the name of the place he was going to. We have spoilt that boy, that's what's the matter."</p>
        <p>Teddy's mother felt just as keenly on the point, for she could not help wondering how far Teddy was to blame for the apparent coolness which had sprung up between him and the Princetss Delilah, but it was not until the lapse of seven or eight days after his departure that downright anxiety was felt on his behalf.</p>
        <p>Up to this time the meagre information he had vouchsafed in the letter had been accepted in good faith, and no one had the least suspicion of foul play. But when the second week of his absence was entered upon, the uneasiness of his parents grew every hour, and Mr. Quicke found occasional relief in exchanging opinions with Lord Alfred, whose admiration for Polly had, by this time, developed into an affection which would be very detrimental to his future peace of mind if he failed to win her for his bride.</p>
        <p>Lord Alfred now found it difficult to keep away from the mansion in Piccadilly, and when he could invent no excuse for asking to see Polly or her mother, he adopted the alternative of posing as Mr. Quicke's visitor.</p>
        <p>Thu; we find two gentlemen in the smoking-room, where Teddy has been the subject of conversation for several minutes.</p>
        <p>"I wouldn't worry so much," observed Mr. Quicke, as he knocked the ashes out of his pipe, "but it does seem odd to me that he should have done exactly the same thing as' my old friend John Mills did when he disappeared." L. "Why, what was that ? " inquired Lord ilfred. "Don't you remember hearing that John' Mills was last seen at the bank, where he drew a large sum of money in gold ? " "Certainly, I remember that, for it is the most abnormal and suspicious item of the case." "Do you think so ? " "Yes, I do. I cannot conceive any ordinary reason why a man in his position should want to cart a load of gold about, when notes would have served his purpose just as well." "Then what will you say if I tell you that the last time Teddy was seen by anyone I know of, was when he went to the London and Westminster Bank and withdrew five hundred pounds, also in gold ? " • At this question Lora Alfred threw his cigarette into the fireplace, straightened himself up, and for the first time seemed to think that there was really room for anxiety on Teddy's account. "What an odd thing! "he exclaimed. "As you will remember, I told you that I met him, and that he said something about negotiating the purchase of a new yacht. But even if he bought, one, it could not be a all necessary for him to pay for it in gold. • It would be a remarkable coincidence if Mr. John Mills and your son have been doing business with the same people. Of course, in that case, the mystery of Mr. Mills's disappearance may be cleared up when Teddy comes back." "When Teddy comes back! God grant that he may I But I shall, get more anxious every minute until I see him now. Do you believe he will come back ?," _</p>
        <p>"I do not think you need feel anxious about that jtist 'yet. He has certainly been away a few days longer than he said he would be, but we all know how time flies when we are enjoying ourselves, and I daremy he will turn up in a day or two. But to return to Mr. Mills. Are you sure that every possible inquiry was made about him at the time of his disappearanee ? " "Well, I did all I could, and the people at Scotland Yard seemed to have done all they could. But we can't come across a trace of him."</p>
        <p>"Do you know whether all the papers and belongings that he left behind him have been care- fully examined or not ? " "I went through all his letters myself, Lord Alfred. So did the detectives, and there was not a scrap of ivriting which could give us the least idea of any unknown business connection of his." "Where had he latterly been in the habit of spending his evenings, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke ? " "Now there you puzzle me. For many a month I could almost with certainty have put my hand on him. If lie wasn't at home, he was here; and if he wasn't here or at home, he was generally at his club. But 1 never saw him after the affair of the star-sapphire, and he was never near his club for three or four days before I went to make inquiries about him. The hall porter at his flat saw him go out that last morning, and was told that he was going to the bank. Just as he was coming down the stairs the porter noticed a little yellow mark on his coat, and while he was brushing it off, John Sills told him that it was merely the pollen off some flowers which he had brushed against while visiting a lady the previous evening." "And do you know who that lady was ? " "I ha,vn't the least idea. Whoever she was, the flowers from which the pollen was brushed were her favourite ones, and she gave one or two of them to John Mills. I believe John must have intended coming back that day, for he told the porter that he would want some fresh water put to his flowers when he, came bask."</p>
        <p>"Why," exclaimed Lord Alfred, "those flowers should prove a very important clue. What became of them ? "</p>
        <p>"They were thrown away, I believe. By the time it was fully realised that John had actually disappeared, the flowers were dead, and in clearing the room, the porter's wife, regarding them as use- less, threw them away, without noticing what kind of flowers, they were." "ll'm, that's a pity; but I have a fancy for going further into this matter. I am not conceited enough to imagine that I have more intelligence than the Scotland Yard detectives; but it does sometimes happen that a fool sees a thing which a wise man overlooks. Besides, it is very seldom that a detective's researches are not limited by financial reasons."</p>
        <p>—"Not in this case," interposed Mr. Quicke, "for I have been particular to let everybody understand that I don't care how inuch money I spend, if it will help to unravel the maysLery. As a matter of fact, some of the men are working now; at least, I keep getting their wnekly bills, so I suppose they are. But what do you say if we go to the ladies now? They will be thinking that I have kept you here smoking long enough." Apparently some such thought had been in Polly 's mind, for when Lord Alfred entered the drawing-room a few minutes later, she was looking unmistakably cross, and Mrs. Quicke privately took her husband to task for his thorightlessiness. . "I wonder at you keeping the boy all that time in the smoking-room," she eipostulated. "You surely didn't imagine that he had come to see you, did you ?" "I dgn t wi.vr 40441c1,9:t," answered Joe.</p>
        <p>"Whether he came to see me or not, ne was very much interested in all I had to say." "Of course he was. Even if you were not talk- ing about what interested him, he would pretend you were, for he's too much of a gentleman to be rune to anybody. 1 wish Teddy was as considerate as he is. I shall get horribly anxious if he doesn't come back soon."</p>
        <p>Contrary to his usual practice, Mr. Quicke neither attempted to pooh-pooh any cause for anxiety, nor to defend the absence of Teddy. Neither did he feel inclined to launch out into any other subject of conversation, and the Gold King and his wife sat in their gorgeous drawing-room, looking as cheerless and unhappy as•if they werP not the objects of wondering envy to countless thousands of people, to whom Joseph Rizzen-Quicke's name had become the synonym of everything that was lucky. Finding himself practically en tete-a-tete with Polly, Lord Alfred "improved the shining hour " to some purpose, and though there was no actual declaration of love on his part, there was no doubt left in Polly's mind that she had inspired in him an abiding affection. She felt pleased, but she was not quite sure whether she felt delighted or not, for as yet she hardly knew the state of her own heart, and the idea that she had the option of becoming a Princess had been carefully fanned into renewed life by Prince Carlos himself. It is difficult at times to separate the chaff of gratified ambition from the grain of genuinely inspired love, and Polly is not the only true- -warted girl who has required an interval of pro- bation, in order to come to a definite understanding with herself. She was, therefore, not quite encouraging enough to make Lord Alfred feel sure of success. But she was at the same time so altogether charming and sweet-tempered that he was confident of unstinted future welcomes from her.</p>
        <p>Having philandered nearly an hour away in pleasant dalliance with Polly, Lord Alfred suddenly recollected that he was prolonging his visit beyond the limits of good breeding, his memory in this respect being jogged by the sound of the first dinner-bell. Springing to his feet in some con- fusion, he was about to take his leave, but was urged to remain to dinner. "Thank you, Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke," was his reply. "I cannot very well do that, for lam not in evening dress." "Oh bother! " put in Mr. Quicke heartily. "What does it matter about evening dress? You ought to know by this time that we are a plain sort of people who don't exactly want to make ourselves slaves to fashion. Slaves to fashion, indeed! Why • I remember- Good gracious, whatever are you doing, Jane? You nearly crushed my big toe to pieces! ,I never knew you to be so clumsy before! "</p>
        <p>"I'm awfully sorry, Joe," responded Mr. Quiche. "But I couldn't kelp it. It was a slip." "Well, it may have been a slip," grumbled the Gold _Ring, tenderly caressing that part of his .patent leather boot which contained his big toe, "but I must say it was an orhard slip for me!"</p>
        <p>"Lord Alfred," exclaimed Mrs. Quicke, who was by this time almost perspiring with dread lest her husband should make any more "slips," "Lord AlfrPd, you haven't said whether you will stay or not, but I hope you will. We shall be quite en famille."</p>
        <p>The "en famille" was excellently well pronounced, and was only another proof of Mrs. Quicke's ability to educate and adapt- herself to any situation by which Fortune might by pleased to test her social qualities. But although she was a pleasant hostess„ who left no doubts of her sincerity in the minds of the people to whom she desired to be hospitable, Lord Alfred proved himself impervious to persuasions.</p>
        <p>"Thank you very much," he said, "but I really must go home, though I hope to see you later in the evening. Did I not hear you say that you were going to the Opera to-night ? " On receiving an answer in the affirmative, it was speedily arranged that Lord Alfred should join the Itizzen-Quickes in their box at Covent Garden. Then he hurried off, homewards, while they braved the invidious comments of their haughty servants by sitting down to dinner in afternoon dress. Scarcely was the meal over, when a visitor sent his card up to the master of the house. Mr. Samuel Mosenthal," the latter read. "What can he want here? And at this time of day,. too! I've a .good niind not to see him." "Yes, I would," advised Mrs. Quicke, glancing carefully round the room to see that none of the servants were within earshot. "We have never done a single thing in our lives that we need be ashamed of. But, for all that, it could do no possible good to have this Mosenthal telling other -people all the things he knows about us. So I think it will be best not to provoke him to enmity by being uncivil to him." 1 "I never said that I should be uncivil to him. But 1 shall certainly give it him straight if he vexes me!"</p>
        <p>Thus observed Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, and it was in this mood that he soon afterwards received his unwelcome caller in the library. "Well, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, you didn't expect to 'see me, did you ? " were the words with which Mr. Mosenthal opened the campaign at once. 1, "No," was the reply, "I can't say I did. But of course, if you have lnisiness with me, that would excuse your calling, wouldn't it ? " "Business!" mused Sammie. "Ile evidently doesn't intend to be too friendly.. But it's as much to his interest to sing small as it is to mine, and I don't expect to have much difficulty with him." "Look here," he observed in an exasperatingly confidential tone, "we have both got our little secrets, havn't we, eh, Mr, Itizzen-Quicke? And it can't pay either you or me to give the show away, can it? So if you'll sing mum about me .havin' kept a pawnshop once, I'll not let out that you and your missis used to be regular customers of mine." : The bargain seemed to be a fair one, but some- how it was distasteful to Joe, who subjected his visitor to a strict inspection before he replied, with his eyes resting on the waistcoat pocket into which Mr. Samuel Mosenthal had prudently crammed the Offending seal: I "I. don't see why I should worry myself about it. If we found things going hard with us once upon a time, as you put it, we got out of our fix honestly; and we paid you twenty-five per cent. on every- thing we borrowed from you, didn't we ? " "Yes, yes, I don't say anything about that. Of course--"</p>
        <p>"You never went out of your way to do us a good turn, did you ? " _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>I "Well—no; I can't say I did. But then, it was a pure matter of business; and I couldn't pretend to inquire into the circumstances of my customers.</p>
        <p>If I Ilad stopped to do that, I shouldn'l have made much money." "Just so! If you had stopped at that, nobody would have blamed you. It was the little bits of Shari) practice that I didn't like." • "Now, I won't pretend not to understand what you mean. You're giving me a rub about that seal. But you can't say that I haven't a legal right to it. I took a fancy to it, and when you didn't turn up with the money till the interest was a day overdue, well, I just stuck to it,' that's all." "Yes, and swore to me that you'd sold it, although I explained that it was precious, for my father's sake, for I'd promised him, on his dying bed, never to part with it. Nobody but a shark would have stuck to it; and I knew that you were telling lies when you said it was sold. You wore that selfsame seal last night. What will you take for it now? "My dear sir, I will take nothing for it. You shall have it as a gift. Circumstances have changed with both of us; which proves that we are both clever business men; \which. proves, again, that we can't do better than go into business together." "What! AS pawnbrokers ? " ' "No, no! You and me's above that sort of thing in these days; and where we used to deal in pennies, we deal in thousands of pounds now. You have earned the name of the Gold King, and that's because you have come across a place that gold can be digged out of. But you will have a much bigger name still if you'll join me in a venture that's on the carpet now. But I forgot: here's that seal!" Speaking thus in an affable, persuasive tone, which was sligl tly discounted by the gutteral oiliness of his voice, Mr. 3losenthal showed how anxious he was to be conciliatory by holding out the much-coveted seal to its former owner, who quietly ignored it, while he asked a few deliberate questions concerning the precise nature of the venture in which Mr. Sammie Blosenthal was interested. Apart from the fact that he was already indis- posed to place much trust in his visitor, he thought that the seal was offered to him merely by way of ti bribe; and this actually caused him to be even more careful than he otherwise would have been.</p>
        <p>As will already have been surmised, 11.11. Mosenthal was working in the interests of the Electrolytic- Goldmaker's Company;' and he drew glowing pictures of the wonderful revenue which would presently- be drawn by all - the investors in what was intended to be the mightiest limited hada,/ company which had over -existed. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>quietly Clict Mr. lidzzen-quicKe listen to all he had to say that he became confident of bringing his mission to a successful close; and plodded( through his list of directors, bankers, solicitors, etcetera, with such well simulated, respectful awe bs would easily have deceived a less acute man of, ausiness than Joe Quicke. The latter, however, had not risen to his present state of financial eminence by biting at every little promoter's line which was baited for him; and he soon upset Mr. Mosenthal's misplaced satisfaction by flatly refusing either to have anything whatever to do with the Electrolytic Goldmaker's Company, ,ar to accept the coveted heirloom which had been )ffered him as a bribe. Nothing very remarkable in this, you will say, for a man who has proverbially steered clear of doubtful ventures. But there was a great deal more behind this business than even Mr. Mosenthal suspected; and, to speak metaphorically, the un- fortunate fish, in escaping! the present hook, was merely floundering towards one which was wielded by a man who was wont to boast that of all the many victims whose doom he had sealed, not one had ever survived to betray him. . In other words, as Mr. Moseiathal had failed to persuade Mr. Quicke to invest several thousand pounds in a bogus company, that gentleman was to be turned over to the tender mercies of the Duke of Hapsburg Solltesein, who, to describe him mildly, was a Judas, a Machiavelli, and a Borgia rolled.</p>
        <p>into one.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
