      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LOCAL WILL CASE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WILL CASE.</p>
        <p>REMARKABLE EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>DISPUTE AS TO BANKNOTES. A p culiar will case came before Mr. Just'es Bigharn at the Manchester Assizes, on Tuesday. The plaintiffs. were Thames Irlam and Peter Kerrigan, the executors under the will of Elizabeth Beckett, and the defendant was Mr=. Lawton, a wide.w, who keeps Fklcroft Hall Farm, at Culcheth.near Warrington. Mr. Byrne and Mr. Gilmlur were council for the plaintiffs ; Mr. Bradbury- and Mr. cton appeared for I the defundant, and Mr. Vostlethw site held a watching brief for next-of-kin.</p>
        <p>_ _ According to the plaintiffs' case Mrs. Beckett, who- lived in Dixon-street, Newtown, MancheAer, and also, near Mold, in North Wales, w:is a widow. She was seventy years old, and a few years ago she went to res,de with Mrs. Lawton. Last May she sent for Mr. Liam, who was a friend of her Sistcr3, to make a will. By this will, which was duly executed, she left £l5O. each to Mary Conway and Sarah Yearsley. her sisters ; £lOO to her niece, Elizabeth Kerrigan ; £lOO to Annie Mercer, daughter of ber nieca ; and two legacies of £5O each to the two daughters of the defendant. Mrs. Bec- kett had made a wi.l in 1896, and the only changes in the later will were the appointment of new executors,. and in the £lOO left to the defendant's dtughters, the decea.e4 laying origidally intended that it should go t) one of them. Alt ‘r the will had been signed Mrs. Beckett produced a mustard tin, from which she took the sum of £6lO in Bank of England notes,together with some shares. Mr. Irlam wanted her to put the money in the ti,nk for safety, but she preferred to keep it with her. Mrs. Lawton was a witness to the will,bat sho was not present when the notes were produced from the mustard tin. Mrs. Beck-tt died on the 23rd June last, and next eay Mr. and Mrs. Irlam went to the lama. where she lay dead. He asked Mrs. ler:wton for the will, and she' gave it to him. He then asked for the mustard Unon opening it ho found that there were only octes of the value of £l5O. which left £460 short. When be asked where was the balance defendat,t said Mrs. &amp;The t had given it to her in order that her son might build a hay shed with it. After the fuaeral she was- aKain asked to produce the £460, and on that occasion she asserted that the money was given t 3 her for her own use.</p>
        <p>The case for the defendant was that the money was Riven to her in recognition of kindness rendered. *ln. her evidence Mrs. Lawton said she had been an intimate friend of the deceased for siat-en years. Mrs. Beckett had 6tayel at her farm for weeks at a time as a euest. After the death of her husband in 1894, Mrs. Beckett. went to live with her, and told her that she had made a will, in which she had left • her some railway shares of the value of £4OO. The &amp;ceased continued to live with her until October,. 1896, when she went to Noah Wales to see her relative, Mrs Conway. Upon her return at the end of four men!hs she said she would never have witne;s again. She also said she had made a new will, in wh'ch she diet NI leave witness anything, it being her intention to give it to her while she was alive. In pursuanee of that intention the deeeased gave witnes; on different dales. two sums Tf £lOO each. She a's, said she would pay the c ,st of platting up a hayshed fur her son. Early in May, 1898, the deceasei witherew from a brewery in Manchester, the sum nf £6lO. few days later witness was in the old lady's bed- room. Witnese, et her request fetced a drawer fro= which Mrs. Beck-tt took the £6lO in notes. Mrs. Beckett gave her this money, saying," We must have a. reckoning up. Pay for the shed ante keep the rest your- self. It is no use reckoning. I don't know how long I' shall live, and no one has done as much for me as you) have." A few days later the deco ised said in the presence of the doctor that she had paid witness well for her services, having given her £BOO altogether. After receiving the note's witness locked them up. The Teas= she gave ta e £l5O to Mr. leans was that Mrs. Beckett just before she died, said she had willed away more mocey than she had, and there was not enough left -to, pay expenses. Thereupon wi-ness told her that she would put it eight. In erosF-^xamination, the defendant admitted that Mrs Beckett told her, after making the will, that she bad left her nothing. She put the £l5O in the mustard tin on the morning that Mr. Irlam came. Mr. Byrne: Your story is that you wanted to give them a present of £l5O. Why did you not give it to Mr Idam and tell him why you ware paying it P—Witness &amp;Klause he was ro cross. _</p>
        <p>. _ By the Jude: Did you think putting till notes in the canister would make him good tempered ?—No. Then why did you do it don't know why. By Mr. Byrne: She did not tell the executors the story she had rad to-day, because they did not give her the chance. Before paying back the £l5O she did not make any inquiries as to the value of the estate and the mount of the legacies. The reason was that Mrs Beckett had said th It there would not be enough to pay expcnsee and she tnok her word for it.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sephtn,a Warringtin district surgeon, who had attended Mrs Beck 4 t for about four years, said h 3 had heard he, say repeltedly that whatever Mrs Lawson did for her she would be well paid On one occasion, trn days or a fortnight before- she dies, he was in the bedroom with Mrs. Lawton, and Mrs. Beckett sad, "What Mrs. Lawton has done for me she has been well paid for it. I have given her £BOO. She i there, and can'c. deny it." Mrs. Lawton made no, remark. Fift(ea shillings a week would not nearly cam• pensate her. Ada Lawton. a daughter of the defendant, stated that she received £614 which she gave to Mrs. Beckett. Mrs. Beckett stated that she had given them up to £750, and she said she nought she had left enough to pay the funeral expenses and purchase a new grave. In cross- examination by Mr. Byrne, witneis said Mrs. Beckett had ordered Mrs. Conway out of the bonze, notwith- standing that she had been asked by her to go and assist her in meking auevy will. Dr. Sepnton, recallecLsaid that Mrs. Beckett requested him not to let her relations come near her, as they it hotlyred " her. She was •very irritable. In sumrninc-up, his Lordship said the question scat; one essentially for the jury t ) d-cide, and thy had to deteimMe whether or not the money which Mrs. Law- ton undoubtedly obtained from the deceassd lady wat given by her to Mn. LI wton before her thath. When people set up an explanhtion for keeping the Property of deceased people, they ought to sitisfy the jury that they had a right to it. The preper thing, if possible, was to get it in writing, althimah his was not essential. Still, the jury oueht to be well eati ;fled that the monef was given to ths person Nvo was settling up the claim. The jury returned a verdict for the cl4endant.</p>
