      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST A CHESHIRE RECTOR. EVIDENCE OF THE DEFENDA:CY. JUDGMENT OF TRE COURT.</p>
        <p>The charges of immorality against the Rector of Nantwich were further investigated at the Speeial Consistory Court held in the Refectory at Chester Cathedral on Saturday. The Ven. Chancellor Espin, MD. presided,and the ol,her asseeaors were the Rev. B. J. Fairclough, the Rev. Canon Moore, the Rev. Canon 14. W. Blackburne, and Messrs. C. E. Thorny- croft. 3.P., of Thornycroft Hall, and James Tomkin- son, J.P ,of Wiliegton Hall, near Chester. The same counsel appeared for the several parties as at Ole previous hearings. The first evidence taken was that of Mrs. Myra Blanche Hope, wife of Mr. Thomas Hope, of Hennuil- lane. Nast with. It was taken on commission, and she said she renvenberel Mrs. Woeds coming to the cottage at Poole. Within the pa,t three or tour years she had seen the rector going towards Poole Cottage in the morn- ing, afternoon and evening. Nine o'clock was the latest hour she thought she had seen him coming to tie 1101'432, and she toed seen him go back to Nantwich in th ) morn - leg, Atoms S-ptpnaber, 1698, her huslesnd nad o.se t) work at half-past five &amp;closet in the morning, arid so ns after that time she ooserved the rector coming from the direction of P. role Cottage. She opened the door and went to the gate. Mr. Jackson, seeing her put oh an extra quick t p round the corner towards Nantw len. The rector was smoking a wooden pipe. S'ee knew Mrs. Woods had a pony trip, and witness had seen tif r driv- ing it. She remensbeLed on one occasion the rector walking up and down a lane for about three-qa liters of an hour. Later on she heard a pony trap comi.ig along the rcad in front of her house. Mr. Jacksoe then hurried up to the top of the lane and met the trap which was stopped by Mrs. Woods. Mr. Jadesoa jumped into the trap. Witness seeing all this, called out to them, "You ought to be ashamed of yourself going with a married woman." They turned rbund in the trap and bolted round at her. Thei said something to etch other,and then laughed and drove off id the direction of Hurleston. It was cisslight at the time. She saw them again the same evening in the pony trap going in the direction of Poole Cott sge, and they passed her house. She knew the Rev. Mr. Steven- son, who was a curate. One day she went into the garden in front of her house and heard somsone walk- ing. She raised her head and saw thst it was Mr. Jack- son, who was walking past the end of her house. Mr. Stevenson's carriage, containing several persons, was passing at. the time, and Mr Jackson, seeing them, staggered,raised his hst,and walked quickly a few yards in the direction of Hurleston. She watched him, and when he had walked a short distance he turned back,and when the carriage had gone over the brook in the neigh- bourhood the rector almost ran in the direction of Poole Cottage. Witness had seen the rector and Mrs. Woodi walking from the cottage up to a beech tree, which was one huedred and fifty yards off. See had seen them afterwards leave each other, the renter going towards Nantarich. Mrs Woods would stand for a little time at the beech tree, and on several occasions she had seen her take her handkerchief and wind it round her finger, and wave it to the rector, at the same time throwing kisses after him till he got out of sight. She had also soon the rntor coming from the direction of Poole Cot- tage, and walk towards Nantwich, about three times during the h. at of the summer. Thyse were occasions when her husband had departed for work at half-past five o'clock in the morning. Cross-examined; She was a friend and neighbour of Mrs. Pace, who had informed her. Mrs. Pace had given evidence, but she did not, give witness any particulars. She had never spoken to the rector ; be had never given her a chance. She did not want to speak to him. She never spoke to Mrs. Woods. Poole Cottage was about five or six hundred yards from her house. She had seen Mr. and Mrs. Woods pass along the road together at the week end, and Mr. Woods would return on the Monday following. She could not give any date when she saw Mr. Jackson go in the direction of Poole Cottage in 1897. She thougnt he was only a particular friend, but was afterwards told he was a brother, and then that they were only cousini. When she heard in 1898 they were no relations she noticed him mote particularly. She only once noticed the rector going towards the cot- tage at about nine o'clock in the evening. She thought that would be in September, 1898. On 'he following morning he came past her' house about 5.30 a.rn , from the direction of Poole Cottage, when he was smokine a wooden pipe. Re etameeed : She had no personal grievance against the rector.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>RECTOR'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>The R.v. Charles Elwin Jackson, the defendant, was than caged. He stated that he was apprize-8d rec'eer of Nintwich on the nomination of Lord Crewe' in 1891,and from 1888 to 1894 he was senior curate of Warrington Parish Church. When he wro ete the Bishop of Liver- peol making application to leave the diocese he received a letter from the Bishop speaking of his character in high terms, and enclosing a cheque for £lO towards the expense of St. Geerge's Mission he had Wit while at Warrington. He also recoived a similar letter at the time from Canon Wilberforce, under whom he had been curate for rather more than four years. The Bishop of Liverenol, in his letter. said, "I am sorry you are leav- ing my diocese. I know what a Ices you will be at War- rington, but you have well earned your promotion. I heartily wish you a full supply of gime, wis- dom, and health, and God's blessing in the difficult positien and large church to which you are going . I ask you to remember your old diecesan in preyor.—Yours sincerely, J. C. LIVERPOOL." Before he went as curate t Canon Wil- berforce he Was at St. John's College, Oxford, and while he W4S there he acted as tutor to some of the children of Sir Hugh Gilz-an Read, who had been a great friend of his. Be had several times stayed at the house of Sr IL Gilzean Read, and he stayed with his, daughter last week. During the time he was at War- rington he, like the other curates, Was on terms of inti- macywith Mr. Wools and his family, and he was a cm- stant visitor there,with the full knowledge and approval of his rector. He knew Mr. Woods' mother very well, and shortly before she died she said that her sou thought a great deal of him, and she hoped he would continue to be his frien I. After he left Warringtoa the friendship between witness and the Woods family still continteed, and Mr. and Mrs. Woo is came to N entwich With other Warringtea people and helped at entertain- ments, especially in January. 1896. lie had nothing whatever to do with recommending Mr. and Mrs. Wo ads to take Poole Cottage, Nentwich, and he knew nothing about them taking it until two ladies, one of them being Mrs. Wood', called at the Rectory and asked him the way to Cinder-lane where the cottage was situated. Ho directed them to the best of his ability but afterwards learned that he led them very much astray. It had been his habit to keep a diary of his parish orteragements. H 3 was chaplain of Nentwich Workhouse, and whenever he conducted a service there he entered it in a book. He had also acted as provincial chaplain for the Free- masons of Cheshire of which Earl Egerton was the head. There was another book at Nantwich Church in which the services and the names of the preacher and celebrant were entered. On February 14th, 1893, he was at Warrington, and he invited Mr. and Mrs. Woods to come over to Nentwich the following day. At the time he had made an engagement te dine With Sir Thomas Boughey, but he asked Laly Boughey to excuse him, and told her the reason. She acquiessei, end asked him to go on the following Werines lay instead. Be had also made an arrangement to dine on the Monday wit a Mr. Bower, architect, Nantwich. Mr. and Mrs. Woods came to hie heuse on the Saturday, and on the Sunday Mr. Woods said to him. "Will it inconvenience you if Mrs. Woods stays for two or three days." He replied ," Not in the least. lam very busy :I am afraid I can- not do much entertaining." Mr. Woods left on the Men- day morninsreand that night he (the rector) dined at Mr. Bower's. When he returned home it was about one o'cieck. Mrs. Wends had gone to bed, and he did not see her that evening. On Ash Wednesday he preached at the even- ing service at the Parish Cnurch. After. service, Lady Boughey. who had been a member of the nongregatioe, went reund with him to the Rectory. He introduced her to Mrs. Woods, and lett them in his rekem while he went upstairs to dress. After dressing he drove round with Lady Boughey for late dinner. When he returned home it was half-past eleven: Mrs. Woods had thet gone upstairs, and he went t his room and changed his coat,and then went down to his study t I do some work. It was a very cold night, and after that he pulled up his chair to the fire and went to sleep. He was e rowed be somebody closing the door,but he did see who it was. The time then was about half-past one. He put away his papers. locked un his desk, turned out the lamp in his room, and saw that the doors were locked, went up- stairs, and turned out the gas on the landing and went to bed. - _ - -</p>
        <p>The Chancellor referred to a statement made by a servant that lac 3 went upstairs with Mrs. Woods. The Rector : It is absolutely untrue. Mr. Smith: Is Vine any founeation for the sugges- tion of a servant that you and Dais Woods wont into your bedroom P—lt is entirely and absolutely false. Continuing his evidence, the Rector said the n-xt day (Thursday) Mrs Woods left his house. Miring Lent that year he conducted a special serviNa.ene Friday afternoon at St. Catherine's Church, Liverpool. No act of impro- priety had ever taken clscs between him and Mrs Wcois. When the Warrineton Dramatic Society visited Nantwich in January. 1896. he put up a large party at the Rectory inelu ling a number or ladies and Mr and Mrs Woods. Mr Woods slept with him that night, and Mrs Woods slept with Mrs Kewley. In till Pn,rl v part of February,to which he had referred,Mr and Mrs Woods never came to the Rectory to stay for the wo.k. On anotbn occasion, April 21, 1896, Mrs Woods with her children canoe. bad tea, and went to a children's enter- tainment. He remembered the theatrical performance on Mae 6. His brother and ceveral ladies were guests, including Mrs Woods, at the Rectory. The gentlermn stayed at the cct:age. He hen slept at the cottage four times, when oa two occasions Mr and Mrs Woods were there, on another Mr and Mrs Fairhurst, and on another occasion a Mrs Kewley stayed at the cottage. It was not</p>
      </div>
