        <p>LIKE A WALKING GHOST.</p>
        <p>There is a woman in Hindley, near Wigan, to whom, rnore than three year ago, a reporter of the Wtyan Observer was sent on a subject whith hag created a great loc sl sensation. Her name is Mrs. Henry Lloyd Roberts, and her present address is 231, Liv( rpool-road, Hindley, rear Wigan. This lady, in a bright, chatty way, told the story ot her trouble and her triumph, and, for a reason which will presently become appar nt, the facts are worthy of republication. It seems that a little before the reporter's visit the wo•ss of indigestion arid nervous- ness attacked Mrs. Hebert with t n•rible force. " I cud not eat anything," ,lie s-i r, "and I was .so ner- vous that when I only expected a letter I was all of a flutter. I could not sleep. In fact,l was afraid of going to al ep, for fear I "tiould co vaie 4gain. A knock at the dor drove me wild. If I walked a yard my heart would palpiat and I had go', to think I had heart dis- ease. It was agony. I could not move about. My face and lips were pale, and people usel to stare at me like a a walking ghost. I was bad for a long while,and I went to an infirmary. Whpn I went I thought I soould have died on the way. They did me no good—indeed, I was worse when I stopped tban when I began." Then , Mrs. Roberts made the discovery which led to her cure. , She read an account of the wonderful cures effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and wrote for a , supply. The ficrst three bores did her good. "I have had eight boxes altogether," she said. "('hey really . have done me a wonderful amount of gool. I could , only eat cornflour, and had to live on slop, but new I can eat anything I please, thanks to these. I would ad-</p>
        <p>vlB3 anyone troubk rves ta try them.: uqed to have regular attacks (lasting forty-eight hour) of neuralgic hfalanhe, but Dr. Williams' Pills entirely cured them, and they never re- turnel." Iwo years after the above interview took place, a letter was written to Mrs. It Alerts on the sub- ject. She replied, "I am happy to say that I have had</p>
        <p>CHRONIC INDIGESTION CURED Vi'HEY FOUR DOCTORS HAD FAILED,</p>
        <p>A Sheffield Weekly Independent reporter was commis- sioned to call upon Miss Helen A. Foster, of 106, South View -road, Sheffield, in consequence of a letter from that lady.</p>
        <p>Miss Foster wai at home, and evidently occu- pied a comfort- able position !n lite. Complimen- ted on lcoking so well and cheerful, ;he replied: "Yes. :ut if you had seen me a, fEw months ago you would have been sympathiringwith me. I enjoyed ‘.!xcellent hnith tntil thres years ,go. Then I began suffer from ligestion which</p>
        <p>chronic, brow;</p>
        <p>;adually became and made me a</p>
        <p>mere wreck."</p>
        <p>" Will you describe some of the symptmns P", " Yes; I could not pomibly hive been worse. I lost my appetite completely, and became reduced t 3 skin and bone. I was alway 3 tired, even when I used no ex- ertion, as much so in the morning after I bad just got up as I was at night." " Were you able to sleep ?" " 1 obtained some sleep, but it never seemed to re- fresh me. I also had pains M. lL ova% For moths I was utterly wretched and miserable, and made everbody in the heu,e so as well."</p>
        <p>Whit steps did you take to effect a cure ?" " I called in no less than four docror3, but they neither did me any apparent good nor told me what was the matter with me. They all said I had indigestion of some kind, but that is all."</p>
        <p>" Now, please tell me of your cure." "I read in the papers of several persons, whose symptoms were sirnilar to my own, being cured by raking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Although I had lost all hope of recovery, and al faith in medicine., I determined. to give them a trial. They proved just what was wanted, and as my strentu began to return I persevered with them. Alter three boxes I mended rapidly, add now I feel stroraer and better than I have done for years. N,hody enjoys life better. 1 always keep some of Dr. Wiliiams' Pink Pills for Pale People in the house ; but I have never suffered a relapse."</p>
