        <p>true that there had been any impropriety between him- self and Mrs. Woods. It was untrue at% was alleged by one witness, that he had been seen at the porch door kissing Mrs. Woods. He had never done such a thing to Mrs. Woods. The witness was then questioned as to the visits of several persens to the Rectory, including relatives, and other visits of Mr. and Mrs Woode. On February 19th, he weat to stay with Sir Thomas Boughey, and in the latter part of the month, when he returned. he (witeess) was seized with illness. On the following Sunday the Rev. Mr. and Mrs. James stayed at the Re.ctory, as Mr. James came to preach for him, ani the following Suniay Mr. George Jackson, his brother, visited him. At Eater of 1897 Mr. Woods came to stay at the rectory, but Mts. Woods was not there, and he thought he and Mrs. Woods went on the E aster Menday to the cottage to partake of tea. On the following day he received a postcard from Mr. Woods, and..in conseq oence ot an invitatioa he weot on the evening of the 30th April, 1897, to the cottage, when he met Mr and Mrs Fairhurst. It was true what was said that a subject discussed was the character of a servant who was seeking employment. Toe weather tbe folio w- ing morning was wet, and Mrs Woods insisted upon a Cab being sent for to take him back to Nentwich. He left Mr. and Mrs. Fairhurst at the cottage. That was the first occasion on which he had slept at the cottage. He proceeded to detail further vigits of relatives to the Rectory, and spoke of a visit of himsel and brothers and Mr. Woods to Ireland &amp;Hewing that for a cond ler- able period his time was fully occupied at the Rectory and hi the church duties. Lie gave his (bees from diaries and church records. al Srptemief 25, 1897, which was a Senias', after performing his chuteh sec- vic-1 throughout the day, he .proc•?.edel to the cettage on a visit to Mr. and Mrs. Woods, srayectthe night, and left the following morning. He gave evidence of subse- quent visits of otleq*ladies to the Rector?, and whose n.imes have transprad during toe inquiry. In the autumn of 1897 Mrs ‘Voeds and her children st wee at the Rectory the week-end with the fall knowledge of Mr. W sods and with his *ppr oval. Witaes I WAS question° i at great length as to the services he performed at the ch irch and the workhouse and other places, some of the the parish church and Workhouse, sbeervaaigneehose,,dut heooaosrreleyaltnow t morning. Canon n ituess) What are the positions of Poole Cot age, the workheuse all the onorsh to own other? Coming from the Parise Church to the Work- house, would 57 Oil have to p 1,93 the Cottage? Wieeess : No; the distaace from the workhouse to the church was not very fir—;b net twelve minutes' walk. Mr. Smith : is there any troth ia the statement thae; on one Suedey mornine, in toe surnmer.you C mei have been seen leaving Paola Cottage after tIL o'clock in the merniag, and rushing off to caurch in time for the ser- vice ?—lc is absolutely untrue. . .</p>
        <p>. _ Having regard to the fierglC33 you took that morning, it would be an absolute impossibility 2—yes, it woull be an absolute impossibility.. Witness continuing his evidence denied that he erer went to Mrs. Woods'e bedroom whoa she was staying at the Rectory,and explained that the night he was said to have been go;rig to herjoedroom hs and his 13-osher,were searching for an escape of water. They would need to pass Mrs. Woods's bedroom. - Alluling.to the statement that he and Mrs. Woods were seen driving out on one occasion, he explained that he was dissatisfied with re- gard to some charity lanl at Banbury. He asks(' Mrs. Woods to drive him there, ana if she would do so be would melt her at the crossroads. Having made the engagement, he proceeded t) look for di plans of the charity laud, but discovered they w.,.r 3 not t) be fouad and so his visit to Buabury would( be useless. lie,how- ever, walked to tee crossroads. saw Mr 4. Woods there with the pony and trap, and having explained to her hew matters stood, they went for another drive. Taey started at a quarter to eleven and returned aboat one o'clock. He could not state wheteer it was at his suggestion or not that they went for a drive. With reference to the occasion oa which he met Mr. Stevenson's carriage, he explained that he was at the four crossroads, and on seeing the carriage coming he turned back to meet it and took off h;s hat. Mr. ' .Tevenson was short-sighted and did not ve him, so tee carriage did not stop. He had never seen Mrs Wools kissing her hand to him. He had never been, as sta:ed by Mrs Hope, in lane at half-east five in a morning ' sineking his pipe. He had been in the habit for ten or twelve years of putting a label "engaged" on his study door. There was no truth whatever in the tale that he was in his study alone with Mrs Woods with this card on the door, and the servants told not to come in. Toe first he heard of this scandal was from the bishop in November, aad he then told the bishop he must sea him. After seeing the bishop, he informed Mr Woods and his churchwardens. He requested his brother to get as many people as he could to attend a meeting of parish- ieners, and it was against his earnest wish that his curates were not allowed to attend the meeting. He did not hear a word of the scandal until he heard from the bishop. He did not remember seeing the witn-si, Mr. Massey when leaving the cottage on one occasion. The witness was questioned as to a certain articla which was said to have been found in his vest pocket by one of his servants, but he denied ever passas3ing sue</p>
        <p>an article.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor: You deny it altogether 2—Ycs. Witness, cOntinuing, and in answer to Mr. Smith.said the servants must have misttken an article which con- tained sweets, and which h 3 had placA ia his vest pocket. Mr. Smith: What were the letters said to have p is,e I betwesn you and Mrs. Wo3d 32—Just little civilities ask.. lug for books to read or something of that kind. Hive you ever had a letter from her beginning with the words, "Dear Charlie," or an expression at the end such as "Dear "2—No.</p>
        <p>Or any such letter which would correspold to the words which have been spoken Co by the witness, hisry Smith 2—No.</p>
        <p>Ras there been a sieele act of impropriety be mesa you and this lady ?--110. And have all her visits to the Rectory and yours to the cottage been with the full sanctioe and knowledge of her husb use ?—Yes, they have. This 00N:citadel the defendant's evidence, and at this staee the C ,urt adjourned for luncheon. Alter lunch the rector was cross-examined by Mr. Collingweod Horse. He said he had not been seen as late as twelve o'clock going to Poole Cottage; he had not left there as early as half-past five o'clock, and hid not been in the Cottage alone with Mrs. Woods, as far as he could reneember.—D yoa know what servants Mrs Weeds had in the cottage? He die not know her domestic arreneements. fie had stayed in the Rectory with Mrs Woods weea there were only two servants at the R-?c- -tory. Hii never talked t 3 Mrs. Wools in her bedroom at night.Helhad had occasion to complain to his servants Deakin aod Clarke about their conduct. He found Clarke deceitful, and she was in the habit of breaking articles in the house and going to tradesmen to ord, r fresh ones without c onsulting hita. He had no oecasian to c naphtha of the girl Sarah Ann Smith. There was no fault to find with her. M try Smith, another servant, was a very untidy servant, out he had no fault to find with her as regarded her general conduct. He could not charge his memory for times and places during the %hole period of three years: The &amp;ark); concealed, t toe beet of his recollection, the entries of every visit of Mrs. WSedi to his house. when he stayed there. Mare was not a single entry in his dairies of the visits of Mr and Mrs Wools at the Rectory in 1897, but there was one intimation placel on it as "A. W." which re- ferred to are anticipate i visit of Mr Woods. Ha kap a record of prospective engagements, but many tif his friends !seethe relatives CAM to See biril arid stay the night. Ha recorded his dinner eugagements from time to titne at Poole Cottage. Hs diary did not record when he left Warringtoa. He always addressee Mee Woods AS Mrs. Woods, When Mr. Needs asked that his wife might stay at the Rectory, Mr. Woods addressed his wife as" ?assay." Ore night on returning home he went into his study to wear, and was awakened by a noise, and it was half-past one o'clock in the morning when he went to bed. he bad not the curiesity to ascertain if it wee the door which had been opened or who had been at the door. He did not see Mrs. Wools in her nightdress going ups airs or leavine the deer. and she never spoke to him asking if he were c )ming to bed. What the servants had said in that respect was untrue. On the night or the dramatic enter- tainment five or six ladies remained in the house that night, and the gentlemen in the cottage with Mr Woods. His visits were not frequent at the cettage,and tin only meson he coald give for tie witnesses stating that his `visite were so freqeent to the cottage as to Cause to lose count was that which was patent to the educated mind—namely, the tendency of the uneducated mind to eriggerate. He stayed at the cottage on four occasions, and he and Mrs. Woods were never alone at night. The servant would in all probability be in the hsuse. Letters from Mrs Woods were dropped into his leAsrbox, but it was not a habit for them to be so. Mr Woods had stayed at the Rectory without his wife. He did not know that Mrs Moods tisually signed her name "July." He had a recollection of notes from her and he did not think any two core,eeutive notes were &amp;geed Us' 54m5 Some- times he had received notes horn her signed "J. Woods." or " PaSSey WOAS." SOMP4tictleS "P. Whei.4," and sometimes "Judy Woods" or "Jute' W." He hid heard Mr. Woods call her "July." He (the rector) had certainly never addressed her as "Judy."</p>
        <p>Mr.-Rope : If it was not a fact that tie word "Judy" was not in tli3 letter, 0413 yen snegeet why the serva,nt shoold invent such a story ?—No. Will you swear there wat no totter addressed " My Dear Charlie "or signed" Judy" left in your hauie ? —Yea, I will swear there was no letter. ' Mr. J. Tonakinson Will you swear you never re- ceived sue'i a letter ?—I do. I never recaiyed such a letter.</p>
        <p>Mr. flops: Then you sty it was a pure invantion on the part of the servant it was. _ _</p>
        <p>Continuing, the witness stated he had bean on a visit in the month of Augur. to Strk, in the Channel Islands, and was followed there by Mr. and Mrs. Woods. It was not an arranged seheme that they should mee: there,flr Mr. Woods wrote to him, and if any reply were sent it wou'd have been forwarded to Mr. Woods. Ho was at Sark when Mr. Woods lift, bat a Mr. Thistlethwaite</p>
        <p>_ 1 went with him (the Rector). He never observed a than on horseback on Sunday morning when it was said he was seen running from the cottage to the church- It was not true that h ejoined edrs. Woods afcer Mr. Stevenson's carriage passed along the road ; and he never went to Poole Cottage, He was not in the habit of spying and Ii ad no/ ,r listened in order to hear what was going on in an adjoining room. Ile never smoked a pipe whilst he was in cleric al clothing ; but he smoked a pipe when on holidays and wearing his flannels. He had sometimes smoke,' in the garden at Poole Cottage, and it was very probable he hal stool near the door with a pine in his mouth. He could not give any reason wily a witness snould invent what she had said about his leaving the c ottage at half-past five o'clock in the morning, He bad not heard of the medical evidence respecting Mrs. Woods. He was not in the habit of keeping the notes forwarded him by Mrs. Woods. It was not true, as stated by a witness named Heath that he wa, seen c ming from the cottage as late as 12 o'clock at night. It was also untrue that on leNving the cottage he ever kissed Mrs Woods. Standing in that witness box and knowing what an oath was he swore that never hal there been in his pocket or in any of his pockets anything of which that was a description as stated by the servant; Smith. It was a pure invention. Councillor slifred W)015 of Warrington was called,and said he and his wife had been on friendly terms with the rector ever since he carne as the senior curate at War- rington under Canon Willis. At the direction of the doctor, he took a cottage at Hendon, Staffornshire, but it was inconvenient for him. Later on his wife became very ill, and she went to reside with a Mrs. Kewley, in Liverpool, and subseqiiently she and the lady proceeded to Crewe to look for a co:tsge, and found one in Poole Cottage, near Nantwich. He received a letter the next morning from Liverpool telling him of the cottage. HI remembered the Rector of Nantwice coming to Warring- ton,and it was perfectly true he asked the rector if his wife could stay a few days at the rectory. They went there on the Saturday nignt,and he left on the Monday, whilst Isis wife remained there a few days. When she returned home his wife mentioned the incident of her having gene dowestair3 one night for a lexPiand he told her what a foolish thing it was for her to de. Knowing ner condition and that she was taking In orphia pills he did not press toe matter because he knew it would be ;Ms: irritating to nee if he said anything more about it He had beea married seventeen years, and since the rumours were circulated about his wife and tee rector, it made no difference to his visits to the rectory. He hail perfect confidence in his wife Every visit of his wife to the rectory had been with his full confidence an I sancti on. When be and his wife were not staying to ;ether, the; cerrespeniel regularly, and if he did not write see wanted to know tney reason why. Ho pur- chased a pony and trait) for his wife and himself to pro- ceed to the cottage. He had heard the whole of the evi- dence given, and he toad not the slightest reason to doubt the honesty and innocence of his wife. He had six childran,the oldest being sixteen and the youngest seven.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined : He rernmibtred what the doctor had said the previous day about his wife, who had got into the hibit of taking; morphia pills ; but he did not know that she was taking such pills until he saw them at the rectory on a pedestal near the door. He wished to state Lila; he had much confidence in the Rector of Nantwich, and they were as happy together as man and man could be in their lives.</p>
