        <p>Tio Cciaucellor remarli3d that he did not think it necessary to do Eli WAS spaking the opinion of himself and that of his fellow-assassors.</p>
        <p>Hr. Smith : f.arn very muco obliged to the Court for that expression. I have s!veral oiler wit' se as to character, as well as members of the Rector's family,but after what has been intimated I should perhaps close my 153 and off-r no further observations.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rope: I unthrstand the Court has practically made up its mind on the subject ? The Chancellor: YeS ;we have. Mr. Rope: I accept it. I don't suppose that any- thing I could say would affe::: your decision. I appoared on behalf of the Bishop, and these charges having been made it Wa9 decidei to investigate them. Mr. Smith: lat once say the Bishop was only doing his duty. The Chancellor: Yes. Mr. Sin th : Tho bishop has allowoll my client</p>
