      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>to come and give a public denial to the scandal which has been started in th” Nantwich district.</p>
        <p>THE JUDGMENT.</p>
        <p>Tho Chlnceßoy said he would, therefore, proceed to deciare the decision of the court, and make a few obser- vations. He was happy to do so with the concurrence of thc other asEessors. In the first place, they were very s rieus and grave charges and if it had been the duty of the court to find the defendant guilty, not ling less would have followed than deprivation and degrada- tion. Therefor', it was very necessary that the strictest attention should be given to the evidence, and that it should be carefully bal3nced and weighed. They did not th'nk the charges were uroved, but they did think that the bishop was not only deinw his duty in having these things thoroughly bottomed', but that he could not have done otherwise. The memorial numerousl signed from the parish of Nautwich entirely bore out that construction of the case, and urged that there could be no delay, and that the inquiry should be prosecuted promptly. Consequently the parish of Nant- wich, and, indeed, he might say the whole diocese, and not InS3 the Rector of Nantwich, owed, a debt of grati- tude to the Bishop for insisting upon these things beiniy, searched into thoroughly. He must say that the medi- cal evidence they heard the previous day, which was of a character that he could not possibly describe in public, made a very full impression on the court, and after what they bad heard then both from the medical gentlemen and also from Mr. Woods, it would have required ex- tremely direct and cogent evidence to justify the court in coming to any other verdict than the one on which they had arrived. It had been suggeited to him by some of the assessors that they should declare that they , did not believe the incident of a certain arlicle. which was said to have been found in the vest pock .t of the Rector's clothing. Whether the witness wer mistaken or whether they were malicious, the coart could not tell, hut they did not believe the story. There had been a perfect swarm of rumour, tittle-tattle, insinuations, and suspicions gathered round the whole affair, and the only way to sift; them was the one the Bishop had estab- lished. As regarded Mr. Jackson, they did not believe that ',here had been any adultery whatever committed between himself and Mrs Woods, and, moreover, they did not believe that there had been any improper famili- arity of any kind. The iatimacy had received the fullest sanction from her husband, and he must say that he never heard a man deliver his testimony in a mare pro- per and feeling spirit than he did before them the previous day. He (the Chancdhr) entirely sympathitezel with him and he was sorry that the name of the laey should have been brought out in sach a very unpleasant connsctiov. The court did not think tre conduct of tae rector had always been circumspect and discreet. A bachelor rector in the prme of life—and be supposed Mr. Jackson was about forty years of age—was bound to be mest careful and particular in his conduct, especi- ally as reearded women. He did not think the rector ought to have received female visitors at the rectery unless his mother or his sister or some person who could speak to the decorum of the establishment, were present. A clergyman could never be too particular abut these matters, and a bachelor clergyman in full manhood above all others should be imos'.; particular. They thcught Mr. Jackson had not borne these things in mind as he should have done,and to that extent they thought he was to blame, as he had given a handle to those wretched scandals that had boen flying about for some time in connection vvi..h his name and that of Mrs. Wools. Now, that wis the result and those were the observations which he h,cl to make. There remained the question of coste b be considered, but he had not had an opportunity of coeeutting with his colleag aes in that matter, come of who:n lead hid considerable txperi- once as magistrates.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said what he felt about the matter was this : The b!6hop ought to have the matt n. inquired into, and the rector should only be too delighted tnat he should have had it. As to the costs, if he had prf seed for them and they were awarded then they wot.td only come out of the bishop's own pocket ; and his client bad given him full authority to say that he would not ask the bishop to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>- - The Chanc*lfor : I was about to say that each party mugt ray their own costs. The inquiry then terminate], and the Rector was sub- j.pted to numerous zougratulations upon the result of the inquiry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>"LIFE SAVING IN ThE 1W YAL NAVY."</head>
        <p>"LIFE SAVING IN ThE 1W YAL NAVY."</p>
        <p>One of our representatives tells the story :—"Stand- ing at the pier-head, Tabury Dock, waiting for an over- due bgat in the recent Channel gale,l fell into conversa- tion with the pier-head mastsr, Mr. J. Palmer, a fine looking man of perhaps forty years of age. He ask d if I had made many voyages in rough weather, and was interested to hear of my experience. "We have had some rough times here during the last few days, too," be said. "Will you excuse my asking, sir, what your profession is?"</p>
        <p>- I answered that I was in the proprietary medicine business.</p>
        <p>Re immediately ticked, almost anxiously. " What pro- prietary medicine ?" knd on my saying " Warner's Safe Cure," Ws lice lighted up with pleasure, and grasp- ing my hand warmly, he told me the Safe Cure had saved his life when serving in the Ryal Navy, on the Weq Indian Station. I asked him to tell me the story, which he did most willingly. "It is pnfectly true that if it had not been for Warner's Safe Cure I should not be standing out here on the pier, exposed to every kind of weather, and able to face it ail, rough or smooth. For in 1889, while in the, Navy, serving in the Wan Indies, I was taken very ill, and the medicine ordered by our doctor seemed to do ma no good at all. My complaint was Kidney and Liver disease, and my legs, hands and fac3 became terribly swollen.</p>
        <p>"I Lad read in the papers about other pe,ple who had been cured of this same disease by Warner's Safe Cure, and although the doctor would .not recommend it, I determined tl give it a trial. I obtaine I a few bottles through the Army and NAvy Store, t)ok the contents perseveringly, and here I am safe and sound,never hav- ing had a day's illness during my last seven years of ser- vice here.</p>
        <p>" I can certainly recommend the Safe Cure to any- body, and shall be only too pleased to do sp." Warner's Safe Cure has be3q befon the pablic for the east fifie,n vna.rs, and arthie7ed a SUCCO33 never equall, d in the hid lry of meiicine, not only in Great Britain, bur in the Uni•:ed States,Germany,France and Australia, and whsra it may be obtained of all chemists. These are not idle statements, but which have been tested and proven by thong Inds of suff ,rers of all continents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SERIOUS CAB ACCIDENT AT</head>
        <p>THE SERIOUS CAB ACCIDENT AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Joseph Rowne, a cabdriver, of 255 ICata-t.eford-road, was charged at the Wa.rrington Borough Police Court,on ller.dav merning, before Ur. F. Manks,A.lderman Black- hurst, Councillor Smethurst, and Mr. T. Birchall, with furiously driving a horse iii Bridge-street. on January 3. —Ur. Arthur Browne defended.—The Chief Constable sail the case had been aljoarned from the previous Monday to get professional assiscance.—One of the wit- nesses for the police—the man Roth well, who was in- jured—was presea;: last week,but unfortunately a letter had been reesived foam the infirm try doctor stating that he was suffering severely from nervous shack.— Police-constable Wright stated that he was on- duty near Bridge-foot on January 3 at 7.5 p.m. w:len he saw a man named William Bothwell crossing the street near the Tower pawnshop.A hansom came from the Cheshire silo of the river at a furious rate and knocked him down. The near wheel passed 11 over his ribs. Witness caul.' not flacognise the driver except that he wore a light coat and a tall hat. After knocking the man down the driver simply turned his Inad, and drove on faster, if anything than before. The man was attended to, and the same night witness saw the defendant,who,however,delied that he had knocked anyone down. He also denied that he had ever driven up Bridge-street. He had been t) Daresbury and then gone round by Arpley Station, Bold-street, Legh -street and thence to toe stables.—James R indall, a cabdriver said he saw the accident, and identified the defendant as the driver.—ln reply to Mc. Browne, he admitted that he told Mrs. Wright, of the Royal Court Hotel, and some cabmm at the Central Station, that he did not know wb ) the driver was. He, however said Vila because he was so much bothered by them. He had however, previously told tho police, as ho knew Rowns well.—Taoinaa Solon, another cabdriver, said he had worked with the defendant, and therefore knew him well. He saw the accident, and was certain that Rowan was the driver.—George Williams gave evidence as to following the driver to try and stop him after the acci- dent.—PAce-coaatahle Stuart Bled he saw the defen- dant come past Market-gate on a hansom shortly after the accident.—Williain Barlow, assistant inspector of hackney carriages, said when he saw the defendant and told him of the accident, till latter denied having knocked anyone down, and said he had not driven up Bridgs-street at all that night.—Evidence having been given by Mr J E Sacker. Ur Browne said his client had been a driver for ten years, and had no mark against him previously. He would deny that he had ever driven up Bridge-street that night, but had gone round Arpley Cannons. The decision of the court was a Fiat- _</p>
        <p>ter 0! importance, as thore was almost certain to he a civil action in consequence of the accident.—Evi- deuce having been given by Joseph Rowne, Hugh F. Greenough, Hugo Greenough, George Bowers, and Wiliam Stephens, the benca considered the case proved and fined the defendant £l, including costs.</p>
      </div>
