        <p>WARRINGTON CEMETERY HOTEL SUBSCRIPTION BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Thuriday last week, t he above club held a soiree hi the Warrington Gymnasium which proved a snccessla every ts ay,over 160 members and friends being present. Occasion was taken by the members of the ciub,through the president Mr. A. H. Crosfield, to present t 4 the Hone Secretary Mr. J. T.Whittle, a handsome case of cutlery, which had been won by the first team of the cub in the Lancashire and Che;hir3 Cluoty Bowling Chimpionship competition last se tun. Air G.L.Brim-low, clairman introduced the President and on behalf of th3club askeel him to make the presentation. Mr. CR-SFIELD in doing so referred to the valuable servicei Mr. WhiLtli had rendered to the club durinre the past seven seasons and how under his guidance it bad reached the proud position it held at the present time in the CJunty Association of Lanosshire and Cheshire, and he wai exceedingly pleased to have the opportunity of banding him such a hands)me pre- sent, and he hovel he would long be able to assist ita helping the club t retain the position in bowling thlt it now held,</p>
        <p>Mr. WITITTLE, in accepting the present, thanked the President Ur his kind express,ogs re2ardin,z himseif and his services, ani also the members for thsir generosity in offering him the prize they had worked, so hard for last season' and stated that anything he could do for the good and advancement of the Club he would only be tO3 pleased to do. Mr. Ccuncillor Sumwocir proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield for his kindness in making the presents- tion, which was vconded by Mr. Amos and carriei with acclamation.</p>
        <p>After the presentation dancing was indulged iauntil 2 a.m. to the strains of Mr. Robert Anderton's band. which gave an excellent rendering of the music on the programrae. Mrs, Hay wan, Sankey-street, provided the par:y with refreshments, and her catering was ex. csedingly gold and gave every satisfaction. Messra. T. Walsh and J.D Br,m4lw undertook the duti9s of M.O.'S and the fo'lowing Elentlernm had charge of the arrlaße- ments: Messrs. A. J. J)nes,John Willett, Joceph Allison, E. Butterworth, E. Taylor, with Mr. G. L. Brimelow as Chairman, and Mr. J. T. Whittle hon. a cretary.</p>
        <p>THE PROMOTION OF POLICE SUPERINTENDENT OKELL.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of t!•,e business at the Dares.. bury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, reference was made to the forthcoming devirture of Superintend- ent Okell from die Runcorn Oivision to Altrincham. —Mr. Browne, on behalf of the local solicitors said that that was the last occasion on which the Super"' intenclent would be amongst Viem. They were sorry to lose him, as they haa always got on so well with him. He sincerely hoped he would have the same pleasant relat'onship an his new sphere et labour. lie wished him every success.—rhe man (Mr. J. C. Parr) slid he was unaware that they were loin g their Superintendent. He had pleasure on behalf of the Bench in saying they regretted losing his services. They re-iterated what Mr. Browne ha:l said, and hoped he would improve big position. The Clerk (Mr. Henry Greenall) said t:e hoped Mr. Okell would succeed in his new sphere of labour, and that his successor would be as cap— able as he had been. Superintendent Okell then thanked the Bench for their kind remarks. He was sure they would like his successor.</p>
