      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY 3rd, 1899.</p>
        <p>FROM all quarters of the country THE Prot,stant there is welcome news as to the Revival.</p>
        <p>extent and the sincerity of the Protestant revival. No such deep religious feeling has been stirred for many years. The papers are full of it, and the matter is almost daily discussed in every household where public concerns attract attention. The hearts of the people have been really stirred within them. The gravity of the situation could not be realised until it was seen how many organisatioas there were within the Church, directed to circumvent or to overthrow some old-fashioned points of belief,and h lw great was the extent to which incense, pictures, confes- sion, and other Rernish practices were in use. When thi; was beginning to be realised—and rea- lisation is matter of daily increase—it was asked how such a state of things had come about. It was found that the complicity and apathy of the Bishops had been acting as a spar to the Roiaaanisers and a depressant to all Protestant complainants The laity were rendered so power- less that they had been indifferent. Some Church- men had said: "Let the Church go on her way— it means Disestablishrnent or death." Others had left their old parish churches, to g) elsewhere or nowhere. It was a disheartening state of things. But now we see a great resival, and it is refresh- ing. We do not desire to see an extended series of prosecutions, but we do desire tc) see, a quick- ened sense of delicacy and ethical propriety. The anti-Protestants are not in their true place in the Church. They should leave it, instead of trying to convert it, by adroit aad often merely cunning practices and subtleties. Clarifying the Church would be better than dividing it up into sects, persecuting each other. But how is the process. to begin ? If apathy is being excused by acquiescence in Rornanising up till now, as it is being, though we do not admit the validity of it, the present revi- val warrants congregations, churchwardens,and all persons in authority exercising what pressure they can in the right direction. We wish to see the church rid of these Romanising practices an d persons. The result will be attained, with ,more or less friction, before we are very much older. There will be a peaceful method of atLaining it , and there will be a militant method. The first must be well tried, and at once, and generally. The demand for more time is rather irritating, seeing ' how much has been lost already, and how small is public confiderice in the spontaneous and effective action of the Bishops. The second is surA to be demanded very quickly. if political leaders are blind and religious leaders are timid, and the Romanisers are resolute. The revival of Protes-</p>
        <p>tantism is real, and it will soon find its own and less legitimate methods of expression if we do not recognise the seriousness of the crisis, and take wise and moderate measures accordingly.</p>
        <p>THE speculations about Specqiating in political futures which at- Fn. arc]. tract so much interest just now are very one-sided. The present condition of Conservatism, for example, is hardly what would have been regarded as possible ten years ago. The change as to workmen's compensation and vac- cination may be especially cited. If ten years have effected this volte face,is it not just as worthy of speculation what is to be the future of Con- servatism as on what is to be the future of Liberal. jam? Not Home Rule alone has weakened the Liberal party. There is the public-house question. A Commission is now sittin3,and may soon report What is the Conservative party going to do in the future? Publicans would like to know, and so would temperance men. If the publicans had not been on the Anti-Home Rule side for the time being, Unionism would not now have its large majority; perhaps it would have had no majority at all. Yet Mr. Balfour, casuist as he is, left out the licensing question al- together, though it lies directly ahead. Now, it is as conceivable that the Conservative party, Ia its turn, should come to grief over the licensing question as that the Liberals should once more be wrecked over Home Rule. The question cannot be left where it is, and it may wreck, for the time, whatever party tackles it. But tackled it must be, or it will deprave and degrade the country Another interesting problem is immediately ahead—currency, at home and in India. A good many Conservatives, including Mr. B A LFOUIa himself, are Bimetallists. How was it he had not a word to say about what is a popular topic iii Lannshire, and secured many votes to the Con-. servAive party from the ranks of the workers Thele is the Irish Catholic University question— was that not worth a passing thought? It is a problem distinctly ahead, and the Conservatives and the Unionists are just as likely to go to pieces upon it as the Liberals are on the two points Mr. BALFOUR selected. As the " Times " opportunely reminds Mr. BALFoUR, the present political calm is " hardly normal or healthy. There is an oppres- siveness about, it which may portend a storm, even though no little cloud be dis- cernible." Political futures are very doubtful all round. Liberals are quite sure they cannot be in a worse position, and Unionists are equally sure they cannot be in abettor. If the two parties are fair to themselves and fair to „pach other, they will supplement Mr. BALFoUR's discussion of the Liberal future by an account of the Conservative future just as bold and outspoken. When this has been done, on the lines we have suggested, there may be special reasons why it was more ex- pedient to criticise the Liberal future than to tell us something about the Conservative present and Conservative future. Within a week of the meet- ing of Parliament, to be as barren of suggestions and indications as Mr. BALFOUR was at Man- chester will not impel the country to believe that her Majesty's Ministers can escape the gradual extinction of their energy, the visible ending of their re-actionary programme.</p>
        <p>The Home Office has, this week, issued the Criminal Statistics for 1897, which year is taken_ as the last of a term of 16 years during which, uniform statistics have been collected, and the figures afford opportunities for some interesting comparisons. We learn that the number of offences reported to the police annually, has fallen: from 96,780 in 1881, to 82,257 in 1897—a not very 4 large but fairly satisfactory decrease. But the feeling of satisfaction engendered by this fact is quickly dispelled by another, namely, that offences with violence, against the person or against property, show an unmistakeable ten- dency to increase. And why? The reason is not far to seek. " Offences of this kind," says the Home Office report, " are mostly the work of pro- fessional criminals who may fairly be credited with a considerable number of such crimes during each period of liberty; and as owing to the reduction in the length of sentences passed in recent years the periods of liberty for habitual thieves must re. cur more frequently than formerly, we must expect to find a growing divergence between the number ! of offences committed in this class and the number of persons brought to justice. In 1877 more than 35 ver cent., anti in 1897 less than 22 per cent., of the persons convicted of house-breaking and other Icriminal offences were sent to prison for terms of more than twelve months." Here we have strong evidence of the evil of short sentences on habitue/ criminals. They are only deterred from commit- ting crimes during the actual period of, and. by, their incarceration. Once at liberty and they return to the nefarious practices for which they have been punished, with a consequent swelling of the record of criminal offences unaccompanied by an increased number of criminals. The cause and effect are so clear that our judicial administrators,if they are at all inter- ested in the statistics relating to the crime with which they have to deal, ought to profit by the lesson and decrease the habitual criminal's oppor- tunities for wrong-doing by the infliction of more maximum penalties. Tempering justice with mercy seems to be a mistaken method of procedure in dealing with persons whose natures are fixed in the path of dishonesty.</p>
        <p>Another point worthy of consideration is raised by further study of the figures available. We often hear the argument advanced that penal servitude is bad for the morale of the man who may be a first offender, or is at any rate, not an habitual criminal. He will, it is said, probably never re. turn to an honest livelihood when once the iron has entered his soul. But there is no means of ascertaining to what extent his soul is affected, and we find also that the statistics do not support this theory. Recently, convicts under- going their first term of penal servitude have been watched when released on licence, and during 1897 it was found that the results were of a satis- factory nature. The convict appeared to have profited by his salutary lesson and showed willing- ness to return to the paths of rectitude and virtue. More extended observations are required before any definite conclusion can be come to but so far the evidence points to the conclusion that it is only the men who are known to be criminally inclined, and inured to penal servitude, who revert to crime after their discharge, or when out on licence. Imprisonment is not proved to have the effeet of stamping all respect for law and order from the heart of the first offender</p>
        <p>Liberalism in theKuutsford Division is a sub- lect about which not very mud] is know n in War- rington. Last week, however, there was pro- vided an excellent opportunity of remedying this deficiency in knowledge by a public meetin g held at Stockton Heath. The meeting was addressed by Mr. Samuel ,Moss' M.P. and leading local Liberals. A more interesting meeting has not been held in the neighbourhood of Warrington for a long time, and it was somewhat of a pity that greater importance was not attached to it by pi3ople outside the Division but within easy access 'of the National Schools. A noticeable feature of the fairly large audience which assembled was the number of Conservatives present. Probably Mr. Moss would just as readily address opponents as friends, for he has the golden opportunity of con - vertingthe former if he is not accorded the sym- pathy of the latter. If some of the Conservative present were not convinced by the cogency anti soundness of Mr. Moss's arguments then We feat,</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>they are past redemption. The member of Par- liament for Denbighshire ha the fluont delivery and reasoning power which one naturally expects from a man with the training and experience of a barrister, and his address on home and foreign affairs was most instructive.</p>
        <p>Captain R3dger, J.P., chairman of the central O ecutive of the knutsford Division, gave some interesting particulars ot the work which has fe- cently been accomplished in furthering the cause of Liberalism in that part of Cheshire. Ile men- tioned, for instance, that the registration had been thoroughly overhauled and properly attended to, andithe register is now in a more satisfactory con- dition than it has been for many years. It is pleasing to note the really hard and systematic labour which has been expended on the division,and there can be little doubt that it -will be attended by the best results in the future. Altogether the condition of things Liberal in the Knutsford Division .seems to be so encouraging that there is reason to hope and believe that Sir John Brunner will, ere long, be relieved from the undesirable position of" splendid isolation," which Mr. Moss humorously alluded to.</p>
        <p>Cyclists will welcome the authoritative pro- nouncement on the definition of lighting-up time, given by Justices Lawrence and Channell in the Queen's Bench Division, last Thursday. The case was an appeal against the decision of the Bristol magistrates who ruled that "one haur after sun- set meant an hour after sunset at Greenwich. This decision was come to by virtue of an old statute which said that whenever an expression of time occurred in any Act of Parliament it should be held, unless specifically stated, to mean Greenwich mean time. it was argued on behalf of the appellant that the intention of the Local Government Act, which governs the lighting of vehicles, was that local mean time should be acted upon, and their Lordships allowed the appeal. Wheelers all over the country will be delighted at this commongense pronouncement, and need not, -in future, wander abroad at even- tide possessed by the fear that the first constable they meet may prosecute them for riding without light " after sunset," while "old Sol "is even then plainly visible. Anything more ridiculous than that the time of sunset at Green- wich should be taken as the standard time when there is an hour's difference in some parts of the country, could not well be imagined, and though We are well used to anomalies in English law we must all feel a measure of gratitude to the Cyclists' Touring Club for having so persistently and suc- cessfully sought the remedying of the lighting-up time grievance.</p>
        <p>Nothing demonstrates more amusingly the amount of latitude given to the expression of opinion than the celebrations of the death of Charles I. The Board of Trade have, it is true, taken the precaution in recent years of requiring that the wreath for the statue in Whitehall shall , be submitted to them in order that the mottoes I may be examined, but that appears to be simply that nothing vulgar should appear on the memor- ial. The inscriptions and even the very title of the League suggest by inference that our present Royal Family are usurpers, and were half such a reflec- tion to made aaainst the Emperor in Germany everyone who uttered it would be sent off to prison fer lese-majeste. It is remarkable that the Bishops of Bristol and Aberdeen shauld have lent their names to a movement for a more Imbitious monu- ment of the deceased monarch, awl the churches of England by law, established should be used for a commemorative service in which the King is regarded in the light of a martyr. It is true that the services are held in a very few churches, bat it is very indiscreet of the clergy, who are supposed to be above allthings proper, to lend their names, in however small a number, to such an irresponsible and eccentric body as the Legitimist League. No doubt they would not go so far as the meeting held in the French Chamber of St. James's Hall a few days ago, which pas, ed a resolution declaring allegiance to Mary Theresa, wife of Louis, Prince of Bavaria, as Queen of England sincelB7s ; but these waves of sympathy sO ill-directed may lead them into strange com- li.any, both as regards religion and practical poli- tics. The less that is said about the reign of Charles the First,or,indeed, about hereditary rule, the better. We may be well content that the reigning family in this country has shown such a keen sense of the duties of its position, and has served the nation as well as it has served them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>DISTRESSING ICE FATALITIES AT HLYDOCK.</head>
        <p>DISTRESSING ICE FATALITIES AT HLYDOCK.</p>
        <p>A PECULIAR INCIDENr,</p>
        <p>The brief visit which the frost paid to .this neighbour- 'bood has not bean unattended with disaster. Many of the ponds in the dis..rict were frozen over, and on the shallow pools, skaters were •numerous. At Havdock a "ventureibme collier, paid for his temerity with his life, and in this connection a p-euliar incident hatopened. About half-Dast nino on -Stturday morning. Daminie Corcsran, 20 years of age, a collier of Elin View, War. Irington-road, Ashton, left looms for the purpose of skat- iu7. on Haydook Park reservoir, Hayd ick. As he did not, return home at night acme alarm was felt,and early as... Sunday attuning, two men 11ain9,1 Pilling and Gill, tcommencA se,roi ing for his body with Toppling irons. Police-sergeant Walker, and other nfficers subsequer.ty joined in the search, and while to engaged they came anross the body of a man named Jam,-S Butler, 39 years of age, a enllien. of Penny-lane, I:l4ydock, who, it is stated, had not been missed from his hutise.as he was in the habit of rambling away at WE etc er,ds. The discnvetV of higbxly in the water was a painful sulpriso to his relatives and friends. The searchers continued their operations, and about an hour later recovered the body of Corcoran, who had his skates attached to his boots. INQUESTS ON THE TWO MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. %Brighouse, county coroner. ;hell a couple of in- quests on Tuesday at the Ram's Head Hotel, Haydock, on the bodies of the two men who were found to have been drcwced On Saturday last through the breaking of the ice on a pool of water at Haydocklolge. Thomas Corcoran; aged eighteen, said that he and five others went to the reservoir to skate about half-past two on Siturday art^rnoon. They ,saw a large hole in the jc., and on the stir ace of the ice was R Cap, which they took to be flutter's. They threw stones at it to ace what it was. They did not tell anybody abotr i*.—lnspEctor-S ton said the relatives of Corcoran came to the police station on !Saturday night, but it was top late to do anything then. —Robert Cr lso, of Ashton, said he saw Butler in front ,'nf the Angel Hot.l, Ashton. He asked him if the reservoir would hld, and Bitter retailed that he had 'crossed it that morning, hut it would b. grand next day —Mrs. Simm„ landhdy of the Commercial Inn, Ashton, stated that she raw Butler bet wen fuar and five o'clock -An Saturday afternoon pass the window rf he house.- - The Coroner questi .n.ti the witness'as to the time, but nhetsaid Eh was sur:ahout it.--Policc-ccntst tble Thomp- son gave evidence as to the reoover7 of the holy of tßutler.—Ser9.anr Walker said lie recovered the body of Cracoran at 113 S on Sunday morning. It was fully dressed, and there were a ptir of Pkitel attached to the feet. •There were two holes in the reservoir about thitty or foity yards apart. Each body was found in 43ne of the holes.—Tne jury returned a verdict " that ',the two men were accidenteliy drowned."</p>
        <p>SAMMY It A itRIERS.</p>
        <p>Thenlembers -ort7i;-org—a—n-iTe-tro—n-had fin ordinary 'Tun trom headquarters on Saturday, going about miles. After _layering three• mites of hard .eogntry it was /decided to make far the road, cawing out close by Bald Hall, and a grand run ix was the result. 3lessrs. (4, W. Eastwood, T. Devlin, and (Riley came in in the order named.</p>
