      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CHESHIRE! TEACHERS'. UNION..</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE! TEACHERS'. UNION..</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Cheshire District Union of the National Union•ot Teachars, was held on Saturday at the Central Rill, Manchester. It was announced that the election of officers for the ensuing year had resulted as follows : --President, Mr J F M.-821tim, Runcorn; vici - presi lent, Mr F Knett, Audenshaw ; treasurer, AU J Woid. Northwich, (re-elected), huh. secretary ;Mr A lt, Macclesfield. After taking the prssidential chair, Mr. Moulton deivered his inatieural address, in which he dealt moss capably with the present poAcion of the na'imal education in England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WIDNES POPULAR CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES POPULAR CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>In connection with these concerts, a capital pro- gramme was submitted on Saturday to a large audience in the Baths Assembly RIGID, Mr. A. Bennett occuping the chair. The first par; of tits programme was of a miscellaneous character, Miss E Williams, T C La, com- mencing the proceedings by a pianoforte seleeti an. Swags were contributed by ,isses IcLouhlii, B Age- mall, Clarke and Leon ; Messrs. J Roberts, J Gdes, J W Howley, T Ashton, O'Rourke, and Master Howley, Master Howley, a boy about seven years old, was un- doubtedly the favourite of the evening. Ells tAagin; anl dancing were excellent. His impersonation of Eugene Stratton was particularly good. Tee secon I moeety of the programme was taken up by a laughable sketch, en- titled " The Kentucky Barber," Clem Haricorn, the barber, was ably portrayed by J W Howley. Billy, the baroer's clerk, was undertaken by J. Giles. R. Tno,nls male a capital'policema . The part of Jane Pain, Billy's love, was well sustained by H. William. The accom- panist was Miss E. Williams. TERRIBLE !SUBBING ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. Early on Monday mr;raing Etlen O'D nueti, a4ed ten, residin' with her parent i at 14, Bridge•street, Widnes, died at the Accident Elotpital from tne effects of barns sustained on Sunday afternoon,. It appears that Mn. O'Donnell, the mother of the deceased, left the house about half-past two ye for the purpose of t %king her husband's dinner to one of the . neighbouring chmcal works, where he is employed, leaving her clauguter Ellen and two younger children alone in the kitchen. S m time afterwards the deceased was seen by Sutll3 neighbours to rush out of the heti te into the streets with her clothes on fire. Sue ran back into the house, whe:e two neighbours m ade herctc attempts to extinguish the flames. She was taken to the Accident Hospital, where she remained partly unconscious until the end came as stated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HALF H *fIR LECTURES AT WIDNES LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>HALF H *fIR LECTURES AT WIDNES LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>The secoud of a series of hilf-hour lectures promote I by the Widnes Free Lib-a 7 C -irnmittee was given lathe lecture theatre of th 3 Technic 1,l Ingtitute oa Friday evening. Mr A E oa,lvert occupied the chair, and the attendance was a great improvement on the f n.mer occasion. The lecturer (Ur W Stainer, M.A.) took his subject " Kingsley," and delivered a most spirited dis- course, which was followed by his hearers with great interest. The lecture was musically illustratei with the following selections frinn KingsleV3 songs :--" Toe Three Fishers," readered by Mr C Hodgson; Mr Callan sang "Oh ! that we were Maying "; " Clear and Cool," from the" Water Babies." was sung by Mss Rithbone. Miss Shore accompanied the singers. At th., cl up) the proposition of Mr Calvert, se :Jaded by Mr Shore. a hearty v,)t,3 of thaoks was accorded to the lecturer. Messrs. Harvey and Hoigann proposed and secondel a vote of thanks to the chairman. RUNCORN LITERARY SOCIETY AT GARSTON.</p>
        <p>A. very interesting evening was spent by a small but vers. pleasant gathering in tbe Garst )n Wesleyan school- room on Monday evening, on the • sion of the weekly mee ing of the Garston LtMary Society at that place. S-vmral members of the Runcorn Pickwick Literary Club had j urneved down to take par: in a discussion upon the r slative advantage; of " Town versus Country Llte." The chair was occupied by the R-v. Thomas McCullagh. The debate was opened by Mr. T. Saunders (Runcorn), who made out a fair case in favour of town the, and the Rev. Mr. Dove replied. The other speaker; taking part in the animated debate were :-.-Niessrs. Butters, B. Crssby, Bawden (Garstnn) ; and Messrs. W. Millington, E. H. linivetoa and Williams (Runcorn).—Tha Ctiair- man characterised the debate as one that-hail given them all considerable pleasure. The meeting close' with the Benediction.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER, M.P., ON THE LIBERAL LEADERSHIP,</p>
        <p>A crowded and enthusiastic, Liberal meeting war held at Winsford on Tuesday eveninq. Sir John Brunner, M.P., and Mr. Lloyd-George, M.P.' were the priocipal speakers. Sir John Brunner said there was no sound reason for the retiremeat of Sir W Harcourt and Mr. !Morley. They had never followed a more 8131:M lid fighter than Sir William Harcourt, but the trouLla had been that during 1897-8 they had seen Su little of their leaders. It had been dispiriting t) see the beaches re- served for ex-Ministers alm,st empty night after night. 'People were crying out for the expansion of the Empire and for new markets, but the acquisition of savage countries was not worth war.—(Cheers.) They had been leipanding in a fashion which contained a good deal of rottenness. He aid not like colonies b longing to limited companies, and if they encsur igrd Mr. Thamberlain to spend their earninzs thr oug'n the taxes upon the develop- ment of estates belonging to companies. they would put the honesty of the Legislature into such danger that their honest interests might before long go by the board entirely.—(Cheers.) FUNERAL OF MRS. MARIA POTTER.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Runcorn C9wtery o ) Wednesday afternoon of Mrs. Maria Potter, aged 62, widow of the late Mr. Charles James Potter, formerly ot Runcorn,amidst manifestations of profound regret. Mrs. Patter pass (z 1 peacefully away on M )nday last, at the residence of her shter-in-law, bdrs. Walker, Ash Villas, Watland's Park, Woolstanton. On Wednesday the body was conveyed by rail to Runcorn Station,whence it was escorted to its last restinz place by the following mourners: Mr W Wylde, Bouthpo t; Mr Charles vVvlde, Liverpool; Mr Jonathan Potter, Mr C B Walker, Mr ir E Walker; also Messrs A Timmins, H T Boulton, J Walker (jun), G Sommerfield, and Michael Barker. The pall-bearers were : Messrs T Simistcr, 8 C Beckett, C Pownall, and C Ditchfield. There was a lark() number a floral tributes of respect to the deceaFed. The burial service was conducted by the Rev CW L Christien, superintendent of the Wesleyan Circuit, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.--CRESHIRE GRAND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.--CRESHIRE GRAND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting ef the above association was held on Saturday in the Mechanic.' Institute, Crewe. There was a good attendance of officers and representatives from VVinsford,Cre we, Weston,Northwieh,and Runcorn. The Grand Division was opened in due form hy Philip Leicester, of North wich, who met with a hearty reception. The funds of the Order having been centra- lized for sick and funeral purposes amount to £358 I6s 74d, and the cadet funeral fund to £l2 3s 1 L The reports were unanimously adopted. It was stated that the Weston friends had done remarkably well with their cadets, who numbered 81. A. scbe-.ne for life assurance for males and females b,,tween 16 years and 45 was dis- cnsied and adopted by the Grand Division. The annual election of officers took place with the following result: —Mr Thomas Cook (Runcorn), G.W.P. ; Mr William Dunbabin (41Treton,Runeorn), G.W.A. ; Mr Price (Crewe) G.W.C. ; Mr Thomas J Htlt (Runcorn); era. d6cribe ; Mr Thomas Ileesom (Runcorn), trassurer ; Mr Ham', t (Winsford), sentinel ; Mr James Moores (Runcorn), G. chaplain ;Mr Joins J hies (Itnneorn). G. trustee, ; Mr S M Timoerley (Northwich). auditor; Mr Brown (Wels'on) G.W.P. of cadets. The °fillers and Messrs D Bisbrown, Wooclyer, Brown and H amhat were appoint° I the man- agement committee. Thanks and expressions of good will were tendered to Mr Edward Key qate secretary), who was leaving the district, on the mo!iln of Messrs A Noden and P Leicester It wq; decided that the nest place cif meeting should be Winsford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>S. PAUL'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.</head>
        <p>S. PAUL'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.</p>
        <p>There was a good attenianee of members on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Posnett addressed the meetine in his usual effective and interastieg manner, taking for his subject "The connection ad pesition Ef Pontius Pilate with the Grueifixion." Mr. Norman with effi ct. Before the Close of the service the annual general meeting was held. The,Treagurer. Mr. Coventry. reported a satisfac- tory condition of the B,neeelent fated. Help had been extended to several members who heel been unfortunate, and still they had a good balance in hand to meet further exieences. Mr. Coventry was re-elected treasurer for the comine, meesioa which offi,:e he aecepPed in a few well cnosen words. Mr. Millinger the secretary, was thanked for his belie, and also re-eleeted for the Sessiee. Be thanked the class and assured them or his best eff irts to make the c'ass a success. He wis sur it wee only necescary ter Mr. Posnett'a extremely interesting ad- dresses to be heard to sown a lareer attendance. The beat wishes of the claqi wer ex'end•d to Mr. Bowyer oe his resigning the position of organs. Mr. J. Narmaa was elected organist in his ate id. Mr. Ceventry here Proposed that the best thanks of the claes be given to Mr. Posnett fur his presence, and the intellectual dis- courses they Were treated to frnm Sunday to Sind ty. Mr. Peemett in reply said he would be pleased, if he pleased them, to continue his short talks but he had come this afternoon resolved to propose that some one else bs asked to address them ore asionally, say some memb sr of the class,and if they could arrange this he would bo pleas,d te came and listen. At the aline- time he therikel them for their expression of good testing, whinh e hoped wonli always eontinue. The officer, and cemmit tee row stand as fellows :—prceident, Mr Poanett ; t-easurar, Mr Coventry; secretery, Mr Milli neer; m;genisr. Me Nerman; ceminirtee, Mr Biter. Mc if eghes, Mr 51.1)r, Mr Scho- field, Mr Bolt h, Mr Seerr</p>
