      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 3, 1'99</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 3, 1'99</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly entvetaininent, in c mriection with the above mission was given by Miss Hil.'g party before a fair attindance. The ch dr was taken by Mr. W.Shaw, (missionary.)The following programme wag then render- ed:—Pianofor'e solo,Miss Flarrism ; song, 6' The Parion came ton late," MasterJ Ratter ; song, Miss Hill ; reci- tation, "Spring," Miss L Shaw ; pianoforte s 10, " Off to Klondylre." Master W Shaw ; recitation, " The Water Cure," Miss Evelyn Brown; song, " Killarney," Ma iter J. Rutter; pianofurte solo, Miss L. Shaw ; song," Only a travAiing tinker," Master J Rutter; recitation, " Newsboy," E Brown ; song. " Ship Britannia," Master J Rutter ; stump speech, " Woman's Rights," Master F Rutter. Some of the kilns ware so well rendered that ,encores were dem mded. eic the close a vote of thanks was passed to the party on the proposi- tion of Mr. Shaw, seclniad by Mr. J )saph HoxwarGh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORS Nu Ll.</head>
        <p>RUNCORS Nu Ll.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORABSPaNDENT Evidence of how man enjoyment could have been obtained out of the Heath and Common was not lacking during the period of the recent sharp frost. A glance at the happy surging crowd that patronised the "rink': should indee I have been sufficient guarantee that any expenditure in that direction would have met with uuiverBal approval. In fact, a small charge would be willingly pail any time for a safe skate.</p>
        <p>Police Constable Fry, of Runcorn, by his success- fa) attempt at recovering an old woman from the Mg of a ragiug fire, is deserving of the highest praise. It was a plucky, daring thing to do; to go headloag into unknown danger, amidst volumes of smoke, guided simply by the groaning of the help- less vict'im and rescue 11,r from the perilous posi- tion. The only pity was that his information of the disaster came so late that although rescued the poor woman (who was about 65 years of age) died from the eff sets of her injuries the following day. Constable Fry ols3, alone and single handed, put out the fire, which was so fierce that the staircase was partly burnt away.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner But., A.? , in his spiecli at the Runcorn Liberal Club explained in a way that re- douods to the credit of Niegsrs. Brunner, Nlond, and Co., the position of the firm as regards the e nploy- ment of min not over 30 years of age. His refer- ences to local affsirs show, that hø is, as ever,deeply interested in the welfare of Runcorn an i on the alert to upli)ld his principles and serve his party.</p>
        <p>But it was to Mr. I.zainuel Taylor's remarks that the greatest weight attached after all, and when 'Air. Taylor declared that he had had enough of the "All church and all politics "of the opposite party, and that he was with. them (the Liberal party) "to the heart," the "heart '' of the meeting literally throbbe I with jiv. It was enthusiasm of no un- mistakable character that made the building re- sound with applause. NOW it will be a pleasure to partake in an election, avec/Ally as the battle will be fought with each side k:towing who's who and what's what! With Mr. Taylor on one bide it wily remains for us to convert Mr. P. J. Norman, (who entertained some time ago—though I somewhat doubt it now—views iientical with Mr. Taylor)and then we can say in the words of the latest, popular song, "Let 'em all come."</p>
        <p>On Friday evening a meetinz of ths pr-milters of the old people's treat in ltainc in and distrtct,will be held at the Town Hall. • A hearty iavitacioa is extended to all who are williaz to assist. True prt— sident of the movement is Air. S4nl,lt3i Taylor, J.P. sir. E. Imison, 0,0., is secretary, and Aessrs. G Howard and WAlillingtoa (treasurers). The move- ment is deserving of every su•dp)rt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SHOCKING CALA.MITY AT WESTON. . A WOIIA.N DECAPITATED.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING CALA.MITY AT WESTON. . A WOIIA.N DECAPITATED.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at Weston Poiat, Runcorn, a woman, named Mrs. Leach ha l her h !ad co nolstely cut off from her body. Sae wss taking her husband's dinner to Mr. Branche's engineering works, and crossing the rail- way which connects the Weston stone quarries with the Ship Canal was knockei down by some passing wagons, the wheels of one crossing her neck. Sestral pets ms wit- nessed the frightful faratiry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>PLUCKY RUNuouN PoLLUMLIN.</head>
        <p>A PLUCKY RUNuouN PoLLUMLIN.</p>
        <p>SAD DFATII OF AN OLD WONIAN.</p>
        <p>On Monday night a young woman passing down Al- conk-street abo.st ten o'clock dis sovered a fire in tho bed• room of a cottage adjoining Haattinson's mineral water works. She gave inform ition to P.C. Fry, who pi-a- cceded to the scene ani found the bedroom on fir 3 and someone screaming wichin. Failing to gain an entrance bv the front and back doors, he succeeded in (messing the window, and mile his way upstairs with some diffi- culty, the dense smoke almost suffocating him. In the bedroom he found an old Woman in great agony. Her elothing was mould 3ring, and he quickly removed the burning portion ant opening the window, threw it into the street. A number of persons had by this time been attracted to the house, and Fry sent hr Dr. McDougall. The doctor was promptly on the scene. and found mat the old woman, who was identified as Mrs. Ann Farrell wivi formerly kept a lodi,ing=bouse, was terribly burnt atout the lower portion of the body and ha lly cut on the hands. The cause of tbe fire cvms the explosion of a paraffin lamp,by which the old woman's clothing and the covering had teen ignited. In battliogwith the fire the worn•in appears to have Fcramhed amongst the broken glass scattered abu the room, and in tnat way met with the injury to her hands. It was t'sought also that she had made an attempt in vain to open the win- dow, as this covering on a stand close by was found to he partially burnt. It appears that she lived with he daughter, the wife of a boatman, but, unfortunately at the time of the acuident, she was alone in the house, and except for the timely assistance of th.o policomin would most certainly have been burnt to death. About 3 o'clock on Tuesday Mrs. Farnll, who had ben removed to Dutton Workhouse, died from the effects of her injuries. INQUEST AT DUTTON WORKFIOUsE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon Mr. J. E. WorsleTy-,l;id an in. (peat on the body of Ann FA rrPll. aged 74, of Ru acorn. who died from the result of barns sustained on Monday- -Constable Fry, of Runcorn, said on Monday he was standing in the stet, when a young woman came to him and sa,d she . thought there was a fire in Alcock-street. H 9 ran to Alcoa-street. No. 16, and saw sparks in the window. He heard a woman scream and tried the door. He could not get in. He tried the window and with great difficelty he got through a win low on the groond floor. He groped his way to the stairs, and got up them. He hard the wemen grooning and got hold of her and roiled her in the bed ling old strippe the burning clothes off her. Her flesh was very much barnt, and came off in his hands. He opened the window and shouted someone to fetch the doctor, and he wrapped the woman in a counterpale. She was crag exhausted bet asked him t) take of the re nainder of her clothes, and he was doing so when the doctor came in. The deceased toll him that she was in the habit of taking the oven shelf to bed will her, and that she had dropped the paraffin lamp on the way upstairs. Upon examining the house he found that the stairs had been partially burnt. He subsequently removed the deceased to the Workhouse in company with the relieving offieer.—Dr. McDougall said his attention was called to the case at about ten o'clock on the night of the accident. He ordered her removal to the Werkhouse Hospital as there was no means of traltinv, her there. fie would censider the Cause of death to Lie due to shock consequent on the injoriea received. The jury' reterael a verdict of " Death from shot* accidentally caused b 7 the injuries sustained by burn- ing." The Jell?, Coroner, and Pr. McDougall joined in ' expressing their high appreciation of the courage dis- played by Constable Fry in the dancers he had facel to relate the p,nr woman from her sad fate, and expressed the hop- that his conduct would be taken notice of by his serrrintendent, as they felt that such behaviour should have some recomty—se.</p>
      </div>
