      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN LI3FML CLUB. -- AN.NITAE MEEtING. SPEECH BY Slit j. T. BRUN-NE,t, BART., M.P.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LI3FML CLUB. -- AN.NITAE MEEtING. SPEECH BY Slit j. T. BRUN-NE,t, BART., M.P.</p>
        <p>TEIE FU fURE POLUY At' L03A.1, EL,EITIONS</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Ru .c orn Liberal Club was held at tee Clue Room, Begin •eat, oe Friday even- lag, and Was of a highly successful a id enthusi atic description. The chair was taken by Sr J. T. Brunner. Bart., M.P., (president of the club), who was supported by Messrs. A. R. Nerman' S. Taylor, W. Headley, R. H. Posnett, J. M. Frith,and T. Lea (chairman of the club). alessrs. A. Riley (secretary), and H. Moore (e-eisuirer) were also present. I Prior to the business of the annu d meetingethe Presi- dent and Vice-Presidents, entertained the company to an excellent meat tea (catered for by Mr. James L ea, High- street) to which ample justice was done. There was a good attendance. After the tables were cleared Sir Jolla Brunner (who received quite an ovation) addressed the meeting. Dealing first with the affairs of the Club, be sail a couple of wars previously or less he heel the pleasure of meeting some of the Club's mem- bers and discussing their &amp;uncial affairs and also wh et steps could be taken for the future. Ia regard to finances the result was that the Club had paid its way euriag the last year,and tad about £4 to the good 0A toe year's working, but the number of na embers nad fallen off. It was, however, a matter for coagr atulation in that they had begun to seriously discuss the future of the Club whilst it was still paying; its way. For often it happeeed that the members of a club did not coasidertheir position or put their shoulders to the wheel to restore prosperity until it had gone beyond recall. Ic was not the case with theis club. They were still in the position of heviting members to join,and ot inducing others to corn' baek and co eel tell them that they wsre still a thorougniy seund institution. The lack of fealties for billiard play- ing which was felt before the alterations in the buildine, which the c ub oceup ed some years ago, had been reMedied, but maybe th-re was not now sufficient ac- commodation for the readers. That probeon had been occupying the minds of some of those who hal met previous to treat meeting, and they had come to the conclusion t 3 do something t eat w u d increase the reading accommodation without in toe least affecting the billiard playing accommeletion. (A.pp.ausee He advised the committee to call together the whole of the members of the club, as the membership ernbr Iced men who ciull d) every blessed thing. (Applause.) They had carpenters, ourlders, architects, and plumbers, and in fact everything; ; and there *as no need to go out of the Club for advice. lodet thee- members utornprieed a large class of thoroughly cernpetent men. S.) nine i for tee affairs of the club, the more f menet business of which they would take up by-ane-b. Speaking in a general character, Sir John Brunner said he was tild that there was a good deal of feeling and seine erneurit of discussion, in Ruacorn, as the ,re had been in Nene- wich and Middlewich, with regard to the recent course taken by the besiness concern with which he was con- neat)]. He was perfectly well aware that his rights were their property as well as his own. And be was perfecdy well aware that when he defended their repu- t aloe he defended his own. He would, therefore, pro- c led to defeni his reputation against the hidden form such as was abroad with reference ti their action, and in a few minutes run over the history of the business which his friend D. Mood and him- set established in vVinsford in the year 1873 After ding so, Sir John said that trade with the Uartei States of America was a mere fraction of what it was three years ago. The film had to do with less labour and therefere decided that the man wee hed been the snortest time in their services should go, and not the old men. (dear, hear, and applause.) Included in these men were a number of o'd men. The principle was net the man's age out the tune he hid been there. (Applause.) Tnerefora it was absolutely untrue that the I firm had discharged old men bec east they were old I men. (Hear, hear.) Where there was ate old ma i who I had seen with them a large number of sears—and th.re were a number of thern—tney were given light , jobs, and when it was found there were I men gone beyond even these light j obs they were pensioned off in cases of necessity. One other personal matter and he had done blowing his , own trumpet. (Laughter.) It had been sail by a gentleman who sat on the Nerthwich Sanitary Board that Brunner Mond and Company's houses were net I what they should be. He cealteeged comparison with any set of houses built for any employee through the Length and breath of the country. He coatended that they only received 3 per cent. return fer their houses out of the rents. Is to overcrowding arid iesmication,they were most careful t 3 leek after the -a +rare areays. mints and in the whole of their 204 houses there were only two cases approaching, to over- crowding, and one of them was the rtslt 4.1 tw) girls coming :nettle temper idly, and Coe other wee a babe. (Len shter and aeolause.) Referring to local affairs. Sir John Brunner s id there had been a recent coatest as they knew fer the electien of pers ins to serve tesna nn the School Bo trd, and that centest caused a great deal if controversy, and in that c natroversy he was aware that some of the ereuments used were not over scrupulouo, aid he believed tie it the lives of the Liberal c andi late's were scanned with a tnicroseopic scrutiny. He one- damned the introduction of persenalities in thee() m etters and hoped tee Liberals of Rancorn wonkl take steps to step it, and j oie him in condemning it. He had ventured to write to trio lo :A papers 3. lo:t .r whittle no doubt A great, many of them 4ad seen,an,l ms,dering .he matter of great imp ertence he c ingest thit letter t 1 tee cereuletee with cmsiaerable care throughout tic town. He die not think the q ,trrel tie had tied wite th e Rev. tV Stables was wortu their atteetieri. i(e.)pla,us ) But o the Vizer himself ear a considerably more impse:lnc person. He wro-e am reedress which tie pubished in his Ii parish Marvz no " and wit I as et ad tress he (t speaker) prepose I to deal at a very seem; length. Th. Vicar said in that letter" mat enquiry had been m ado into the rum eir that the N nicenforrnisc children in one of tee C iurcmi seta) 'is had been addressed by a mi t ',erg in terms watch were said to tee eine&amp; t i t is people." A school which as almost eruArelv mntiutainel oet of public morieyeeh mid on the site of the managers be held',' in t tut f r the public, and he rill not think that the rnenagers of thi school should &amp;low themeelves to Use the in .nay amid those • buildings, the whose administres Lion of whice was paid for out of public money in such a way as to hurt the ' roofings of the parents who were forced t send their ch !erten there. (Applause.) He charaeterised the in- quiry as a farce. They knew in the examination of a child as to what had been. weld and wait w is to be said; e-pecielly before those pe )pie whom the child did not wise to offend. They kaew now tenderly children had to b 3 handle I, and hw they did at Card to sav a iy- thing disagree able to the curs of those who (pest tele i them. If it was to be a lair trial, he centsnded, that half of the court should have been Non-c onf ormists (Hear, hear and loud 'ape' euse ) He wee vril gad to near of the foundation in Runcern of a Free Cnerch Council, and it should be borne ire mind that the Free Cburch Council had not ben formed in a -.tee" mime to the Church of England. It had been to:me i for th e defence of the rights of Nonconformists. (Aoplause.) And be claimed that no Churchman oueht to at tack those rights, and no Cnurchrnan should deny to I the Nonconformist the same rights as they t item-elves possess-ti. He expressed a hope that amongst other timings the Free Church Council would defend the rights I of the chiialree, and that when anything was said in the schools teat ceignt not to be said that the Fr ere Church Couucil woutd bring it to the ears of the public and shame much action. (Loud applause.) Dolling with general politics, the speaker said that the world had been greatly excited be a letter which appeared in tee p-o 3 ere some short time ag ), from Sir William Herceurt, ad also one from Mr. Jouu Morley. (Haar, hear.) de greatly r egreted thee course t Ikea by Sir Wi ham Har- court and Mr. John Morley. 'indeed (Firing his Peelle- meetery career, he hat taken a pleasure arid a pride ie following Sir William Haman, for there had not heett a more doughty fighter .for Linerel prin. elpels on the franc opposition bench. (Applause.) He greatly regretted the loss the party would sustain by the attitude of Mr. John Morley, fo: the r'ason that there was no man living from whom the Englishman —and the Irishman, and the Scotchmey, and the Welshman—there was no man living from wheel they I had learnt nobler lessons in liberal principle; than Mr. John Morley. He cetild essure them that there was .no lack of ability on the front Opposition Beach of the House of Commons, there was no leek of tree c urage, no lack of tact and no lack of energy amongst tee leaders of tee Liberal parte. (Applause.) They bed beard aegreat deal at old age pensions hut aline present Government had been able to do was to appoint a. Reyal Comraession, and although 100 proposals were. submitted by that Commission every rine 'of them hod bren r+ected as imperfect. Ito- ferring to the re- edjustment of taxation, Sir John said it was a very good rule, to be just before being generous. He pointed out that the poor people paid comp iratively the heaviest rate. His contention sees that the bulk of the taxation should be borne by the rich man, or at least that be should be taxed uuun time actual value of the property. 'Take for example the case of a person possessing land ear to an important centre. Lend suitable for building purposes. Tne pos- se,sor of that land paid rates upon a small sum, the equivalent of land outside the area, and was not taxed upon the price ne wanted to sell it at. fie contended that that should be dole, and if it was, the resett would be that the man would have ta sell, and so the poor , people could buy ground sufficient to build I roomy hou,es upon at a reasonable cost, and I there would not be that overcrowding which was the result of the present system. The people would be healthi-r and surrounded with a better moral atmosphere. (enplausee Haying submit- ted farther illustrations, Sir John continumg, sail the Liberal party had been accused of being against the extension of tte empire, and the Tory party were said to be the true Imeerialists. It was not so. The Liberal party were not,oppesel to the r'xtemio,:s of the empire at all. But they were ?pn)s,d to its ex'.'enc,ion by so called Limited Liability Compinai. (Applause.) He Could assure than that, there was a large ashoant of</p>
        <p>cark,al afloat in London for any unscrupulous person so disposed to take up for flu pur- pose of opening new colonies. And latterly they had heard a great deal aboat "front page directors "of companies of that nature. (Laughter.) Personally he believed in opening new markets, but he preferred they should deal with peoplu who wore clothes in preference to those without. (Increased laughter.) In the course of his remarks Sir John also expressed himself favourable to the Czar's peace proposal. In his opinioa it would tend, naturally, to the re Juction of taxation.and correspondiugly increased trade with other nati0n3.£63,000,000 of money was an enormous amount and if the ineohla tax payer paid it it would be some- thing liko 23 61 in the pound, yet, all that money was paid by the people unknown t.) teernselves by the pur- chase of tobacco. spirits., Sz3. Tnerefore he recoin- men,del to their consideration the proposal the Czar of Russia had given to the world. He concluded by stating how pleased he was to be ana)ngst them, ani th.t tile reason he did not appear oftener in their miast was the vr Liat of an invitation.but should:they invite him at any time they could depend upon it he would come., (Loud app:auie.) It was his businets then to call upon the Secretary to read the minutes of the last annual meeting. (Loud and .coatinued applause.)</p>
        <p>, . The minutes were duly confirmed. THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Riley (secretary) submitted the annual repor: for the year ended 31st Docember, which stated that though only to be regarded as a 'quiet year it neverthe- less resulted in their having met all the expenditure out of the year's reveaue,and had also leit a balance in hand of £8 2s 6.1 wilier] as usu ii had been applied to the re- duc:33on of the debt on toe club's premises, which now stands at the coriaparatively small suns of £250 121 101. Toe income for the year hal been derived from the fol- lowing sources : Msmber3 suhscriptioas, £2l lls 61 ; billiard table uceipte, £9B 23 81 ; entrince fees (handi- cap), £2 23; sale of refreshments, 103 21 I; sale of papers, us ld,wrrich with the balacce in hand at the commence- ment of the year, £18; 61, male the total income of £124 Sc 141. The expebditure incluld: Caretaker's salary, and commissiont£44 03 91; papers and magazines, £7 las 9d : billiard tAb e requisites, £1 03 11.1 ; repairs to club premises, £4 8 2 I ; handicap priz s, 103 ; secretirv's salary, £7 10;; a total expeaditure of £ll9 .5s 6lrd. The membership at the close of the year was lesi than during the previous year, but this it was hoped would only prove of a temoorary nature—;hear, hsar—probably contribatcl to by a variety of eircum- statues. 'There were now happily indications that dur- ing this year a considerable accession of members might b 3 looked for. The general conduct of the club had re- ceived the anxious attentioa of the conarnittes during the past year, and the removal of the c tretaker to Opine he nariug hem appointed steward of cue Liberal Club ia that to orn, necessitated a change in the hours during which the club bad been open. Oabof the members Mr. T. Ei. Carruthers was appointei earetaker.ansl the hours o: °atoning, fixed at 2 p.m. on Wocinesdays and Saturdays and 6pm. on the other evenings of ths week. These changes had, the commit re believed, been satisfactory t) the nvirribsr3 and while mere ing their wants had also resulted in a saving of 6:rpm:di:um Tna committee lead for Siale time tett that the present structural at aagsment of the club premises did not allow of their making suitable provision for the literary and educational side of its work. A considerably increased active member- ship would no doubt lead to a saisfac7.ory 6o!w,ion of the difficulty and the party would as a consequace gain in power and influence. (Applause.) 'rne report was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Samuel 'Taylor, who was enthlOastically re- ceived, said he had a very pleasing duty to perform in prapasing the president for the ensuing year. It would be a waste of words, and a waste of time for him to say aaything itt favour of their present president, Sir John Brunner—(applause)— Arho was so well known in Run- corn to all parties. .113 (the speaker) could not say muca but tie w )uld say that Sir Joha Brunaer was one of the greatest and best friends tnat Runcorn had—with his large heart and great generosity it was not even a questioa of politics. (Ap- cilium) Sir John had referred to local matters. As they were aware they had been up to the neck in electioneering matters daring the last couple of months. Lie could malt' sty, now that it was over he took a very ilfferent view of local elections ta what he had don-e. (Rear, hear, and applause.) Tney knew his policy had nem to keep politics out of local matters—and he lot- Hayed in that still if they could ge: their app -meats tot) the same f eeling—but when they would take a !voltage of everytlaing, aid make believe they were their gre 'test frialds, whilst they took everything, and gave nothing, he felt jascine I in changing his opinion. Referring to the GiuntyC.sunc 1 elac i ia. Mr Taylor said it was with no desire to upset the Liberal pare that he to )k the view he had done during the last 20 yatrs. 0 L the other hand he was a N tecoeformist,, tk,- ing taken to the NenconForrnist Chapel from the time he was 3 years old, aid h e st.t -midst is ever sine • and we V; still, and schen,v.er th sir iltr)sts were at sztk he was found to be on their side. (Rear. hear ) Ile could assure them, therefore that in everything affecting' their intereet he would willingly aet,and expres ;941 c )ofi lience in winning the next County ClunciFelectinn. (Aoplaute) Having referred to the I ecessity for every member of the club in tking a serio is effort on it s behalf in the direc- tion of maintaining thi interest a-id increasing the mein- borship, 3itr. Taylor concluded by again expressing the pleasure he telt at submitting Sir John Brunner's name for the presidency. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. i. R Norman seconded the resolution. He was sure they ciuld not have a b -Met president. fie ervioNed every word that Mr. Taylor had tit-Ared. He believe I th-r- -was 113 ono present in that room who enjiyed what Mr. Taylor lead said more than himself. As far as Mr. Taylor was concerned his principles were not changel. But fa, a c irtsilerab'e time he objected to palitics in local affairs, and the othrr side always made a great cry of no politics whear,:wr they thought they were going t ) rece;ve a rap, but when they got a charics they turned round and jr was all Church And politics. That being AO he we very glad that Mr. Taylor had taken the action he hid. Personally he had felt the result of Mr. Taylor's action.Re advised that in futur they should go up in political lin.ss. (Applause.) Referring to the great clanga that ha I ems over the Libbral pa.rt,y:Mr. Nor- man said they should remember that for many years before the death and retirement of Mr. Gladstone, the Liberal party possessed a leader who was head and shoulders above his collea :nes and oven contemporaries. And whilst Mr. G-lsoistooe was stil their loader, he (the speaker) used to ask himself what would bscorne of the party wh in they lost him. It was a sort of reaction. , (Applause). Toe rank and file of the party were muted, an it only remained for the leaders t ) bscorne so, ant ke believrid it w, u! I s sob hi br night about. (applause.) Re had groat ploa.sure in seconding the rtsolutioo. (Loud app'aii se). Mr. H. El P hut. tt supoore I the re iolution. In doing so, he said if it was an invi'ai in that Sir Jahn required, thy ward vv. ir ti; Y 1 rh at nighr,—(laughter an lao- plause)—and hi hop d Mr. Taylor would couple the in- vitation witis rh resolu i (Rear, heir.) They would be delighted to hive Sir Jiien amon ;st them as there were several rn atter.; of political interest which h s for one would like t It ,ar eKoitindsd. Fir example the Bill fer Ssconlary El ication. Then there, wts t se qle,,- tion of .the Churn;. Re suppored toe resolution wita pie .sure. (A.oplause.) Mr. Taylor pat thJ proposition,- which was carried with acclaniat;on.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner, briefly resonviing, thanked the meeting' for the compliment. Mr. Taylor bid said he was very well kaowa. His ()Onion was he hid becortio better known is some (porters. (Laughter.) He Was pow known to come pi,ipte is a diff areat cnir. tc.er. Is one of the local papers hi hid kit rather hard ia,sup- port of the rights of Nonconfairmiit children in Churh School and should omasion arise he was isreparod to hit harder. (Applause.) Mr. Taylor'd spee:h toe hid lie- tamed to with great pleasure, and he was glad toar, Taylor had now m ids up his mind to sup oort tie Liberal porty. (Loud applause) Os the proposition of Mr. T. Dian.secanded by Mr.T. Wood, the re-election of the retiring vice-presidents was unanhfltUaly cirried. ' Mr. rir. Brimslow proposed, and Mr. Wesley Cooke seconded the re-electon of Mr. A. Riley, toe retiring secretary. Both gentlemen paid a high compliment to Mr. Riley's worth.—Toe motion was unanimously cnrrisd. Mr. R. Moore, the retiring treaaurer, was uoani- mously re-electei, on the inotioajof Mr. J. Stauvvay, sec,nied by Mr T. Loa. Mr. W. .11audny, moved the re-,lection of tir audi- tors Messrs. J. doiker and H. Chtim:cf. Mr. Clarke seconded, and the coveting, unanim,wly assented. The following ,wer elected the co ornirtme for doe eh- suing year :—Messr. John Bate, W. B. Brimelew, John Clarke, Joon Elhotr.J.dolliday.John Jones,James dcllev, Edward .1' ohnsol. Jiines Loa. W 'ire- R•ley, George R. smith, Thomas Wood, George Whitbearl. Slit John Brawler having to depart, expressed a wish that the meetiog tvou'd organise thc'ncevei alai forward a requisition to the Chair non of toe D s riot Council asking him to riaa am -)t cnnqi le, the Uz tr's peacla proposal, an 1 w:mither Ruocorn sh mid join in the almost universal support of it. As Sr John left to -iv:en his train, the coinnany sang for " Fl t's a j oily good tallow," and three hearty elvers wore rois</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT FATAL FALL OF PLANKS. COUNCIL. THE RESULT OF THE RECENT STORM. The meeting of the above council was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday. There were present :—Messrs S Taylor (chairman), W Handley, D Bisbrown, E ttni- son, J Wilson, W Collier. T asan, G Hewitt, J Horsefield Jtmes Pritchird, J H Grice. J W Lightburn, F Like (law clerk),E Marshall (secretary),J Wilding (surveyor), and Dr F McDougall (medical offimr).</p>
        <p>THE DEATH RATE. Dr. l'acDougall repnrtei 22 deaths during the month, two of which wera subjects for inquests ; being an aver- age of 15 per cent per thvu4and estimatel population, or 137 per cent, exclusive of inquest cases ; 43 births were registered. During the corresponding period list year 23 deaths and 56 births were reaisterei.</p>
        <p>lIIPEOVEDIENT OF CUMIN CHAPZL. The Highways and Buildings Committee recommended that the plan of Mr. R. Abel of a bay WiIIOOPI and porch to be constructed at Crofton Lodge be approved; and the plan Gf the Trustees of Cam Jen Wesleyan Chapel of a porch proposed to be con,tructed at suet' chapel ba approved ; that an additional lamp be fixed in Moaghland-lane. near Trinity Vicarage, and that legal pioceedings be taken against the following persons tG Mlnover instal- ments and interest in arrear in respect of Private Street Works Account .—Messrs William Bate, William Gibbon, E. Prince, Henry White, Exors. T. Borstal!, T. Andrews, William Bankes, Trustee of the Rose of Sharon Lodge of Oddfellows, Edward Owen, J. Hudson, G Partington, R. Grounds, F. Rigbv, J. Rayner. J. Vose. Samuel Kennerley„ also Mn. M. A. Co r and Mrs. Grounds. Mr. D. Bishrown in proposing the adoption of the minutes of the Highway and Buildings Committee ex- plained that the committee made the recommendation to proceed against the var;ous persons named as scrt of " Hilegon'a choice." Since the recommendation was made Mesas E Prince, r Andrews, 11 Gerunds, Samuel Kentierlay, and Mrs M A Cox had paid, and he propose the adoption of toe minutes with those names excluded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewitt asked whgther the perioas had been served with the proper notices. The —lees explained that the prver notices had b2ea ser red.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Da questioned whether anything would be gatnel by the pabication of the names of the parties, and thought steps should be taken to prevent them be- coming public. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Bistorown tanught nothing was to los' gained by an ooposite course. The Chairman pointed hut th et alrad7 the names were in print on the agead ts, and consequently public property. Mr J. Wilson sennded, and the proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>lOn Thursday afternoon, Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner. held an inquest at the Police Court, Run- corn, on the body of John Handley, aged 31, who met with an accident at the Wiggs Works on January 12t1 last by a plank falling upon his head. —Mr. C. Linaker, solicitor, Runcorn, represented the widow; Mr. Newman appeared for the Man.. cheater Ship Canal Coy., and Mr. F. J. Norman on behalfof the United Alkali Coy„ (Wiggs Works). Evidence of identification was given by Mrs. Sarah Ann Handley, 13, Stanley-street. Her hus- band was brought home in an unconscious con- dition on the night of the great storm (January 12th) having met with an accident. He was 31 years of _age, and a joiner. Since the accident her husband had been attended by Dr. McDougall. He died on the 31st inst. Daniel Exleston (joiner) stated that he raided in Ann-street, and on the right of the accident he came in company with the deceased from the works.Thera. was a terriffic storm raging. As he and d =ceased were going past the Old Quay House they heard a noise. He staggcr,ed back and the next thing he remembered was that Eiandley was lying on the floor with a plank across him. Witness had also fallen. Be did not see any LI the planks fall and did not known where they had Come from. He sp Are to Handley lout got no aaswer. Mr. Dyer and another man came up and gave assistance to Handley and he was conveyed to the house. Be attn. buttd the fall of the plar,k; to the force of the storm raging.—ln anlwn. to Mr. Linaker he said there was no doubt in his mind that the planks came from the saeds which were in course of construction.</p>
        <p>John Dyer, High-street Runcorn: gave corroblrative evidence, and Mr Philip Pritchard, engineer of the corn. luny, gave ev:dmce as to the construction of th venti- lat-ir. It appeared that the ventilator was not quite completed, and constquently the wind was able to get under and force the planks off. The building, if any- thing, was above the usual strength. He had no doubt in his mind, but tbat the boards which fell u.;on Hand. ley came from that unfinished ventilator. Dr. F. McDougall, Ru4corn, stated he was called in to the deceased about half past five to six when he received informatim of the acidet. Having another case to at. tend to it was same time before he could give his atten- tion t 9 the case. He went to Handley's house several times but found that Handley had not been bnuglat home. He COUR&amp; rnt understand why but on going to the works he fourhst a number of people had been delayed ..wing to the difficulty of getting over tbe swing bridge acroFs the Ship Canal,and that wag why Handley had not been brought home. On examination he found Randle i t) be suffering irom a scalp wound about 6 or 7 inches long extending zig-zag across the forehead in the direction ot the left eye, he was in a semi-conscious state,and paralysed from the middle down: The doctor was of opinion that dekth, which resulted on the 31st inst., was due to a form nt pneumonia set up by lack of vitality caused by the accident.</p>
        <p>Atter a retirement of about quarter of an hqur the jury returned a verdic: of "Accidental deith caused by the boards of the ventilator falling upon him," The jury exprassed an opini.an tnat if the working of the Swing Bridge, had ben effectively carried out the man's life might have been saved. The jury pa:sed a vote of conthlence with the widow.</p>
        <p>Mr. Newman explained that they had done all in their power to close the bridge and hid all the available macual power, and tw,) steam engines working, and but for a lull in Vac storm they could not have got it cllsed at all. He would like to say, for he thought the jury might not be aware of the fact, that a lattice worked bridge offele.l more resist trice to the wind than any other kind. He felt callfd upon to make that explanaw tion.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnigLtly mating of this Council was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse. Th.ra were present: —Messrs. J Knowles, T Wright, J B Chadwi2k, C E Linak,r, E Ciaike,T Darlington, J M Wifliaum, J Tudor, P Leather, H Talbott, J G Kydd, C Roylance, J Spruce, T Baxter, J Whitlow, J Mclntyre, E Lewis, T Walling- ton, G Pilling, J W Baguley, W Pickering, J Edwares,T Antwiss, Admiral Jones Parry, with Mr J Ashtm (clerk) Mr G F Ashton (deputy clerk), Mr J Farrington (inspec- t)r),Mr W Diggle,and Mr J Swiaton (surveyors),and Dr Adams (medical officer).</p>
