      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>THE WATER COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>Made the follo ming r commendations—That the water nsain from the coatinuation of Percival-lane to the tow- ing-path of the Runcpra and Westun C4nal to diverted at an estimated cost of £lB, and that the ten&amp;r of The Staveley Coal &amp; hoe Company for the supply of 3" iron pipes be aclopted. The Water Committee recom- mended that a 3" water main be laid down Westfield- road from Crofton Lodge at an estimate.l cost of £125, and that application be made to the Local Govern m, nt Bard for tour eacction to a loan for such sum for a period of 30 years. Mr. ,Ta nis Pritchard moved and Mr. J. Wilgon'secon- ded, titac effect be, given to the above, which was unani- mously carriel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>HEATH AND COHAION:</head>
        <p>THE HEATH AND COHAION:</p>
        <p>The Parliament try amd Genet. tl Purposes Committee recornmend.ad that the Bills of Costs of the Law Clerk and Measrs. B4ker, Lees, and Company. in relation to the application for and o'ot lining of a Provisional Order from the Board of Agriculture for the regulation of Run- corn 'Path and the Bill to confirm the same ba for- warded for taxation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Eland ey explained that the Law Clerk had gener- ouily give.n his services up to a certain stage in the pro- ceedinln gratis, and that th-!Cauneil hal get off cheaply in the matter of obtaining the Provisional Order. He moved the adoption ol tho rec)rnmtmdation. Mr. D. Biibrown seconded and the motion was carried. APPROVAL OF TICE CZAR'S MANIFESTO.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary Comtnittle reported that a com- munication h td been receivAl from a committee of which the Ririt Rsverend the Bishop of London was the Chair- man asking ill tt rssolu:ions be passed by representative b idles and through °tier channels relative to the Con- ference prop )sed by th 3 Emperor of Ru:sia to arrest the growth nf armam •nts. They therame recommended no adoption of the followi.ig props3al and th it the Seal of the Council be affiwd to the resolution :—".That this Council cordially approves the pr)posal of th 3 Ernperar of Russia suggt sing the convening of an International Conference having as its primary object ths arr3sting of the growth of armarneuts, als3 cordially aFproves the action of her Mtjesty's Ministers in giving energetic and hearty support to the proposal, and expresses the hope that the noble initiative of the Emperor will b:4 crowned w•tl3 euecess and that the Conference will realtie the fruitful resu!ts which it is designed to secure."</p>
        <p>Mr. Handley in proplsing the resolution above and further that copies of it bn sent to Lord Salsbury and the BisiAnp of London, admitted it was outside tile duties of an Urban District Council to deal with such resolu- tions, but contended ti4t the matter was of such im- portance as tojustify them doinzanytlaing to strengthen the feeling ia the town noun such a matter. Mr. D. Bisbrown seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Imison tnough6 that the consideratio,n of the matter wai not within its propir channel. and if as Mr. Handley nad said thPy wen to c)nsider matters that affected thl peoplo,thev would be wasting time over the discussions of such subjects as vaccination, local option etc. As a matterof ord.r ar_d a guide to the future he should like to know whe:lin Iva was right in his conten- tion.</p>
        <p>Th 9 Chihli:tan explaineN that the requisition had been seat to him, and he submitted it to tie committee und-r the imprAssion that it would, as Mr. Irdsie had said, save considerable time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Iraison adde I that he had regariel the suggestion made by the member for the Division, at a recent meet- ing held in the town as the best course, and a public m.etirt, on the lines suggested, would have been irst. Mrr Tay'or explained that ho was present at the meeting referred to and had informed tho in'ner that the Council had the tmtion under cmsideratioit. Considerable di tcussion castled. Mr. Griller thought a patriotic c.ornmittee ought to be formed. (Laughter.) Subsequently the motion was carri.d. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Wilson reporttd that the Techtfcal Instrultion Committee had reconsidered the plan of the pr)posed New Wing to the Technical Institute and decided to sub- mit an amended elan on the grouni the.% it was in ad- visable to spend the money which would be required to carry cut the original plan. He submitted the accep- tance of necessary contracts and the approval of the amended plan, als-) that applieati )n he made to tne Cheshire County Council fir an increased grant in aid of the Institute. The elmmittvi further gu'-nnitted the conlirmation of the appoititment ef Miss Mary Westmers Wavertree, Liverpool, asiis;ant mistresa, in the plaea of Miss Wigl ,r ..-,igned. at a salary of £BO per annum. The clst of t.t,e buildingetc., would be £530 and of that sun Sir Jahn 13runner had pronaissd £3OO, therefore it only remained for th'm to provide the b3lanc- £230. lie considered that ths COuntv COII3CII might give them an increased grant, and that the school fees etc., would ass4t then in obtaining the rest. knyhosv, ther had no intention to mortgage the rites or go into debt over it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier calved considerable discussion as to the aublect,which was not whally inserted upon tha agenda, being in order.N. The Standing Orders were tnerefore suspended. Mr. Imison without wishing to throw cold water upon the scheme considered that Mr. Wilion was too opt mis- tic. He understood that the County Council would Le unable to increase their grant,as already all the svailable funds had been allocati d dr ear-markei if not appropriated. Sa that half fro n that source was daubtful. Then scith regard to the School paying its way, he did not think they could depend upon that. For the demands of the Government and the Department each saceeeling ycar were consider- ably More expensive if ca-red oat. He hoped they would be prepared to make se ne soeci4l eff prt if the resnlution was passel. He wa,lttiti them to go into it with their f-y.3s opened, and nat as on a former occasion go upon optimistic grounds. He thought the money would have to be raised. The Chairman hoped that such a sum as £230 would not be the ca.us3 of them having to aunt the schools and turning the 6ceolara iota the streets. (Hear, hear). The rrtrAiqn vla in .11c,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THELWALL MATTERS</head>
        <p>THELWALL MATTERS</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Thelwdll Fetish --Council (Mr. W. B. Cook; wrote stating that the estimate for piping tbe Thelwall village stream was hid before the Council at their Ist meeting and it was decided to r luest the Dis-• trfct Council to execute the work as soon as possible. He was instructed to ask why this work should lxi charged entirely upon the township of Thelwall seeing that the District C)uocil had already executed a similar work in the township at a cost to the District rate. Mr Cook also wrote stating that tho Parish Council were of opinioa that the Teewall Ferry was being satisfactorily worked at the present time on similar lines to the Old Manor Ferry.</p>
        <p>- Admiral Jones Parry slid he was waiting for the anawer as t 3 why it was chargeable t 3 the Parish. Tile Clerk said it was a sewerage work ; It was a sanitary matter, arid eaca township paid for its owa sanitary works.</p>
        <p>Admiral Parry thought that if the pond was cleared or; and he believel it could be done at a co3t of £s,and the roadman was to spend about half-an-hour a week it would be sufficient to keep the channel clear. He pro. posed the Council ask that that bo done. Mr. J. Mclntyre secsnded.</p>
        <p>In answer to the Chairr.an, Mr. J. Farrington (inspec- tor) said the &amp;wind was a sewer, and there was sewer— acrq in the pond which c)uld ba clianed at a charge of £5, which ha consiJered would be chargeable to the parish.</p>
        <p>TYPHOID FEVER AT FRODSHAISI. Letters wen read from the Rev. H. B. Blozg (vicaz),. and Mr. Francis Boiton, Cadov,an House, Frodsham, c tiling attention to an,allegei outbreak of typhoid and enteric fever at Main-street,Frodsham; ewe case had ter- minated fatally, and Mr. Boston had caused another to be removed to Chest:2. Infirmary. They asked that something should be done imme liately in the matter. Mr. J. M. Williams stated that the cases were serious, and he thought hat something ought to be done so as to authors o the doctor t ) deal with such cases,in future. Dr. Adams said he visited the place and wished to make the fol!owing re :ommendations : that the privies, which he explainel were in a t ,rrible state, be abo'ished and rrbuilt on another site ; that the sub-sofi awl the sites should be removed Co a depth of about 4 feet, and substitated with new. Attention was also called to the open sewers in the vicinity of the house, that the houses should have separate drain grids, and that privy accommodation should b provided for each separately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GU Mt DI ANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GU Mt DI ANS.</p>
        <p>} The fortnightly meeting of the above was held at : Dutton Worknouse,on Tuesday. There were present :- 1 ' MeFsra. J. M. Williams (chairman), S. Tivlor,J.Meadow- croft, T. Reynolds, the Runt District Councillors, with Mr. John Ali ton (clerk), and Mr. George F. Ashton I (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>BOABD-ROOM EXTENSION. Mr. E. linollys, assstant secreta.y to the Local Government Board, acknowledged the letter of the Board, requesting sanction to the expenditure by the Guardians of a sum not exceeding £330 in connection with the enlargement of the Board-room at the Work- house in addition to the £5OO authorised by the order of the s:h J4nuery, 1898. Be pointed out that the sum of £lBO and £67 expended on furniture and repairs rt- ape tively did not require the sanction of the Board. Toe Board.did not WiSt4 to exprrss any opinion with re- gard to these items but they wcu!d not refuse to sanc- tion their being peid for out of the proposed loan. The Board would address a further communication to the Guardians as to the frees to be taken befere they issued the necefe ary order authorising the additional expendi-• tine ot £B3 en the alterations in connection with the Board-room and the borrowing of a sum not exceeding £330 to defray the cost of the whole of the work to be repaid in 20 years. THE PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEE. rbig committee reported that teey had taken into consideration the vari,ais resolutions delegated to them from the last naeeteag, from other fluids of Guardians, and they decided th.t they 6houll be supported. Mr. J. Mcletyre m )ved the adoption of the Perna- meettry Committee's ;open. —Mr . C.Lioaker seconded. Mr. J. Meadoweroft said the varioui questions were of such itnportarce and involved so many vital points that they ehould be considered :eparately. It was ex- traordinary procedure. After some discussien Mr. J. Meadowcroft proposed that the 'diEctisaion of the resolutions be deferred. Mr. J. Edwards seconded. On ave t 3 being taken the original proposition was carried, ouly two voting for Mr. Meadowcroft's proposi- , bion.</p>
        <p>Subsequently Mr. J. Meade wcroft was elected upon the Parliamentary Cornmit tee.</p>
        <p>FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thrmas Wright sa-m—ltTed—the ficancialstatement. Out 1 e is grantfcl ;Is follows: Runcorn £3s;Frod. sham £25; Badworth £2l.</p>
        <p>ORGAN RECITAL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday an oigan recital was given in the Golborne-street BaiAist Church by Air. Thomas Lane. The items given were Andante (Smart),. Cantilene (Jutes Grison), Bercen se (Botting), March (Niendelsschn). Mr. George Gough sang "The mighty River" and Mr. Levi Rinhurdson, " Come. unto Me " (Cowen.) The various pieces were well rendered and lii,tenA to by the majority of the congregation. A collection WdS made for choir fonds. Next &amp;Inlay evening the last of the series wid be gisen by Mr. W. H. Payton (Mug. Bac).</p>
