      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS FELLOW</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Boreugh Police Court on Wednes- day, before Mr. GE dies (chairman) and Mr. Birchall, Jahn Maddock was charged with being drunk and dire orderly in Annie-street, the previous day; and also on same date with committing wilful damage to windows to the amount of £2 ; with assaulting Mary Dentoe; and with using threats towards his wife Matilda Jane Mad- dock.—Defendant pleaded gui!ty to being drunk and to the wilful damage.—P.C. Muskett said he was requested by prisoner's wife to ge to him. Prisoner admitted breaking the windows and said he" would taki tier life Some other time; they had best lock me up." He was drunk.— Martha Broadhurst, Sharp-street, gave evidence in respect to the ' damage. Her mother (Mary Denton) was struck in the face by defend- ant for interfering to prevent her (witness's)father being struck.—Mary Denton, 37, Sharp-street, said sho saw prisoner break the windows at 11, Annie-street—Mr. Shaw, owner of the property,said the damage would be about £2.—Met'lda. Jane Maddock, wife of defendant, said he bad not been living with her for five months and did not allow her any money, He came in the house on the day in questton and when she askei him ito go out he threatent d her. He said he had come to finiati her. Witness had to send out for two tram to protect her. She was afraid of her life and afterwards sent for P.C. Muskett.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had been 13 tinvei before the Cott t. His conduct bad been described as being more like that ot a wild beast than that of a man. Ile was an exceedingly dangerous man, drunk or sober.—The bench fined defendant 51. and costs or seven days' hard labour. for being drank ; two months' hard labour for the wilful damage ; two nionths hard labour for assaulting Mary Denton ; and defend- ant would have to find two sureties of £5 each to be of good b3haviour for six month., or 14 days' imprison- ment in default, for usual threate towards his wife ; each term to follow the ether. A LOCAL PUBLICAN AND HIS-CUSTOMERS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, at Daresbury Petty Sessions, before Mr. J. C. Parr and other magistrates, Mr. Home, of Mes3r3. Roberts, Dickson, and Barne,selicitors, Chester, applied. to the maYistrates to order Mr. Alfred Broady, landlord of the Walton Arms, Higher Walton, to eater into reeog- nizances of £5O ts appear at the next Knutsford Quarter Sessions to answer an indictment for having refused to to starve two men on two different occasions with reason- able refrashment. The case was before the last Quarter Sessions. He produced the certificate of the Clerk of the Peace of such indictment having been found.—Mr. A. Browne.of Warrington, said he was instructed ta defend at the Next Quarter Sessions. Two off,!nces were com- mitted, one whilst M.:. Broady was at church, the man having gone to the back do,r of the house and frightened the servan's. Oa the other occation Mr. Broady recognized the man as a Warringtocian, and having only two miles to go, refused to served him. The indictment having been found, the magistrates cluld dc netbing but commit, and Mr. Broady, having kept the house for 25 rears without a flaw against him, might easily be ord,red to nter into recognizances of 53. and not such a sum as £so—Toe magistrates fixed the Wm at 15.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINO it CASES.</head>
        <p>MINO it CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DOROUnII POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAT.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Roberts. Dr. Gornall, Mr. Junes Hepherd and Councillor</p>
        <p>Smethurst. A 'Bu „, DRIVER FlNED.—Heary, Bradbury, 44, Manchester- road, was charged with illtreating two horses by working them whilst in an unfit state on January 13tn.--P.C. Brad- ley said he raw defendant's 'bus in Horsemarket-strect on the day in question. He noticed the horses were restless and witness drew the attention of Serge nit Evans to them. They found two sores on the horses one two inches across and the other the size of a two shilling piece. There was blood on the collar also.—Defendant admitted the horses had sores, but said he put them in the 'bus in the dark, and did not notice their candition.--The Bench indicted a flue of 10s and costs or 14 days in default. IdestniY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Aldermac Blackhurst, Councillor SmPthurst, and Mr Birchtll. BRELCH OF THE PEACE.—John Green. 31. Victoria-street. games Hill. 6, Dakins Court, James Meakin, 24, Orchard- street, Thomas Barlow, 27, Orchard-street, and jatnes Nor- man, 19. Dakins Court, were all summoned for committing a breach of the peace in Churchstreet, on the 23rd January. P.C. neddington proved the eases.—The Chief Constable said the footpath was rendered almost impassably opposite the Gladstone Arms by lads.—The magistrates fined defendants in the costs._ -</p>
        <p>_ CASES OF Oesmucriox,—The folowing were summoned for causing obstruction with vehicles on the 25th January James Dagnall, Clough Hall-yard. wita donkey and cart in Market-street; William 111cHroy. wheelcart. in Horse- market-Street; Marlin Bowen, School Brow, with pony and er rt. in Lyme-street; Robert Sampson, School Brow, with handcart in Market-etreet.—P.P.Otough proved the ( %ses,and said be first warned defendants but they returned.—M. W. H. Wallington Assistant Weights and Measures Inspector said they were not allowed to stand in the street, except when selling to elastomers. They were Continually pestered with complaints at the offile as these hawkers interfered with the stall-holders in the market. -The bench fined defendants 2s. 6(.1 each and costs or seven days in default,. AN EXPENSIVE DEIVE.—Lomax Gill, fish deliverer, 12. Beamont-street, was charged with furious driving in Win- wick-street. on the 22nd 4anuary. and also with being with- out lights. —Sergeant Lewis said he was on duty in Winwick Street. on Sunday night when the defendant passed him driving a bay horse in a light trap. Tlnre were three or four other young men in the trap which had no lights. The horse was in a very fatigaed condition, perspiring very much and dropping on t its knees. —P.C. Baxendale who corrotor- atod said one of the occupants of the trap fell out, as he was drunk.—Defendant said he an! a friend had been to Knots- ford for a drive and picked up one or two friends on the way home.—He was fined 2s 6d and costs in each case. MINOR CASES—Thomas Houghton, 3, Furness-stn.:et. was I ned 5s and costs for not carrying a lighted lamp with his vehicle on January 21.—John Taylor, of the Unicorn Inn, was summoned for having his dog at large.—P.O. Healey said be was atta ,ked by a large mastiff dog without a muzzle In Dallam-lane and it took a piece out of his cape. Witness had had several complaints from forgemen about a mastiff dog. —Defendant was fined 5a and costs.</p>
        <p>A.volntan PAYMENT 01 RAILWAY FARE.—Henry Stephen Jones, 71, Catherine-st., was eh trged with travelling from Thelwall to Warrington on the London and North-Western Railway, on January 22nd, without paving his fare.— Deteltive Howell said defendant got in the train at Thelwall without a. ticket and when the ticket collector asked him for his ticket at Arpley defendant aslied if he had got any change, and then said he would pay when he passed the t7cket office. He did not pay.—Evidence was then called in support of the ease —Defenqaut stated it was a case of for- getfulness —He was tined 5s and costs. TUEsDAY.—Before Mr. Garnett (presiding), CouncilloreShaw Green, and Mr. Birchall. TILE ADJOURNED CASE OF AITEMPTED SuunDE —Harrie; a young girl, who was suinnioned a week ago for attempting to cOinnait suicide by jumping _ Into the Bridgewater Canal. was again brought up.—She promised to go to a home, so the magistrates withdiew the ce.se.—Mr. Taylor agreed to mak ethe arrangements. A BRUTAL HUSBANl4,—Joserh Ellison, was charged with atisaulting his wife on January 21st.—Mary Ellison said her husband kicked her, and then " downed " her on the floor with a sr:zond kick.—The Chief Constable said a police-nan chased d4endant, who ; was eventually brought to the Police</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>IStation and the woman taken to the Infirmary where she was unconscious for 4 time.—The Bench committed defend- ant to ptison for thr-e months with hatd labour. DESERTING HIS CHILDREN.—Joseph Bradshaw was charged with running away from the borough on January 17th and leaving his two children chargeable to the Union.—Mr.Shearer who prosecuted said defendant was brought from Dublin the previous day. —Tha Bench adjourned the case for a week to give the defendant a chance to show he was willing to provide for his children. _ _</p>
        <p>A NEGLEC-riIIL WIVE.--Ilary Kelly was charged with being druak and disorderly in St. Austins-lane, the previous night.—El 0. Kelley gave evidence in support of the case.— Defea lant'a husts.nd who was in Court came forward and ex- plained to the Bench that his wife neglected his home whilst he was at work. —The defendant who seemed to treat the mattar as a huge joke, slid she would sign the pledge and the case was suijourned for a fortnight. CONSCIENTIMIS OBTECtOr.S.—Arthur Booth. and Albert William 14ill, (21) applied for and were granted voccination exemption orders for their children —The latter said his child was sulering fro rt eruptioas of the ,kn.</p>
        <p>WMIR,TNGPON couNry PErtY SIMIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDA.Y.—Before Mr. W. H. Bleckley (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ball</p>
        <p>DRUNK ON LICENSED PRESII4E4 AT WaYoocnr.—Jonathan Gleave. of Haydo.lk. was summoned for being lrunk on the licensed premises of the Huntsuan's Arms, Haydoek, on the 6th May, 1898, and with assaulting P.O. Richard Dagger at the suite time and place.—Dafendant pleaded guilty.—P.O. Dagger said that at 10.15 p.m. on the date in question he was called to the Hutatnan's Arms. where he saw prisoner, with his vest and jack Wort, challaaging people to fight. Defend- ant refused to leave the house, and he was put out. On the way to the Police Station defendant tripped the dicer and kicked him on the ribs. He also struck him on the face and head.--Inspector Higginson said defendant hid a bad char. ae,er.—For the first offence defendant was fiaed 5s and costs, and the latter 20s and costs.</p>
        <p>_ A BURTONWOIO AB3CONDER.—Geor,ze Wilbraham and Michael McDonald, of Burton mood, were charged with being drum( and disorderly on the highway at Burtonwood on the January.—Defendants did not appeear, but Mrs. Wil- braham represented her husband —lnspector Hig.rinbotharn stated that (McDonald had absconded.—The magistrates granted a warrant for his arrest.—"Wilbraham was fined 2s 6d and costs. P. 0 . McGarry proved the case. CIIA.RGE OF PERIBTENT CRUELTY.— Bdward Boylin, of Sankey Bridges, appeared in answer to a °barge of persistent cruelty towards his wife. Try ohena Buckt..m (magistrates' clerk) said the case was adjourned at the last court to se sif d. fendant would make amends and enable Mrs. Bayla to bring witnesses.—Complainant said de- fendant had behaved very well since the last court, and he promised that if she would not press the case against him he would sign the pledge. She would, therefore, withdraw the charge against him.—Mr. Taylor. notice court missionary, promised he would make occasional visits to the home.</p>
        <p>DARESBUNY rE rrY SESSIONS. TUESDAY.—Before feless-s. J. C. Parr (cnairman), J. Knowles, If. Meld. and H. Talbot. • DISORDERLY CONDUCT AT STOCKTON klEATlL—Hustanc Bennett, of Stockton Heath, w s summoned for being dis orderly at Stockton Heath on the 27th December Defendan t pleaded not guilty.—P.S Goslimg said he VIA% at the Police Station at 7.2 p m. on the day in question when he heard a noise in the street. Defendant Was challenging people to fight, and a man said -hare's tile man that will do you," meaning witness. It was defendant's regular practice to go round me tne public-houses &amp;Wien:ring people, and unless he was un lee bond they could do nothing with him. A witness stato.d that he was with defendant in the public-house, when a man struck him first. Defendant did not challenge any- ons.—Befendant said the row cemmenced about wh ) was tile best sharer. Ile did not challenge anyone to fight. —Defend- ant was tound over to keep the peace for six months. No LIGUTS —Thomas Leah. of Warrington. and John Arnold of Wilderspoal. were summoned for falling to carry lighted lamps attached to their carts et Antrobus on the 29th December at 10 p.m.—Defendants were each lined 24 6d.— Frederick Hayes. of Stockton Heath, was similarly sum- moned for an offence at Appleton on the 17th January, at 6,30., and was fined ss. _ _ .</p>
        <p>- DOGS AT LARGE—John Lightfoot. of Walton. and Joseph Whitlow, of Warrington, were summoned for unlawfully allowing their dog, to ba at large unmuzzled.—Defendants were each fined 23 6d.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATK CASES —Gilbert Miller. of Stockton Heath. WAY summoned for oeing drunk on the highway on the 24th Debember. P.C. T ‘rte proved the ease and stated that defendant wanted to fight. Defendant denied being drunk, but said they were " h %ring a family quarrel." Fined se. Alfred Taylor, of Stockton Heath, wa summoned for being drunk and .disor.terly on the highway on the 6th January, and Dominic Kno:kton. of Stockton Heath, was similarly summoned for an o/ence on the 25th January. Ddendants did not appear, and on the application of In- spector Okell, warrants were granted for their arrest.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETIT SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDA.Y.—B;i:re—liies-srs. James Handley (in the chair), S.</p>
        <p>Taylor. and W. Timmiiia.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED NEGLEGL. Of A CHILD AT RUNDoRq.—The adjoarned. case against the defendants aldward and Ann Phithin. Surrey-street, for the allege! nagle !t of their child. was re-heard.—lnspector McAteer explained that the child had been removed to the Children's Hospital. Liverpool. but a letter from the superintendent of that place stated they would be unable to keep the child, who would be 13 years old In a day or two. He therefore asked that the Bench further adjourn the case so as to give the defendants a chance of gettinz the child taken somewhere else.—The Bench decided to adjourn the ease until the 27th of February.,—Defendants gave an undertaking to do .01 they could, and Inspector McAteer promised to assist them. —Tne Chairman promised a recommendation to the Royal Infirmary. Liverpool, which I nspeJtor McAteer accepted on the defend:in&amp; behalf. A SEA4II lee TReClDLE.—itudolph Hammer, a seaman. In answer to aeh arge of being drunk and disord arly, in High-street, on the 29:,b. of January explained that he had come ashore from his sr.earnahip which Wee lying in the Shio Canal, to fetch some provisions and some clothes. When he got back the ship had cast off and proeeeded on its way. P.C. Wilding stated the prisoner was very drunk, in High. street. on Sunday, at abotLl half-past tkree. He tried to persuade him to go aboard his ship, bat he would not. oubsequently he was locked up —ln answer to the Bench the officer stated, upon searching the prisoner 3s 3d., was found in his possession fine of 2s 6d. including costs was I m posed. AFrien Nia.NY DAYS.—James Robinson, Neilon street, was brought up in custody, charged with refusing to answer a summons on the 31st of October, charging him with being drunk and disorderly on the 2ith of Oatober. He pleaded that he did not receive the summons at all, and that he had given himself up on Sunday last.—Police-constable Smith proved the case of drunkenness. and prisoner said it 'night be so; he could not remainber.—The. lien sh, considering that the prisoner had been out of work for socni time, imposed a fine of 2s and 14s costs or" 14 days.</p>
      </div>
