      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR. COUPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR. COUPETITION.</p>
        <p>OLDHAM v. Sr. HELENS,</p>
        <p>The match betwoPn these clubs at o.dhan2 attracted a crowd of some 15,000 people. The ground was in goed conditios,and up to half-time the game was interetting, withiag t.eing scored. Ia the seclnd hal the play underwea a great change, tho Oldham team being seen to marked advantage. t!lo ui after the resumption Davies gained two Wei in quick succs-sim. Frater convetting one. Later on Davies got in again, Frater placing a gpal, and Lawton Ws) added a try. Toe risult was a victory for Oldham by _2 goals 4 tries to _nothiag.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SWINroN i. MORECAMBE</head>
        <p>SWINroN i. MORECAMBE</p>
        <p>The weather at hl&amp;ichettr via frosty, and there was only a small company on the Swinton ground to witness this match. The first half was evenly contested,an lat the interval nothing hal been scorel. After clanging ends Swinton showed improved form, and h%d the best of matters. Goodman dropped a goal a,:id gained a try, while Lewis also crossed the More'mmbe line, G iodman improving. As their opponents failed to scort, Swinton von by 2 goals 2 tries to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ASSICIATION F. C. v. APPLETON RANGERS</p>
        <p>Played a Runcorn before a good attendance in frosty weather. Toe home team lost the toss, and Weston c6mmencine operations play was soon in the visitors quartets, where E. Burrows had a futile shot at goal. Even play followed up to five minutes from half time, when trom a centre by Dumbill, Weston headed past Hough, thus opening the score for the home team. After the interval the homeeters attacked strongly, and from a shot by C. Burrows, Bough saved splendidly, at the expense of a corner. Dumbill taking the cocuer kick one ot the visitors backs had the miefottme to put through his own goal, thus leaving the homesters wip- ers of a grand game by 2 goats to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CUP. THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>BARNTON ALBION v. NEWTON-LR•WILLOWS.—These teams met on the ground of the former, in the third round of the above competition, on Saturday afternoon. About three o'clock the ball was set in motion by Barn- ton, and toe initial stages of the game were of am even character. Thompson, oue of the visitors' forwards,was obliged to retire front the field for a time owing to a sligiat injuryebut he returned, and his side assumed the aggressive, hat were at fault with.their shooting. New- ton wore then guilty of an irregularity, and from the en- suing kick Barnton forced the p ice. F. Hindley re- ceived on the right, and clearing Ws opponent grandly, centred, for George Hindley to open the score with a beautiful shot. Resuming an exciting scrimmage in front of the heme goal was the next item of note, but nice play on the part of the homesters removed hostilities t 3 the other end, where ilickson played the ball nicely across, ani Beck .tt added a second goal. Half-tiene—Barnton Albion, two goals ; Newton, none. ThompFon started for Newton after the interval, and both teams pressed in turn. Newton were penalized f3r shady work, which was taken by Lockkett and cc nvertid into a goal. Toe following play was net productive of any scoring, although there were good at- tempts made by both sides, and the final arrived with the score—Barnton Albions, three goals ; Newton-le- Willows, nil.</p>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN ROVERS V. W ITTON ALBION . —Graat interest was centred in the above match which took plec on the Mesnes ground, Ea.rlestown, on Siturday, on account of the fact that Witton are the present holders ef the cup, and were this year's favourites. Witon kisked off in the presence of about 300 spec'ators and against the hill. Owing to the recent keen frost, the ground was very hard and slippery, and the players tound it a difficult matter to keep up. The im- mediately made tracks for the Rovers' goal and fenced a fruitless corner. The Rovers /0061 retaliated and some pretty passing by the whole of the forwards placed the Albion's goal in danger, the ball once being right under the bar but the visitors' backs eventaally cleared. The visitors egain got going, and Davies effect( d a splendid clearance when be was hard pressed. Considering the dangerous state of the turf the game up to this point bad been very fast and excit:n' g the visitors having slightly the best of the e xchanges. Bhuebotbatia and Ellis showed good com- bination, and they came within an ace of scoring more than nice. Ellison next got possession and raced away with the ball, but he was too anxious and ran the ball over the line. The home left, by the aid of a foul, again placed the Witten goal in jeopardy, and Ellis eventually put the hall into the net ; the goalkeeper making no attempt to clear, but made strenuous appeals to the referee for offside. The point was, however, eventually allcwed by the referee. Half-time :—Earlestown Roveis, 1; Witten Albion, 0. The resumptiou saw the visitors aeain attarking, and Jones brought off a marvellous save, just managing to scrape the ball from under the bar. fhe Albions had all the play from now to the finish, and the homestrrs were so hard pressed that they bad to rescrt to kicking out tactics. Try as they would, however, the visitors could not score, and they never seemed to be able to eauge their shooting at the bottom end. Final :—Earlestown Rover;, 1 ; Witton Albion, 0.</p>
        <p>THE ELECTION IN ISMWSEY WARD,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.,</p>
        <p>RESULT OF ITIE POLL.</p>
        <p>Polling took place in the Bewsey Ward on Monday, to elect a member of the Town Council. It will be rernetn- bered that on the 13t November last Mears. Thomas Broomhall Malkin (Labour), and William R3gers (Inde- pendent) in the election in the same ward polled 319 votes each, bet as the preening offi er (Alderman James Fairelouge) declined to give his casting vote, a fresh election was neoessary. A mad trnus to bold another election was applied for is the High Court, London, and the election was fixed for the 30:h Janu try. Toe same candidates again entered the lists' and con- siderable interest was takea in the election. Although neither party held a wetting of electors, the work of canvassing was done very efficiently, aad it was antici- pated that a close contest would he the result, though en the promises given for Mr.' lidalkia it seemed probable that 114 would be at the head of the poll. Toe number of promisee, however,subrequently proved to be fallacioas. 'Tnere was a plentiful display of mural literature. The Tra les and Labour Council issued an appeal on behalf of Mr. Malkin, in which they urged the working man of the ward to" Vot I for an honest work- lug man and give him a thumping majority on Monday, January 30h." Mr. Rogers was also well to the fore in the matter of appeals, which incluied the following If you want your inter-sts attended to hoeestly and well, vote for William Rogers '• " " Vote solid for the indep na endent caniidate,Williaßogers,:he Bewsey Wøt k- ice Man's friend ;" "Rally un,Bewssv. We'll return our old friend William Rogers again the time with a thump- ing majority." There were two polling booths, one at 98. Hoyie-etreet off Bewsey-road, and the other St. Ann's Schoolroont, Dannett-street. The presiding officer at the Hoyle-street polling booth was Mr. W. B,yle and the poll clerk Mr. A. St.arkey, while at the Dann stt-strect Seooolrootn Mr. Stanley Wilson officiated as presiding °Meer and Mr. T. Woods as poll clerk. Voting was brisk throughout the day. The ballot boxes were taken to the Town H all shortly after eight o'clock and tne counting was at ouce proceeded with ie the large committee-room. in the pre- sence of the Pre.i ling Alderman (I.lr. James Faircicugh) the Town Clerk, the candidates, and several members of the Corporation. The poll was a very heavy one, as of about 750 persons entitle-I to vote, 354 decided in favour of Mr. Rogers, and 336 in favour of Mr. Malkin. At the conclusion of the c iunting Councillor ROORRS said it gave him great pleasure to propose a vote of tnanks to the Preaioing Alderman, for the manner in which he had conducted the election.</p>
        <p>Mr. MALKIN secondel the motion, and said that, of course, he had a feeling of regret that he bad not been successful.</p>
        <p>The motion was heartily approved. Alderman FAIHCLOUGH, in reply, said he was sorry both the ctudidates could not be returned. (Laughter). Lie then proceeded to the top of the steps outside the hall, and declared the result as f ,lows</p>
        <p>W. Rogers 354 T. B. Molkia</p>
        <p>Majority lB The result wag received witu cheers by the large revs d waiting below.</p>
        <p>SPEECEIES AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Severel speeches were delivered in the Billiard Room at the Liberal Club, alter the declaration of the poll. Councillor SHAW GREEN said they could not disperse without saying a word or two. The tight had been a bard one, and it hld taken place under unusual circum- stances. Althougn they had reasou to regret the result yet they could still bold up their beads, as they were neither ashamed nor disgraced. (Hear, hear.) Person- ally he very much regretted tbat a censtitieency which I was known as a working class co sstitueacy had not had the heart—should be say tee manliness P--to return one of the most useful, one of the most generous, and one of the most well disposed working men to be found in Warrington. (Applause.) Those who had watched Mr. Malkin'e career since he undert aok public duty on behalf of the union, and on behalf of working men especially, had realized that in him they had a downright solid faithful friend. Had ha been returned to the Council frequeet opportuni- ties would have been found when his advice and help would have been invaluable. Personally, as a member of the Council. be regretted that they had not one or two more men of his stamp amongst them, with his special training and his special knowledge to help those of them who were more theoretical, and had not the special gifts which Mr. Melkin possessed. Bewsey had had its chance, and although he was proud of the ward he regretted that their good friend had not been returned. (Hear' hear.) It would have bean unfair and ungenerous to have gone away that night without expressing the sincere regard they had for those who had so devotedly and so , thoroughly worked that day. (Applause.) Mr. MALKIN who was received with rounds of ap- plause said he could assure all present that it was with heartfelt regret that he announced that he had net been returned as the representative of the ward in the Coun- cil. The result was not owing to any want of energy on the part of his supporters, who had worked zealously for him througheut the day, nor had any pains been spared by himself. Still it had beeee a hard time to have to co through two elections and to be engaged up me electioneering matters for about four months. Some of his friends urged hint to"keep his heart up,"but the men he hadi sacrificed se much for had somewhat deceived him, and also, perhaps, themselves.—A Voice : "I know a lot of thern."--However, "there would come a time," to use a well-known expression. He had served the people faithfully and honestly as their leader for 25 years, and he bad never asked for anything, his only object being to do his duty towards his fellow working- men. (Applause.) He very much regretted that the promises that had been made had not been fulfil'ed. When he left the polling booth a few minutes ago he felt certain of victory owing to the large number of promises he received and the number of voters who were brought to the poll. They would excuse him saying more that night. (Hear, hear.) Alderman ROBERTS said he would have preferred words of congraulation to the electors and workers. He did not want to hurt anyone's feelings,and if he let him- self go he might do so, but he was puzzled to know way any man professing to be a Liberal should vote for Mr. Rogers.(Hear.hear.)—A Voice:A lot of Liberals havebeen voting for him.—They supposed the Liberals were gifted with a certain amount of common sense,and it was quite palpable to any man that Mr. Rogers had been sailing under false colourteboth at this election and the previous one. (Hear, hear.) He had posed as an independent candidate, and yet on the evening of the Let November last, at the Conservetive Club, the Chairman of the Association expressed his regret that their friend Mr. Rogers had not succeeded in winning the election. He added that if there were any Conservatives who had not voted for him they would regret their conduct, so that the clap-trap about Mr. Rogers being an independent candidate was a most absurd fallacy; and how any in- telligent L;beril working men could be deceived was be- yond his(the speaker's)comprehension.lt struck him that they would not be very long before they had strong evi- dence that his independence was a sham and a fraud, and that his vote would be found on the other side; (Applause.) He did think that Mr. Malkin'e services would have secured for him the - abeolute los alty of his. fellow trade unionists in the ward, and if there was one ward in the town more than another that was a workingman's ward it NV4S the Bewsey Ward. (Meer. hear.) It was also a Liberal ward, and yet Mr. Malkin had been disloYally deserted. (Applause.) It was not policy on his part to throw down the apple of discord, but it was hard lines on Mr. Malkin that he should have been defeated in the circum- starces, and they could not allow the matter to pass without a strong expression of opinion. There was another matter that should be mentioned. Several of. the large works were represented in the Town Council but the biggest, the most heavily rated, and which employed the largest number of hands, were absolutely unrepresented. There had been an opportunity on that occasion of returning a man to the Council engaged in the works to represent Messrs. Pearson and I Knowles. On all the grounds he had named he regretted the result of the election, but they need not despair. They had had their successes and their losses in days gone by, and it would be a great mistake, bitter as it was to be defeated, to be entirely depressed. They must rally on another occasion, sooner or !ater, determined that their flag should be in the ascendent in the BOWAOT Ward. (Applause.) He did not believe for a moment that it was a Conservative ward. They must make up their minds not to lot the ward be captured again. He believed Mr. Rogers could be beaten even yet, and he felt sure that those people who had gone wrong on the present occasion would some day come to their right mind, and they would retain a solid represeetetive vote in the Town Council. (Applause.) He would ask them not to allow this defeat to slacken their efforts. Nearly 93 per cent, of the electors had voted which was an enormous poll and the surprise was that they had not won. Be considered that Mr. Malkin had laid the Liberal Party in Warrington under a great obligation to him inasmuch as he had come forward twice within a short period to fight the party's battle in the ward. (Hear, hear.) Their only regret was that when there was an opportunity given to workingmen to send a workingman's representative they had not taken advan- eage of the opportunity and returned him at the head of the poll. (Applause.) Councillor PEACOCK considered that every Liberal member ot the Council who had not come forward to assist Mr. Malkin ought to be ashamed. If they had all done so the result would have been different. He was rather surprised that trades unionists had not supported Mr. Malkin better. Mr. Malkin had suffered personally on aceount of his connection with workingmen and his advocacy of their grievances, and he was surprised that workingmen did not rally round him better than they had done. (Hear. hear.) He believed the workers at the Winwick-rod end of the ward had done their best, but he could not speak for the other end of the ward. Three hearty cheers were then given tor Mr. Malkin and the meeting tn.ninated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CA.LIFORNIA SYRUP OF FIGS.</head>
        <p>CA.LIFORNIA SYRUP OF FIGS.</p>
        <p>NEVER IMITATED IN QUALITY.</p>
        <p>The highest object of the art of advertising is to cor- rectly inform the public of the merits of any article,and truthful statements always prove most effective in time. The valuable reputation acquired by the Cali- fornia Fig Syrup Co.. by reason of the excellence of the plea,ant laxative remedy California Syrup of Figs,which it manufactures, confirms the statement which the Com- pany freely makes that the best of remedies only should be used when needed by the human system. The more one takes Of salts and pills the more constipated the systtth becomes,while on the other hand one enjoys both the method and the results when California Syrup of Figs is taken ; it is pleasant and refreshing to the taste, and acts gently yet promptly on the kidneys, liver and bowels, cleanses the system effectually, dispels colds, headaches and fevers and overcomes habitual constipation permanently, also biliousness and the many ills resulting thereform. The emit trouble with all other purgatives • and aperients is nor, that they fail to act when a single dose is taken ,but that they act too violently and invariably tend to pro- duce a habit of body requiring constantly augmented doses. Children enjoy the pleasant taste and gentle ac- tion of California Svrup of Figs, and ladies find it de- lightful and beneficial whenever a laxative remedy is needed, and business men pronounce it invaluable,as it may be taken without interfering with business aud does net gripe nor nauseate. The excellence of Calbornia Syrup of Figs is due not only to the originality and gym- plicity of the combination, but also to the care and skill with which it is manufactured by scientific processes known to the California Fig Syrup Company only, and therefore we wish to impress ore alt the importance of purchasing the true and original remedy. When buying note that the name and trade mark of the California Fig Syrup CC'. appears on the package. Of Chemists, is. lid and is. 9J.</p>
