      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>BENJAMIN FRANK FOUNTAIN.</head>
        <p>BENJAMIN FRANK FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>It is the deficit, children—thw delicate from infancy—that are constantly threatened with illness. It needs no explan ttion of symptoms to make it clear to parents whether or not their children are delicate. If children do not thrive in growth they Make the fact kn)wrs in a hundrei ways. They seem to be always ailing, and if no definite malady asserts itself, it is more on account of good luck than anything else. To transform delicate children into healthy boys and girls. giving every evidence of vigorous growth, is as simple as can he, if the advice given in the fol- lowing letter is acted upon : "108, St. Alban's Road, Dartfori, "August 26r.h. 1898.</p>
        <p>"Gentlemen,—! am very pleased to tell you of the good Scott's Emulsion has been to our little boy, who has always been a delicate child from in- fancy. For some time he had been suffering from swollen tonsils, which brought him into a very low state of health. I was tecornmended to try Scott's Emulsion, and just at that time had your pamphlet left at the house, and was determined to try it. Ile has taken two bottles of it and I am pleased to say is now quite well and healthy. I have recommended Scott's Emulsion to my friends, and I am sure it is an excellent preparation for alaybody in a delicate</p>
        <p>state of he iltb.—Yours gratefully, " (Sigßed) MRS. F. FOUNTAIN. The simplest way in Lt:18 world to be coavince.l ot the efficacy of Scott's Enulsion as a r.itneiy for chit- dreit's Maladies is to try it. If you have a child that is in a delicate state of health, or gives any in- dication that its system is unable to meet the de- mauls of growth,you will find by trying the genuine Scott's Emul,i)n, that this preparation does for weak children all that we claim tor it.</p>
        <p>We cannot speak for other forms of cod-liver oil, because wo know that they are not as effectual as Scoct's Ernulsi.;n, but we do know, and the proof overwhalmingly bears us out, that Scott's Emulsion i 3 effec'ull where other temediPs are not.</p>
        <p>The very nature of this preparation is in its favour for it contains cod-liver oil, hypoptio3phites, and glycerine in proportions approvei by the medical profession, and in a form that is perfectly palatatle and of no tax whatever to digestion. The experi— ence of Mrs. Fountain is the universal experience of all who try Scott's Emulsion. - _ - - - -</p>
        <p>You may obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bovvne, Limited, Manufacturing Chemidts, 92,Gnat Saffron liul, London, E.C., and mentioning this paper.</p>
        <p>The Criannierr said he wished to endorse all that had. been said. A batter man they could not havo in the position. . .</p>
        <p>The re3olution was curiel unanimously. THE VICE PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Two names were suggo.sted for the position, of vice.. president, viz : Mr. Andrew Rathb-me and Mr. O. E. Park.?r, of Penketh. Mr. Rathbone withdrew in favour of air. Firker, and on the proposition of Mr. Fakelough seconded by Mr. Fleming. Mr. k'arker was unanimously elected.</p>
        <p>01,3111 t ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to ad the Vulcan Foundry Company and Messrs. Robert Gonett and Son to the Council of the Chamber. Mr. Joh?' Murray was appointed hon. treasurer; and Myers. Zits Armitage, J.P., and Thomas Tunstall, F.C.A., hon. auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce was held at the offices of the Cham- ber, Market Gate Chambers, Warrington, on Mon- day. Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present, Mr.A.Rathbone, Councillor William Pierpoint, J.P., C.O , Mr. W. J. Forshaw, Mr. C. E. Parker,Mr.Collingwood (Vulcan FOundry), Mr. John Fairclougb, Mr. J.ll.Fleming, with Mr. A. Bennett (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The 22nd annual repsrt was submitted. Alluding to the question of British trade with China, the report says :—The action of the Warrington Chamber in this matter waa specifically alluded ta by the Marquis ot Salisbury in the House of L3rds. Tao Council are strongly of opinion that every reasonable opportunity should be taken to promote the commercial interests of the Empire in the East, and, at the annual meeting of the Associated Chambers,urged the desirability of secur- ing a definite sphere of infleence in case the policy of "the open door" should fail, a course which has been since adopted. Allusion is made to the differential duties in West Africa, to the metric system, to the nationalisation of telephones, to the sugar bounties, to the annual meeting of the Associated Chambers, to the action of the County* Council with reference t, War- rington Bridge, and to the Industrial Exhibition. The lay-by at Stag Bridge,the report continued,has been com- pleted during the year, and the Council communicated I with the Ship Canal Company upon the subject,and indi- cated the desirability of endeavouring to develop a local traffic. The company replied that they had appointed Mr. John Miller to visit the district, ascertain too pos- sibilities of the trade, and make the necessary arrange- ments but we regret to state that nothing definite ap- pears to have been done in the matter. and believe that the lay-by might be utilizee, in many instances, with great advantage to local tract ra and with substantial profit to the company. In conclusioa, we may say that one firm has withdrawn, and that three new firms have joined the Chamber durine the year, making a present 7otal of forty members ; that the rules have been altered so as to increase the representative character of the I Council ; and that we have recently had emphatic testi- mony to the practical utility of the Chamber, extensive foreign orders having been secured as the ! result of information which it has been able to supply. An attempt was made, in the autumn, on the suggestion of Mr. E. L. Fleming, to reorganize the Chamber and. provide a reading room, supplied not only with the principal commercial papers, pamphlets, blue books, rine returns, as at present, but witu the leading daily and weekly papers, and with a regular tekgraphic news service. It was pointed out that this could °ale be accomplished on the basis of a much augmented list of members, aryl the Council are quite prepared to entee,aiii the proposal if they receive the needful measure 01 support. In other towns of less industrial importance than Warrington the Chambers, in the generality of inste.oces, are numerically stronger ; and, although the leading manufacturers of this locality, with few exceptions, are subecribers, they do not take that active interest in the affairs of the Chamber which ! would so much coniuce to its utility, whilst the Council feel that there are other claEsts interested in commercial matters whose support might well be given to it, with immense advantage to theemselves as well ae to the cause it represents. The statement of acceuets showed a balance in hand at the end of the year amounting to ;MO 7s. 83. Councillor PIERPOINT moved the adoption of the re- port and balance sheet.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joirm FAIRCLOUGH seconded. The CHAIRMAN ca,lled attentima to the fact that the balance in hand was £3 less than last year. The SECRETARY said the new members wuuld make up the de fie eacyl The report and balance sheet' were adopted. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The Chamber tlaen proceeded with the election of - -</p>
        <p>Councillor PIRRPOINT : nave you any objection Mr. Garnett to serve as prf siciont for another year. Toe CHaxamaN : I tiavt, a very serious ohjectiln.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT stil ho wou'd move that the vice-president —Alderman Henry Rooerts be appointed the pre-ident for the ensuing twelve months. -Tbfy would all agree that if th 3 public spirit and the ability of Alderman Rob?rts was given to the work of the Chamber as in public affairs, they would be very fortu- nate in having him as their preliclent. (Hear. hear.) He had pleasure in moving that he be appointed for the ensuing year. Mr. Jour/ FAIRCLOUGH seconded, and said they were all agreed as to Alderman Roberts' ability,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>VOTES OF 'MANES.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF 'MANES.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINP proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Robert Garnett, for his services as presidenr, and to the other officials of the Chamber for their work during the past year. Mr. JoRN FAIRCLbtIGH snonded and the resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PROPOSED COMMERCIAL. MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED COMMERCIAL. MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY, in reverting to tnis subject, said it had occurred to him that as the erection of a Technical Institution was a project of the near luture in Warring- ton, the Chamber might endeavour to get a c_mmercial muieum as an acjunct to it,and unest to the Corpora— tion the deiirability of making provision for a collection of local manufactures. Mr. PARKER : His the erection of a Technical Institu• tioa tagen any definite form ? The SECRETARY : The Corporation are asking for the necessary pnwers. It was most likely that the question would b) taken in hind during the presout yEar as the new police buildings would disturb the present Techai- cal Institute.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPIINT hoped they would be able to. establish a commercial museuin in connection with the new Technical Institu:e. Such mits3unis were of great value, and he could assure them that his visits to the Bethnal Green Mll3lOlll hid added to his store of know... ledge as to the use of animal products. Toe knowledge he was abhs to gain there had been ,f servi,us tl him. If they had cases of the in in Warrington having refer— ence to local manufactures.,.they would be of use to rot connected with the tradAs concerned, and might be the means of bringing people to the Own to look at them. Mr. RATEBONR suppose I that the contributions to the museum would be voluntarily given by local manufac- turers.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN Slid that would ho so. He knew that Messrs Pearson and Knowles had such a case, as had been described referring to their manufactures and would no doubt b 3 pleased to add it to a commerciai museum.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said he had seen commercial museums cm the continent. lie did not think there were- many in England. He would !time a resolution captaining the wishes of the Chamber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MEM3ERSELIP OP THE CHAMBEP..</head>
        <p>THE MEM3ERSELIP OP THE CHAMBEP..</p>
        <p>The SEcc-ir-an—r-reterred to the suegestioa of Mr. E. L. Flemimy, thal the Chamber should appoint a canvasser to endravour to gtta the membership up t) NO, and then th:-.3r could limn a reading room and other facilities for =lmelda' men.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN saii Mr. Fleming ought to he present :o urge that question. . _</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said Warrington wait very much be. bind in this mitter. He btslieved their membership wet one of the smallest in the kingdom. Mr. PARKER said if they allowed a larze number of small traders to join they might lose more than they would gain by the larger manufacturers leaving them. Councillor PIRRPOINT said if small! traders were inter. ested in the welfare of the two he thought they were entitled to be mernbsrs. They must swallow any little differences they might have. The CHUICKAN said nothing maid be done at present. They must keep the matter before them. This eQeclucle I the bu-lop.q.</p>
      </div>
