      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RECEPTION AT PAR a HALL.</head>
        <p>RECEPTION AT PAR a HALL.</p>
        <p>Cities which are term4l cosmopolitan have gener- ally some reason to show tor the title; they are either cities of age, and -if quaint aspect, or they are places where the pulse of life throbs energeti- cally; both types of cities attract people of all na- tions. Yet Parr Rail is cosmopolitan—if we may be allowed the use of the word in connection with it —as its uses are of " infinite variety " though it has neither of the attributes mentioned. There is a certain amount of sentiment attached to a building when Vie mellowing hand of time has touched it, but Parr Hail, without the charms or age, may be looked upon with some degree of sentiment considering the gatherings of all descriptions which take place under its roof. At times of public meetings, it looks sober and thoroughly in keeping with the event; whsn con- certs, art or industrial exhibitions are held within its walls its aspect seems entirely changed ; in fact its adaptability to the occasion is one of its chief charms. A new mantle descended on its shonlders on Thursday last week, when it was the scene of a reception and conversezione held by Mr. R. A. Naylor, F.R.s.E., and Miss Naylor. Pretty bunting relieved by flags, had been wreathed in a tasteful manner round the balcony of the gallery, and the walls of the ball had been embellished by oil-paint- ings,which were themselves a source of considerable interest to the visitors. The side rooms and side pas- sages had been given over for refreshment, which took up no inconsiderable part of the programme. About 600 Band of Elope workers and friends re- sponded to the invitations extended to them by Mr. and Miss Naylor and were personally welcomed by the host and hoste,,s as they entered the Hall. The pictures and curios exhibited in the building were a source of interesting study, from which an excel- lent musical programme proved an agree- able - change. The affair was purely social ano the time passed pleasantly indeed with the intervals for conversation and refreshment. The Band of Hope Choir were responsible for one or two choruses rsn- dered with excellent. effect. Miss H. Brough contri- buted a solo very creditably, and Mr. Levi Rickaid- son was also heard to advantage. Mr. Grounds' gramaphone was an interesting item of the evening, and also contributed to the pleasure of the young people who were allowed to indulge in games in one of the side rooms. Mr. Frank Ford was successful with a, cornet solo, and Miss Bessie Jones quite charmed the listeners by her skill on the harp. Mrs. Horton made an excellent accompanist during the evening. The guests in- cluded: Mrs John Naylor and party, Rev F Powell, Rev E T Reed, Rev W Potts, Rev J Schofield, Rev J Davies, Rev G Harbottle, Rev P J fltyrne, Mr W Wilson, Mr William Roberts, Mr Weaver, Mr F Per:- dlebury. and Mr H Taylor. During an interval in the programme. Mr W C Wilson (secretary of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union) ad- dressed the party, and at the close of the evening vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Naylor for his erosity.</p>
      </div>
