      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORATION BILL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION BILL.</p>
        <p>• Sir,—Permit me to occupy a littte ot your space on this important question. The Corporation have had three atternativc schemes Wore them, namely :- 1. To taks, water from the Livarpo:.l Corporation Vyrnwy Aqueduct. 2. To sink wells and erect pumping stations at Win- wick at a vary large cost. 3. To take water from the Bold Hall Estates.</p>
        <p>Mr. England, in his letter appearing in your la3t is- sue, correctly pointml out that tee Vyrnwy scheme, be- sides being very expensive, was subiset t) other insur- mountable difficulties. He then go!s on to say that with regard to the third s-..theml, that of tiking water from the Bald Hall Estates,it could only be of a tempor- ary nature, and, therefore, would nece3sitate in the near future, a further scheme to be entered upon— Sherefore, this was not seriously debatsd.</p>
        <p>—it:is is 'exactly the mistake jI desire to peiat out to your readers. The scheme has not received serious consideration because someboiy suggested such a supply would only be temporaiy, but without having any grounds whatever for such a suggestion. The water at Bold is derived from two large 20 feet diameter shafts at a point about 120 yards trona toe surface, and in a be of sandstone 150 yards thick, this forming the great water-bearing stratum ef the district. The works are of a permanent character, and c tpable of pumping a much larger quaatity of water than has been eacounteted up ta the present time. The present supply of water is upwards of 130,003 gallous per hour, or 3,120.000 gallons per day. The supply is derived from exactly the same source as the Corperaelou prop ose to tip at Wiawick and, there- fore, the supply is no mere likely to be temporary at one place than at the other. • It is alto a matter of moment that is not sufficiently known, that in sinking through the sandstone one shaft may be quite dry,and another,only a few yards distaac, may tap the water supply. It, is, theretore, perfectly possible that a new sinking at Wiowick might either entirely fail to tap the undergrouad flow of water, or •only tap it in insufficient quantity. Mr. Deas, the water engineer, eve they have from :the present boring at Witiwick drained a taints of 2,} miles, yet they propose to put down a second bare within half a mite. Any expert in sinkiug knows that such a second bore-hole witnin S3C a a distance would probably only rob the first one. It is, therefore, quite incorreet to say thee the Bell Supply may be only temporary; it is just as permanent •as any supply that can b e derived frera Winwick ; and at Bold tne Water has beea tapped and is now teeing pumped,whilst at Winwick the borings may be a failure after spending enormous stuns of the ratepayers' money. -Upon the question of cost there can be n) comparison. Mr. Roberts in his speech stated tne cost of iayine •main from Bold at £43,001 How these figures are arrived at he does not state, but the cost of laying a 3.2. inch main equal to conveying 1,500,000 gallons per day is £2,000 per mile, at which price I know of a con• tract having been let. The distance is six miles from Bold to Warrington, so that the main should not cost more than £12,000 ; a reservoir to hold 2,000,000 gallons would cost £6OO ; and crossings of railwaye and canals, say £5OO ; total, £13,100. The Bold Hall Estates have offered to sell the water to Warringtou at 21. per thousand gallons, a price which only just covers the coat of pumping and is half what the Cor- poration estimate the cost of the Winwick supply. The Corporation would taus save nearly all their capital ex- penditure on sinking and erecting pumping stations, and only have to lay down a main at a cost of £13,100. A further advantage is that Bold is 53ft. higher tnau Winwick, so that the town would have ample pressure to deliver water at the top of the highest buildings which they could not do from Wiuwick without buile- lug expensive water towers and forcing the water to the top of them. Tne only remaining paint is al to the quality of the water, and heee again the water deeivad from the red sandstone is the same whether it is pumped at Bold or Winwick. This is shown by the analysis that has been made. It is a somewhat hard water, but beautifully pure, as it must necessarily be, having passed through 100 yards or more of red sandstone, one of the best Do33lble filter- ing mediums. The water from the Celtic's Green Colliery (of which lam also chairman) is identical in quality; has been supplied for 18 years to St. Helens, and is used both for manufacturing and dem,stic purposes. Widnes is also supplied from Netherley, wit lin two miles from Bold and from the same red sindstone water.</p>
        <p>It is cfear that the advantages of the Bold scheme have not b3en properly considared, nor have thy been placed befere the rate piyers in any aaequata manner. I hope they will not h sitata to vote agaius; a Bill which pro- poses to speed £280,000 of their money, most of it gut 3 unnecessarily.—Yours, 8t3.,</p>
        <p>HENRY FAIROLOUGH. Chairman of the Bold Hall Eitates, lannitet</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Heary Naitolough toe Chairman of the Bold Ball Es:ates Limi:ed, has tad tue c)urtesy to send-me a copy ot the letter which he has addressed to you on the above subject. The letter contains s'Atements wlaica although not purposely misleading are calculated to pre- judice the puolic mind by ex iggerated comparisons of the scheme advocated by Mr. Fairelough with the scheme propo3ed by the C)rporation. Mr. Ktirchu.lh . _</p>
        <p>the outset of his le7tor stats that the Bold Hall Scheme has" not received ssrious consideratioa b-..tausa soap- body suggested inch supply would only be tmworary." Mr. Fdirclough must have arrived at his conclusions toy inference as that and other s:atemonts contained in his letter are contary to the fac:s waich he c )uld have as- certained for himself had he thou.ght fit to have made inquiry at the proper quarter. The Bold Hall Scheme has not only hid the fullest consideration by the Water Committee (who have had a written re- port on the subject from their water Engineer afzer per- sonally eiamintng the works) but they placed the matter before Mr. James Mansergh the enginler, and Professor Boyd Dawkins the geologist. These two gentlemen not only condemned the scheme but agreed that Parliament would not sanction the expenditure of</p>
        <p>- capital moneys in acquiring a supply of water of such an unsatisfactory character and from a source which -could only be regarded as temporary. The Corporation were not prepared to act contrary to such advice and 'therefore abandoned the scheme.. So muila for Mr. Fairclougla'a statement that the scheme had not received serious consideration. A prominent difficulty lay in the fact that the 801 l Hall Colliery is beyoad thp limits of supply of the Cerporatien and that the Corporation have no power to construct works outsiae their limits. I have stated that tie en ;loser awl geologist reported that the water was unsatisfactory and the supply of a temporary character. Let me make this Trite clear. The water trom Winwick Waterworks whicu is admitted to b 3 one of the best domestic sup- plies, is nevertheless a bard water containing 11 degrees of hardness, but the water from this Bold Hall C )Iliery is nearly twice as hard containing net leis than 21 de- grees of hardum. I quote the latter figure from aü analysis obtained by the company itself, and supplied to the Corporeion. The w iter therefore obt %hied from toe Bold Hall Colliery is without 'treatment unfit for b)th domestic and maaufac:uring purposes. Now as to the temporary character of the supply. I do not wish to infer for one moment that the water at Bold Colliery is not likelf to be as permanent as at Win- wick. Teat is net the point. The Bold Hall Colliery is a business speculation and a supply fro:n that source would be in the control of the owoers of the Colliery. What guarantee would the Corporati ,n have that the owners for finaucial or other reasons mizht not close the works? The Corporetion would be entirely at the mercy of the Company in the event of a breakdown in the machinery or a strike among their men. I doubt -whether the ratepayers of Warrington wouldsupport the -Corporation in the expenditure of large sums for a sup- ply of water depending upon se many uncertainties. Mr. Fairclough states " that the supply is derived from exactly the same source as the Corporation propose to tap at Wing/ink" but the excess of leirdness of the water .whic'e I have quoted shows that Mr. Fairclough is not correct in his statement. There are two faults in the red sandstone between the two points. Mr. Fair. dough further staees that the Corporation propose to put down a bore within a mile of the existing one and that the second bore within such a dis- tance would probably only rob the first one. There again Mr. Faircloup,h is labouring under a mietake. Professor Boyd Dawkins after carefully considering the matter advised the Corporation to sink a bore bole at a point a mile and a half from the existing one and that position has le,en selected. Profe,s.or Boyd Dawkins 'states that the second bore hole will not affect the first one.and that we shall find an abundant supply of water. Now let us examine the figures given by Mr. Fair- clough as to the cost of obtaining a supply of water from the Bold Colliery. Mr. Fairclnieh of C-urse has had no experience in the construetion of waterworks •and therefore one need not be surprise 1 at his proposal to supply 70,000 people by means of a 12 inc e main. The main from Wine ick is 18 inch and in laying new mains we should tske care to make them big enoueh to -supply the inhabitants 50 years hence when the popu- lation will probably have more than doubled. We are advised that a 27 inch main would be required and the -cost of laying such a main alone would be £31,926. Then Mr. Fairclough puts down £6OO for the construction of</p>
        <p>LUXURIANT LUSTROUS HAIR, , With a clean, wholesome scalp, free from irritating and scaly erup- tions, is produced by warm shampoos with CIITICtraA Soap, followei by light dressings wt h CIITICITRA, purost of emollient skin cures, the most eff,.ctive skis purifiers and b3autifiers in the world. They clear the scalp mui. hair of crusts, sales, and dandruff, d stroy microscopie insects which feed on the hair, soothe irritated and itchng surfaces, stimulate the hair follicles, ani supply the outs with energy and nourishment*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>a reservoir to hold 2,000,000 gallons. To begin with Mr. Fairclough is only making provision for one day's supply which is rather a short sighted suggestion. What would happen in the case of etoppiges and break downs ? Mr. y.airclough does not descrite the reservoir, cut by the pro- posal it would almost seem that what he has in his mind is that of a huge brick pit. The estimate prepared for the construction of a reservoir at Winwick to hold 3,000,000 gailens is £12,500. The existing reservoir at Winwick which holds 2,000,090 gallons cost nearly £6,000. Mr. Fairclough providos nothing for contingencies and suggests the modest sum of £5OO tor" crossings of railways and canals." The Corporation recently laid two mains under the canal at Sankey Bridges and the cost was £6OO, and the work Mr. Fairclough proposes couli not be done for les i than £1,500. Perhaps the most important point in Mr Faircleugh'e letter, in his statement that 'the water from Collias Green Colliery has been supplied for la years to St. Helens, and is now used for both manufacturing and domestic purposes. Mr Faircloult as Cnairman of the company ought to know, and no doubt he will be sur- prised to know, that scarcely any water has been taken by Sz. Helens from Collins Green Colliery during the last two years, wed that the Corperetioe of S.; Helene have paid to the company the minimum rent of £l,BOO per annum, and have run the bulk of the water down a brook as being unfit to supply to atneir inhab tants on account of its extreme hardness 10hich is exactly sim.lar as Mr. Fairchueh admits, t) th% water obtained at the Bold Colliery. Tne contract with the Se Helens Cor- poration has 16 years to run and they will have to pay £l,BOO each year for water which they cannot use. Surely Mr. Fairclough as a ratepayer would not Ilk) to S9B Warrington placed in a similar position. Wnen the St. Helens Corperation took the water they were ()hazed to construct softeeing works at a ceet of £lO,OOO and it cost them it. per 1,000 gallons to soften the water so as to make it fit for use. Taking 2,000,000 gallons per day this would work nut at 2.1,520 per annum. Mr. Felrclough states that 2d. per 1,000 gallons "only just cevera the cost of pumping." Toe cost of pumping at Win wick is less than Al. per 1,000 gallons as Ins been proved to Mr. Fairclough, and if tee Corporation were to accept the supply from Bold the Company would make a haniseme prefit out of the ratepayers. JOUN EDW/IRD WRIGHT, Mayor.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! have not had an opportunitylof perusing the Bill which the Town Council are desirous of passing through Parliament, but I am informed by competent autsority that it contains some items of an objectionable nature. With two of these I will now deal briefly. In the first place I understand that powers are sought to levy a rate in ail of a scheme for provid- ing pensions for the offi2igle of the Corporation on their retiring from the public service. En all proba- bility the only men W3O would benefit from the fund are those who are well paid for their work, and one cinnot but admire the boldness and un- usual benevolence of the proposition to make money payments to such man for no work at all! If the Bill intended to benefit the carters, street sweepers and other humble and poorly paid servants ot the town criticism would be disarmed, and few people would grudge an extra penny or so on their rates for such a purpose. But the idea of providing re- tiring allowances for man in good positions and well able to take care of theuriselves is simply prs- posterous. And it adds to its ugliness when one th,inks that the scheme may have been devised by members of the C3uncil who are not very liberal with their own servants, and to whom the thought of pensioning them would be an abomination. 1 cannot, and never could, understand way people in public offices should be pensioned while men who render equally valuable services to private firms should be left out in the cold. - _</p>
        <p>And th n, in the second place, lam informed that the Bill proposes to COMMIIte the burial fees now paid to the Alglican and /Oman priests into a capital sum to be handed over to these men. Pay- ment of barial fees in any form whatever I regard as a gross scandal to Christianity, anl an impost for which th-re is no need whatever. "Freely ye have received, fee -31 y give "said J 89113 to his humble followers. But our modern apostles do no seem to have grasped the meaning of this divine rule. Not content with tithes, pew rents, collections and other devices for raising th dr stipeu is they Must needs follow the dead to the graveside, and exact fees for saying a few prayers over the lifelels clay. As the plpulation of We.-rington has risen consi- derably during the past few years, these fees (night be roughly put down at £lOO per annum. Tnis sum at 30 years purchase would involve a charge of £3,003 on the mates ! Will the ratepayers sane-don such an imposition? Anglicans and Romanis's may do so in the interests of their priests, but surely the Now.lonfornaists of the town will not betray their traditions of civil and religious free- dom by sanctioning an expenditure so unjust and anti-chrietian !—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>THE CiNDITEON OF STR,EET3 AND PAS3AGE3</p>
        <p>IN ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I have the privilege of inviting the TYCV(I Councillors of Orford %Yard. namely Messrs. Joseph Wilkinson, Urace and Peacock, through your valuable paper, to pay a visit to the unpaved streets and passages, as they are in a 010.3 r, deplorable condition They will find passages from 9 to 15. inches deep in slivch and water. If they as representatives of the 'Ward can do anything in the Town Council, or with the property owners,to improve the condi- tion of the streets an i passages, they will be doing their duty to their supporters and folfilling the pledges that they madQ by doing it. When cinders can be cartel at such a nominal price to improve backs and passages, it' is nothing more or less than a disgrace ta the property owners to allow their property to be in such a fill thy and insanitary condition. It is a great won- der that there has not been more sickness, and death caused by the insanitary condition of backs and passages, and if our Town Councillors can se.) their way clear to pass a bye-law to see that the above nuisance is stopped and also to see that the lanilords are compelled by the above-mentioned bye-law to clean their houses thoroughly every five or six years, i would cause hiss removing ot tenants and make the people more h e al th y.—Yours etc., . HUGHES</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>THEI HUMANITARIAN LTA.GUE</head>
        <p>THEI HUMANITARIAN LTA.GUE</p>
        <p>Sir,—The abov•l society has for its objet the diminu;ion of suffering ani,3nge, the dumb creation and one of their nob:e endeavours cnneerns the acinatic carnivore of the Arctic and Antartic zones —in other words, seals and the obtaining of their fur.</p>
        <p>It is perhaps not generally known—l hope nit,for the sake of the reputation of the gentler sex for kin-lness and thought to dumb creation—that in order to procure the fur with its natural gloss SO much prized by ladie4, it must ba removed from the animal while in life! Skinning alive, in round Saxon. Can any true-hearted woman when she knows that enjoy wearing liqr sealskin coat or jacket or whatever she calls it? If anyone will read "The COV; of a Sealskin Cloak" published by the al:nye Society (L3ndon), it will b found not necessary to go to Turkey or China in ssarch of horrors; they will be found to be in close connection with this highly civiliz:d land and that across the Atlantic.—Yours. etc..</p>
        <p>ALEXANDER WADDELL.</p>
        <p>Sale, Cheshire,</p>
        <p>THE TEMPERANCE GAUSS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l had the great delignt of listentng is the eloquent and soul-stirring address delivered by Archdeacon Madden on Tuesday night last,Such an appeal and such a showing-up of the personal re- sponsibility of individual teetotallers for their duty to the State and to the locality in which they live, more especially in regard t 3 toe election of mein- bers of Parliament, Councillors, Guardians, and other public men, I have ant often had the privi- lege ot hearing. As I looked round the hall and saw the ea-nest faces of the hundreds of strong, vigorous, healthy men and women present, I could not help asking myself the question— bow was it that the influence of the Temperance party on public opinion in this town is at so low an ebb ? Perhaps it may be argued that I am mistaken in my views, brit when a working class constituency like Bewsey Ward re- warns a publican in preference toa man who for 25 years has stood a'; the head of his fellow workmen as their advocate and champion, I confess that if temperance sentiment is really strong in the town, at any rats during the recent contest it must have been very sleepy. Again look at the fact that the Council of this town as now constituted, includes in its 3a members no less than 6 men who are direc'ly and actively interested in the liquor trade, besides a number of others whose sympathies are in the same direction, and can we be surprised that con- victions for breaches of the licensing laws are so few and far between a'though drunkenness visible in our streets is of daily occurrence. Again, is it consistent with true•Tem- Rerance sentiment that men who sign the nomina- tion plpers of ex-publicans should hold prominent and responsible positions in the cause? How is it possible for a true and healthy and consistent ac- tin to be taken by the Temperance pirty as a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EXAMINER: FRII)AY, FEBRUARY 3, 18,99 EnryrißTowN &amp; NEwroNH WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER: FRII)AY, FEBRUARY 3, 18,99 EnryrißTowN &amp; NEwroNH WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>whole when such inconsistencies obtain. At the meeting on Tuesday night a few of the speaker,, with someivvhat questionable taste proclaimed their utter indifference to pelitics as party poatics I and their opinions that one party Was practic- ally as goad (from a Temperance standpoint) as the other. Such good peenle must have been densely ignorant of the peAtien of things in Warringt-n or they could not have so spoken. Is it not notorious that the alliance beween the liquor trade and the Tory party in Warrington is as close and complete as it can possibly be? Don't let me be misunderetool I am fully aware that at the last Municipal Election there were a number lof Conservatives who abstained from voting, and a few who supported the Liberal nominees as a pro- test against the action of the Tory party in forcing I publican candidates upon the Wards. But at the present time out of 21 Tory Councillors an Alder- men no less than 6 are in the trade, and on the let of November last, out of 5 Conservative candidates no less than 4 of them were then or lied been recently liquor traffickers. Tne Tory Party in Warrington is permeated with the influence of the drink trade, and is b3und hand and fast to the !iquor interest. The Member for the Boraugh will do nothing to help on our cause. What then is the obvious duty of all men and women in War- rington who profess temperance principles. Is it net perfectly clear that if their professions are to be of any value in affecting public opinion and in ridding the municipal government of this town of an ele- ment which is daily and hourly prejudicing its bee anl most important interests, there must be in- dividual and c3llective action and that of a thoroughly aggressive character. In other words, the temperance men and women in our town must organise and each and all must take a part in °ppm- ing all liquor candidates for any and every pub!ic penition. This is the real test of loyalty to prin- ciple. It's all very well to go to meetings and to listen to able and eloquent speeches anl event) pray for suceess,but all this an mare is wires than useless, unless there is personal, cmtinuous and active work done in the direction I have indicated. At the present time the liquor trade is so wall estab- lished in our midst that its supporters can ani do laugh at our meetings and outpourings of excited verbiage, but let the men and women who were present on Tuesday, hundreds of whom up to now have contented themselves by looking on and wishing us well when we have been fighting liquor candidates, let these good pe3ple I say come down from their pedestals, take off their gleves, aye arid their co ats if necessary, and fight the battle of right against wrong, of poverty against unscrupulous wealth, of man and Ged against the devil, and be- fore long the Trade and all its evil associations will receive such a check as the most enthusiastic Tem- perance men could hardly believe possible.—Yonrs, etc. VIGOR.</p>
        <p>MISMON SEIINIC ES AT EARLESTO W.</p>
        <p>Ii coanection with the Earlecown Wesleyan Chapel, a nine di pciai misiion wai brou4lit to a tercaiatition oa San lay by the RiV. J. H. Doldrall of Prescot. Tae services have been very w.ll at- tended during the period in which they have been held, and on Sanday the rev. gentleman delivered practical discourses b3ariog chiefly on Sunday ob-. stervans with particular rsference to Sunday trading. It is cmisidered th it a la.ge amouat of go )d work ha i been done by the mission, and on Sunday several were b,o igilt to the parrEent form.</p>
        <p>A mentinz of this Board was hell in the Board-room at tad Workh noio op Wednesday morning. M. ti. in presided, aud there were also present !—Rev. W. a. Drum -nand, Rev. J. 11. Riddette, Mrs. Cloy, Messrs. A. J. J ,hey. A. Jolle7, ‘lVilliam Elickrn tn. T. B. A3h3roft,S. DAViAS, et. Br ookPs, El. Thornton, Willi trn Floughton, R. Forshaw, J. W. Knowles, with Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk), Mr. S. Suttpa (as,istant c'erk), Mr. Ward (workhouse master), and Mr. Wr:ght (sup r atendent of Pact;ite sc loots).</p>
