      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHIIRC-H, EARLEsiaWN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHIIRC-H, EARLEsiaWN.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea and social in Call- nection with the ab)ve church, was held in the Aission flail. Earlestown, on Saturday night. Tea was partaken of at 5 o'clock at which about 200 Sat down. After tea the room was cleared and a short entertainment was given at which the Vicar presided. The Chairman °paned the proceedings with an address in the course ot which he gave a general review of the past year's work and made special mention of the Sunday school which is held at the Mi3iioll Flail saying that they were urgently in need ot more teachers, Songs, glees, and recita- tions were rendered by Various members of the choir. Messrs. Wild and Clarke gave humorous re- citations and Mr. liarrison nffiliated at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>CHILD BURNT AT EARLESTOWN</head>
        <p>A CHILD BURNT AT EARLESTOWN</p>
        <p>On Nionday evening, a child named Harry Mason about, four years of age, son of Albert SlasJit, Sepn - ton-street, Newton Common, was badly burnt as the rault 0' playing with fire. It appears the mother of the child went out about Ave o'clock to visit a sick neighbpur, leaving the child in the house by itself. As soon as she left the house, the youngster fastened the door, and then proceeded. as he afterwards said, to help his mother by light- ing the lamp. In attempting to do so, his pinafore caught fire, and he commenced screaming. His cries alarmed one or two of the neighbours, who ran to thi hluse, but .owing t the door being fastewd they could not open it, and they could sea, through the window the poor little fellow in flames. The mothe,, who had arrived on the scene was frantic, and other assistance being obtains I the rio)r was t,urs.-., open, awl the flames ware then un- tue extinguishei. Tie lad was very seriously injure I about the head and face, the whols of the hair being burnt off his he it Dr Hand vsid3 is attending him but he is in a serious condition.</p>
        <p>CLLRENCE LnDGE (FaRLESTOWN) DISTRICT ORDER OF DRUIDS.</p>
        <p>A. public entertaiummt ivas given on Monday evening under the auspices of the above in the lodge-town at the Ram's Head Hotel, Ilaydock,pre- sided over by D. G. M. M. Conway, who was sup- prr,4'l by the Organising A4tent, Dr Houghton, D.C.S.G. Parder, Bro. Rigby of Fernhe id Lodge,and. Bro. Roberts. Songs were gis en by Bros. Rigby and Redfern and were much appreciate L Bro. Roberts occupied the greater part of the evening with his phonograph. So goxl were the productions that in sonie of the well-known humorous songs the auiiencejoind heirtily in the cams,. During the interval tne Organising Agent addressed the meet- ing and pointed out the necessity of young men joining a friendly society and exhorted all who had n't already done so, to give in their names and be- come members of the Clarence Lodge:). Eleven new candidates were secured. A vo-e of thanks was given to Bro. Roberts for his kiddness in provi ling so interesting an entertainment. In acknowledg- ing the vote, he expresso I the p'easura it gave, him to pay them a visit and to entertiin them. This was a means o? Ancouraging the members and keep- ing them together, thereby keeping up that sPirit and interest am3ngst them which are so necessary to a healthy and successful lodge. A similar vote was ace ,rded to the Organising Agent for his addrem A. successful meeting was brought to a close by a vote of thank 4 to the artistes and the chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EARLTISTOWN SOCIAL CYOLING CLUB</head>
        <p>EARLTISTOWN SOCIAL CYOLING CLUB</p>
        <p>The third annual dinner and smoking concert. in connection with the above club was held on Tues- day evening at the Wellington Hotel. The dinner was excellently served by Mr. J. W. Thorpe, and about 59 persons partook of it. The after proceed- inzs were presided over by Mr. C. Charlton, Mr. P. McNiahon occuoing the position of vice-chairman. le Chairman, in opening, congratulated them upon the healthy appearance of the Cycle Club. He alluded to the many enjoyable runs waich hat been held during the summer without accident, an t remarked that a Cycle Parade and Fancy Dress Car- nival and T)rchlight procession which took place in the summ sr was an unqualified success. The late Lord Newtn was one of the principal donors, and she tradespeople of Earlestown and Newton besides giving cash, gave over 63 prizes. The proceeds from the parade were devoted to an Old Folks Treat, at which 294 old people of the district were present without distinction of class or religion, so li)ng as they were over 60 years of age. Ile con- eluded by giving the toast of ' The Queen," which was duly honoured. The other toast was " The Earlestown Social Cycling Club," proposed by S sr- goant-Major Strange, who sail that whatever he could do with regard to lending men and horses for the purpose of assisting at the Cycle Parade, he would de will great pleasure. (Rear, hear.) The programme consisted of songs, ciarioaet solos, and stump speecoes, and was sustained by Messrs. A. _Ailey, W. H. Kay, El. H. Dukes, J. Clare. W. Seph- ton, J. FT. J Huddleston and S Swann. r. A. Walker officiated at the piano. A. vote of thanks was. accorded to the Chairman on the motion of Councillor Forster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
