        <p>Tde ml lutes of cne 05 lerl Co nmiltoe contain 3.1 re- solutions am3nding th 3 pr)po:ed fees t) M,ssrs. flbon, Birch%ll and AukfanJ, as vac i 'mini officers for twat diitric-A. Tee IrriladmlrrA were Ave tn, rHE OEN MI6 POOR LW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>• Mr. tfocroarog m)ved :—" That pursuant to the re iolu paivid at the annual Board meeting, the Buni be represented at the Central Po3r Liw Conf-rance, Co ton held in the ConnA Chamber of the Guild Ef al, London, on February 1611, and 17G11, and that the Ch 4 irmin and senior Wee-Chair- man te appoitred, with the clerk, to attnl th 3 Confer- ence as such reprPsentatives." It ws explainni that the fol'owing subjec-s w mid bl introduced :—(l) wid powers for guar bans. (2) Toe best means for provid- ing and training nurse; for the indoiir poor. (3.) Old aze pensions and tle Poor L %sr (4) Best meth° 1 of startin in life and carinz far Poor Law boys. Mr. DAvIES sennded,talr ASHCROFT supported,and the reso,wion was noanimmilv carriPd. THE HEA.TING AND LIMITING OF THE NEW INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAII OWSN, arcui:ect,read the m'nutes of the Infirm try C ornmictee an 1 the Sab-commetee. It was stet ed that a depatetiea from the B )10 vieitei tee Whstri and Burnley Worktiouses aril the Mill-road In- firmer;, Liverpool, for the puepts I of inspecting the meth° is in use for he tting and lightinr these institu- Meng. Several meetines of the committee were after- wards held and rit was considered necessary to undertake the heating with hot water of the new infi m try, the workhouse buildings,and the infectious diseases hrspital (propo:e Ito be usel as a mat ernity hospital) and that there should be power suffieuent for heating all such po- tions of the old hospi•al as may be tefc standing and be used for other purposes, and also power for some future , additions to the new Infirmary and the Workeouse. It ' was decided that the buildings named should be heated practically throughout, and on erasing, first ant second floors. Alluding to the visit to the Mill-road Infirmary, Liverpool, it was stated that the relectric light plan; was the chief object of the visit. There were.tWo high pressure !milers, three dynamos, one too ister and 60 accumulators. The total number of. lights which had been wired for was 1340 and the tete CA': of the plant was e‘timated to be Petween £6,500 and £7,000. Ai; £7,000 the cost would be £5 4s. per Toe wive were all laid in steel insulated tubes exposed on tha vralls.lt was asc rtained that the pre- sent cost of lighting the Infirmary lee gas was £920 per annuam, and tha; the decoration cost about £350 per annum It was estimated that the decoration would. with electric light, last four years without renewal and that a saving of 70 per cent in respect of decoration would be made. Oa the 301 e December, 1898, a visit was male to the Burnley Union Infirmary and Work h-aise, and the result was given in the minutes. At a me: ting of the sue_eommitee held at the offices of the architects on the 18th January, consideration was given to the question of heating. After discussing the methods adopted at Whiston and Burnley, and the general inf ormaeion obtainei there, it was decided to renommeni that no hot water heating in the workhouse buildines should for the present be taken into consi iera- Mon. In cousidering the settlement of the system of heating to be adoptel the committee felt that ,it would largely depeni up on the decision as to whether electric lighting should be introduced, as electric lighting would require considerably larger boiler power. After censicler- atioa it was decided that before going further, the archi- tects were to get approximate cssts of tb heating of the new Infirmary by the system of Messrs. Wilson and C onspany, Manchester, and the central system with water heaters as in use at M tnchester. The architects were also to ascertain the cost of gas at t e Warrington Union Workhouse and the number of lights used. At a further meeting held on the 25th January, the architect laid before the committee plans showing how Messrs. Wilson and Company would propose to introduce their system and how they would undertake to heat the three blocks of the new infirmary buildings and supply them with hot water for bates and lavatories for about £2,000 beiuz a self-coatained plant with only one boiler to fire. Tao t ottl cost would be about £3,000. The cost of gas throughout the whole of the Warrington Workhouse baildings whice included the lodge, Board room, and offices, vagrants' cells, house &amp;c., was £144 53. 10d ; for the 12 months ending 31st of October last. Tile number of gas positions was 250.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J H RIDDETTS said he noticed in the minutes thlt the committee had been able to tell them the actual cost of g4B in the Workhouse. He would like to know whether the archit Ts were prepared to give them an estimate of what the actual charge would be for electri- city. _ _</p>
        <p>kr. Ow sti said the elecb,ric light initellation at the Mill-lane lufirmary, was ript c )(notated, so na'; they were unab'e to get an esimate of the cit. With rezard t) the number of lights at the Workhowe they were working out ths actual clet per li ;ht. 3.nd t've number of h)un each light was burning, and from that they would be able to supply an eitimate of what the electric light would clot._ .</p>
        <p>-Tll9 Rly. J. El. RIDDETTE said the cost of ingt AllatiOn was so very con3iderable for electric light nal:, he thought they ought t 3 mak3 basce very slo nly in the matter. I mht bs more advAntagsous to m tits their own gas as a';S.Helens. _ _</p>
        <p>Toe- CHAIRMAN said the committee were considering the matter very carefullv. The sun Committee had to report to the Boildingi Committas, ani that commEtee to tie B )ard, ED thic really, there were two checks on thorn. _</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON then mlved the f Alowinz resolution ; "Th -it the minutes of the Infirmary C)na- mittem, including the report of the sub-3omaritree, be re- ceivel and printed and a copy sent to ealh Guardian ; and that the clerk be instructed to forward a copy to the L3cAlGevernment Bard and to arrange for an interview betwen Mr. Gor ion Smith ani the representatives of the Bo ird at th I CintrAl Conferenc3. and Mr. William Owen. architect upon the snbject of heating and sup- plying with hot water the New Infirmary, and also upon the .Y.xpediency of adopting' tha system of alecteic iight- ing." H said the minutes of th 3 c)mtnittee showed that they were giving the best consideration to every particuTar. Such questions as Mr Riddette had raised would b s better discussed when they ha I been considered by the Guardians. Mr. ASHcRoFT seconded.</p>
        <p>The CLERK Raid he noticed at Wak •field that they hai erected an infirmary something similar to the one being erected by the Board,and they had put in an instillation of electric light at an expense of £2,000, They would prob by be able to obtain an estimate of the cyst per light from tai Wak,field Union. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE expressed the hope that whet' th particulars had been asce taine 1 and they were ready or publicadon, every Guar,lian would be supplied with a carefully prepared eslimate of the rela- tive cesc of the two systems of heating and lighting a week bef 3re they were called to vote up3n it. He ob- jected act a member of the Board to be aske 1 to vote away £6,000 on what might after all b 3 a fad, without prnoer consideration. The Outsmart said Mr.Riddltte wal very severs upon the committ le. He would like to call the attention of the Board to V-te few. that Mr. RiddeAe was a member of the Buildings Committee.. Mr. KNOWLES : Does he attend? Tne CHAIRMAN: No. Mr. KNOWLES : Then we ought to move a vote of cen- sure upon hitn. Toe Rev. J. if. RIDDETTE : I Can give my reasons for not attending. D you with me? The CH.kIRMAN : No.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE : It is fortunate you don't. Continuing he said a vote had been t Oren that morning respecting vacc-nstion, which was referrsd to as a bc. He had not voted beclum he did not thoroughly under- stan I it. Wnen a vote wAs being taken at that Board involving the expinditure of large sums of money they ought not to shut their eves and hold up their hands for it. The business of the Board was managed by a clique of four men and his reason for not attending the com- mittee was loccluse he was expected to be silent. It was a fact.</p>
        <p>Tile CHAIRMAN : If you did your duty by attendiig the re nmlttee meetings you wouli know more abmt the matters. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE : Tiii3 matter has been brmght before tho C mmittee— The CHAIRMAN : Allow me to sp3ak. We are only doing what w, have been comp 41ed to do by the Local Government Bard. If you had been at the lest meet- ing you woull hive known all about it. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE ; IWO here at the last meeting and protested against it. It has been decid yl at some hole ald c-trner -meeting. This matter-of vaccination charges was not before the list Board m2el log. „.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. ASHCROFT : Mr.Riddette should attend the. met- ing s and n4ar everythinz.. _ .</p>
        <p>74r. TnoawroN : Am! to understand that the estl- mated cost of electric lizhting at Liverpool did not include the cost of the toiler.</p>
        <p>Mr OWEN (architect) gaici it was probible that the ees- of the boiler was rot iucluded.</p>
        <p>Mr. TIIORNT3N thlught the Board ought to have all he matters pus, before them. A week's consideration</p>
        <p>wai vita little enlugh to coasider aa expenditure of £5,000.</p>
        <p>The CILIIRMEIN : We are 133uni t 3 give a week's notice.</p>
