        <p>ROIIAN cAraoLics AND THEIR ATTENDANCE AT</p>
        <p>CHURCH. ANOTHER SCENE</p>
        <p>The CLERK read the following lett?r from the Local Governrnenr Boar]:—" Local Government Board, White- hall, S. W., 30;h January, 1899. Sir,—l am directed by the Local Government Board :to transmit to you for the can leration of the Guardians of the Warrington Union, th accompanying copy of a letter which the Board have receive I from Mr. Thomas G. Big, and to request that that the Bard may be furnished with the observatious of the Guardians on the Pubjaet to wnich it relates."— Mr. Sutton also read the letter referred t, as follows : " Catholic Guardians Association, 8, Cavan-street, Inn- don, S E., 17ch Jateuary, 1899. Sir,—l am directed to call your actention to a section in some revised regula- tions drawn up by the Guardians of the Warrington U.:ion, which reads as f :—" No leave of atsence shall un ier any ciraumsta,oces be granted to inmates in Class D." This rule pre gents many Roman Catholics from getting out t 3 Church on Sandays. 'rho Guardians say they cannot alter their rule. although I understaud they fully appreciate the right of individuals to practice their religion. I, therefore, trust your Board will do what is possible to bring about a salsfactory arrange 7 ment.—l am, Sir, your obedient servant. THOMAS G. KING."</p>
        <p>Toe Rev, J. H. RIDDETTE: !I think it right to sty that the authors of the classification tcheme overlooked th elementary right of personal liberty when they mad the rue. We have a classification system in operatione Heaven kaows how it is working. It is a poor thing and if it ueede i any word of condemnation, I think you have it in the letter that has just been read. If you have a c'ass of people, however bad they may be, under your charge, they must be allowed to go to their own place of worship. I am aware you have a Roman Catholic chaplain who visits this institution from time to time, bun you deprive the Catholic inmates of the ministrations of religion of their own faith on the only day on which it woull probably be convenient for them to attend church. At whatever difficulty to our offic..rs we ought to allow even the persons in Class Dtogo to their own place of worship. Ic might involve a little trouble. I speak as a Protestalt.and on behalf of thoae who don't take the same view I take on theological questions. I belong to quite another church,but I have always most strongly dlsapproved of Mr. A. J. Jolley's absurd classification scheme, especially with regard to the particular class whinh has boon named. I think it a great shame. and an intolerab'e wrong,l h)po the Catho- lic; will take further action in the matter if the House Committee slbr over it and mike no ch Inge. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : The Rev. J. H. Riddette is this morning appeariog in the character of a " conscientious objector" to veracity apparently. When members get up to speak at Ineetino of the Boari they should at least take the troub'e to obtain accurate information. Mr. Ridiete is a member of the House CommEtea. He was a member of the special sub-committee that drew up the classification scheme, and now he is endeavouring to get a cheap advertisement.</p>
        <p>Mr. KNOWLES : (Hear, hear.) But we have no gallery to talk te here.</p>
        <p>Mr: A. J. JOLLEY : When Mr Riidette accuses the committee of endr avauring to interfere with the religious opinions of any inmate he is making a statement which is untrue, and if 11,4 had talum a little trJuble he would have known it t) b untrue. The Illy. J. H. RIDDErrk a'aout the letter?</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND : It should not go forth to the public that we are preventing Roman Cath...lies from the enj )yment of their religion. It will be known to all the members of toe House Committse that we have enchavoured to make arrangements whereby Mass shall be conducted in the workhous) for th 9 Roman Catholic inmates. The matter restsi with the Roman Catho'ic Bishop of Liverpool and the Roman Catholic awhoricies to meet us with regard to it. We are anxious that as we have a Church of England service here every Sunddy we should have a Roman Catholic service as well.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. illeKimax : We always endeavour ta do our duty to all the religious bodies. Tho letters were then referred to the House Csmmit- tee.</p>
        <p>THE SILE OF INTOXICANTS TO CHILDREN. A resolution was submitted to the Board by another Union for submission to the Local Gowransent Board asking for action to be taken to stop 'he sale of intoxi- catinz liquors to childrm under 13 years of ago for con- sumption of the premises.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. R. RIDDETTE moved that the Board sup- port the requisiti.m. - _</p>
        <p>Mr. TH3R-NTON seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. A.J.JoLLsv said it was not a matter for the Board to take in hand, and moved as an amendment that it be allowed to lie on the table. Mr DAVIII3 seconded.</p>
        <p>Oa a vlte being taken the resolution was carried by six votei to four, VERSONAL EXPLANATION BY THE REV. J. H.</p>
