        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The watt! fortnightly meetiag of this Council was held at the Bewvy-streer: Cnambers on Wednesday afternoon There were present :—M!sars. Joshua Ball (chtirmin), Brooks, F.)r4ha a?, Houghton, P Longton, Ete.nry Thorn- ton, and THticon, with Mr F Kerfoot (impactor), Dr Sephton (midical ofli3er), Mr J C Sut:on (e.lerk). Mr S Sutton (deputy clerk), and Mr D St.° ae (for M: W Ow, surveyor.)</p>
        <p>BIIRTONWOOD AND THE LIGHTING ACT. Mr. FORSIIAW Stld Ile had been requested by the Parish the Council of Buttonwood to bring up befor s the Council matter of his parish adopting the Lighting At and urban powers. Buttonwood was a very larz.e parish and the northerly D at of it whci is situated in the Colliery Die- tact and Golins Green was dere'oping ; a'so the assess- meat of Buttonwood was increasing. The northerly part was becoming thickly populatal, and the people wanted to adopt the L'gnting Act bu; the people who RYA nearer to Warrington said it was is,t fair they should to called upoo t pay for anythinz purely for the benefit of those people in the north:tly part. It had been decided to devide the parish into two wards for the -pure pose of either ward. adopting the Act. Dr. avhton said in regard to toe Lightin ?. Act : If the pariah decide to adopt it the expanses would have to be borne by the whole parish. The CLERK said.any alteration of Burtonwood slaould spring train the Parish Council or a parish meeting and this Council could then coasider it, but th • Council did not like to enter into any question before the whole parish of Burtonwooi agreed.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL -OFFICER'S REPORT. Dr. SEPSTON reported that there had been 28 births and 14 deaths during the month. tria escass of births ouer deaths was 14. The death rate from all eaneea was equal to 16 per 1,090 a,:ainst 12 the average of the last three months and against 7•5 the corresponding moth of !art year. Th., death rate from zym ltio diseases was equal to *8 per 1,000 against nil the ay.rage rate of the last three ra-mths and agalnst*B the corresponding month of last year.</p>
        <p>Toe r-eport wts adopted. THE NoisANCE AT LONGFORD—MR. DES AND THE PRESS. The INSPECTGR (Mr. Kerfoot) reported that he had written to Mr. Dead as requested by the Council at the last m=,eti,ig ani Mr. D:as had replied that he had placed the letter Mors the Sanitary Works Committee and they were willing to dikcuos the matter if c:rtain matters were not given to the public by the press. The CHAIRMAN S kid it was coutrary to wria was de- cided at ills last meeting. Mr. Matzos: S)me eighteen months ago we met a deputation of the committee on Longfori Depot. We have got no fatisfaction from that day to this. Thsre no harm in bringing these matters before the.public. The CHAIRMAN • I t are it that it means something in the way of some great invention they may have in hand, and which tiiey do not want to become pablic property. The CLunit : Mr. D?as mcans that they are prepared to come b-fors you, but publicity was not to be given t 3 certain things. The CHAIRmAN : Where are you going to draAv the line?</p>
        <p>Mr. FULTON : This matter has been put off so long, it is no f lir it should be further delayod. The CHAIRMAN: L was u•ianimoully agreed at our last meeting that we shout i 3.4 Mr I:1-as to come here, and anything he wished not to bi reportPd we would grant. I think it is some particular scheme he does not want to b, made public.</p>
        <p>Mr. THORNIT.)N IC says in the letter" anythinz." The CIIAIRAUN I sh')Uld not xgr.e to amytaing ac,airpit our interest. I take it vre sh al be the rulers whether a thing shculd be reporte.i or not. The CLERK : Mr. Deas is not speakinz for himself; he says his committee will meet you if the repJrters are net present, _</p>
        <p>Mr. TflostiToN : Neithcr Mr. Deas or the Corpora- tion hive ay ognt to as that.</p>
        <p>The Cmatima;: We have all expris3ed ourselves against tli3 nuiaance. If it was our own private busi- ness we shquld net want it to come before the public. Mr. Havrobr said they could arrange for a matter of that deslription. The CHAIRMAN : We should only agree to ke 3p from the reportf•rg any matter we thougut of no consequence to the public. Mr. THORNTON : As matters stand at present, if they don't abate the nuisance we shall hay:: to take pro. ceedihgs.</p>
        <p>The -CHAIRISIAN al-ked Mr. Halton to move a resolution that the ',porters be present. Mr. HALTON said he was surprised that Mr. Deas was not present under any circumstanu!s. Tne INsPacroft said Mr. Deas told him on Monday he would not come under any circurnstanc:l if the re- porters were present. The CLERK said the discharge from Longford was clearly a nuisance. But before they took any steps they required the opinion of a comprztent enzineer. it was no use going to a superior court for a mandamus requir- ing people to do what they night not be able to do. The Corpor *Gioia would say they were pursuing their best course' and the Council would have to show them a better before they could be forced to do anything. Dr. SEPHTON said if a manufacturer was using the best means in his power they could not do anyt`sing against him. If the Council engaged a competent Ean i- tary engineer would the LongfJrd people allow him to go over their works ? If they were conducting any pri- vate work or experiments they would not. There was a pubic nuisance from the brook, chimney, and the works generally. Sa far as the nuisance from the works was concerned they 'could do nothing but remove them to do away with the nuis- ance. The CLERK said the Council would have to prove the Corporation were not trying to prevent the nuisance by every possible means. It was unanimously agreed that tbe Clerk should write to Mr. Deas asking as he bid nit been to see them to prepare a report before the next meting of the Coun- cil as to what progress had been made in the direction of the matter promised when he last saw them. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>RIDDETrE,</head>
        <p>RIDDETrE,</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. RIDDRTTE said he had been rudely challenged id certain matters as to his attendances on committee;. With regard to the buildings Committee he wished to withdraw from it. He would have attended if his health had permitted, that as they knew be bad suffered through ill-health for some time. The CHAIRMAN: I am very Sorry. Tao Rev. J. EL RIDDETTR (continuing) said with re gard to the House Committee meetings he thought his attendances had been very fair. As be had previously statei the date of meeting had been changed from the special monthly meeting ta the old system of fortnightiy meetings. He had very serious realons for not at-,end- ing recent meetings of that committee. lie did Rol wish to say anything that woald be painful to anyone* But he had received letters which he might divide bate two classes. One set of letters implored hien t 3 attend the Huse Committee meetings because of the valuable service he was able to render ; the 'ltem of the other class did not suggest that he should withdraw from the comm;ttee but contained charges which were absolutely unfounded. One charge was this that he had no other cbject in attending than to obstruct every proposal that came from the chair. He had been a member of that Board for four years, he had not always agreed with the majority ; but he challenged.any member present or atsent to be able to say that hie actions at the Beard meetings had been the action of an obstructionist. Rethought his conduct at the Committee meetings had been in relation to his char- acter on the Board. He had been pained, and extremely pained at the charges which had beenlevelled at hitn,and he had hal under serious consideration the question of Wiring from the House Committee until perhaps differ- ently constituted. Perhaps be would be wise in leaving. Any man with self respect who had had such an absurd charge made against him was justified in absenting him- self from attendance' Mr. ASEICROFT : Are these letters from members of the Board ? ' The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE : Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. AsErcaoFr : Will you tell us whom they are? The Rev. J. H. RIDDHTTII : I am not disposed to divulge their names. Mr. ASHCROFT : I should be very sorry indeed if Mr. Riddette retired from the House Committee. The CHAIRMAN The committees will be .reformed shortly, so that it is not worth while Mr. Riddette re- tiring now.</p>
        <p>The Rev.J.H. RIDDETTE said it was a personal matter, and that waa why he asked leave to 'Mention is. He bad to come a longer distance than mcst of the mjmbera,and being a Nonconformist some of the members did not associate with him ; he did not meet them during the week; he had not the opportunity of walking to board meetings with the members and thf refore did not know what was coming before tbe meeting.and he had not any prior information which some members obtained. He came with an absolutely open mind and with an hoßept dealre to do what be could to administer the Poor law, but because he exercised the right of criticism, and ex- pressed himself—he hope I nor rudely—with plaintress on the subject, be was j limped upon either publicly or by letter. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. ASECROFT did not think it was right for any mom- t4 send ouch let",ers.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. Riddratte then left the room, an the Board rose.</p>
        <p>PERSuNAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Taylor, of the Warrington Angling Associa- tion, was the local representative at the annual dinner of the East End Angling Association, which took place at the Bee Hotel, Liverpool, the other evening.</p>
